window.KOMPOZ_SEARCH=[{"p":"Kompoz documentation","h":"","u":"index.html","x":"Kompoz is a declarative UI library for C++20 in the style of Jetpack Compose, with Material 3 components, running on Linux, Windows, Android and the web from one codebase. If you know Compose, you know Kompoz: the same way of thinking (composable functions, state, recomposition, modifiers), the same names where C++ allows, and Material 3 components that look and behave like Compose's. Version 0.1.0 is the first public release, licensed under the Apache License 2.0. void Counter () { auto count = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); Column ( Modifier (). padding ( 16 ), {.verticalArrangement = Arrangement :: spacedBy ( 8 )}, [&] { Text ( \"Count: \" + std:: to_string (count. get ()), {.font = typography ().titleLarge}); Button ([count] { count. set (count. get () + 1 ); }, [] { Text ( \"Add\" ); }); }); } Application kompoz:: createApplication ( int , char **) { return {.title = \"Demo\" , .content = [] { MaterialTheme ( lightColorScheme (), Counter ); }}; }"},{"p":"Kompoz documentation","h":" Get started","u":"index.html#get-started","x":"Setup : build Kompoz, add it to your CMake project, and run on each platform. Tutorial : build a first app step by step, from a line of text to a Material list. Coming from Compose : how Kotlin's Compose maps to C++, at a glance."},{"p":"Kompoz documentation","h":" Foundation","u":"index.html#foundation","x":"Thinking in Kompoz : declarative UI, composable functions and recomposition. State : observable state, remember , rememberSaveable , hoisting and state holders. Lifecycle and identity : how calls are identified without a compiler plugin, and what makes them skip. Side effects : coroutines, LaunchedEffect , DisposableEffect and the other effect APIs. Phases : composition, layout and drawing, and how to keep each one cheap. CompositionLocal : handing values down the tree, and the built-in locals."},{"p":"Kompoz documentation","h":" Design","u":"index.html#design","x":"Modifiers : decorating composables, why order matters, and writing your own. Layouts : Column , Row , Box , flow layouts, FlexBox , Grid and adaptive layouts. Custom layouts : Layout , constraints, intrinsics and SubcomposeLayout . Lists and grids : lazy lists and grids, pagers, carousels and nested scrolling. Material components : a tour of the Material 3 components. Theming : color schemes, dark theme, dynamic color, typography and design systems. Text : styling text, fonts, selection and text fields. Graphics : Canvas , brushes, paths, shapes, shadows, images and vectors. Animation : animated values, visibility, content, transitions and shared elements. Gestures and input : clicks, drags, pinches, the keyboard, focus and drag and drop."},{"p":"Kompoz documentation","h":" App","u":"index.html#app","x":"Architecture : unidirectional data flow, ViewModel s, flows and saved state. Navigation : screens, the back stack, typed routes and deep links. Resources : assets, the library's texts in the system language, and platform services. Accessibility : semantics, screen readers, touch targets and the keyboard. Testing : headless UI tests with a virtual clock. Tools : previews, rebuilding on save, scripted screenshots and profiling."},{"p":"Kompoz documentation","h":" Reference","u":"index.html#reference","x":"API status : every Compose API and its status in Kompoz, and the build log."},{"p":"Setup","h":"","u":"setup.html","x":"Build Kompoz, add it to your own CMake project, and run your app on Linux, Windows, Android and the web."},{"p":"Setup","h":" Requirements","u":"setup.html#requirements","x":"A C++20 compiler: GCC 13 or newer, or Clang 17 or newer (Kompoz is tested with GCC 16 and Clang 22). CMake 3.24 or newer. SDL3 and FreeType. Optional: HarfBuzz for text shaping (Arabic joining, Indic scripts, ligatures, emoji sequences). Without it, text is drawn character by character. Optional on Linux: libcurl, libwebp and libturbojpeg, which AsyncImage uses to download images and decode WebP and JPEG. libdbus is loaded at run time, if present, to read the desktop's accent color. On Linux, install these from your distribution's packages. For Android, the web and Windows, Kompoz builds SDL3, FreeType and HarfBuzz from source; scripts/fetch-deps.sh downloads them (see below)."},{"p":"Setup","h":" Build the library and the showcase","u":"setup.html#build-the-library-and-the-showcase","x":"cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release cmake --build build -j ./build/showcase This builds the core library ( kompoz_core ), the SDL3 backend ( kompoz_sdl3 ), the showcase app, and the headless tests ( ./build/kompoz_tests ). Useful options: On desktops that have lld, Kompoz links with it unless you chose another linker: about one second instead of GNU ld's twenty-five for the showcase."},{"p":"Setup ›  Build the library and the showcase","h":"KOMPOZ_BUILD_SDL3","u":"setup.html#build-the-library-and-the-showcase","x":"ON Build the SDL3 backend. OFF builds only the core and the tests, with no window system."},{"p":"Setup ›  Build the library and the showcase","h":"KOMPOZ_BUILD_EXAMPLES","u":"setup.html#build-the-library-and-the-showcase","x":"ON Build the showcase."},{"p":"Setup ›  Build the library and the showcase","h":"KOMPOZ_BUILD_TESTS","u":"setup.html#build-the-library-and-the-showcase","x":"ON ( OFF on Android, iOS and the web) Build the headless tests."},{"p":"Setup ›  Build the library and the showcase","h":"KOMPOZ_VENDORED_DEPS","u":"setup.html#build-the-library-and-the-showcase","x":"ON on Android, iOS, the web and Windows Build SDL3, FreeType and HarfBuzz from third_party/ instead of using the system's."},{"p":"Setup","h":" Use Kompoz in your project","u":"setup.html#use-kompoz-in-your-project","x":"Add Kompoz as a subdirectory, a clone or a git submodule, and link the SDL3 backend. The backend brings the program's entry point. cmake_minimum_required ( VERSION 3.24 ) project (myapp LANGUAGES C CXX ) set ( CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20 ) set ( KOMPOZ_BUILD_EXAMPLES OFF ) set ( KOMPOZ_BUILD_TESTS OFF ) add_subdirectory (kompoz) # a clone or a git submodule add_executable (myapp main.cpp) target_link_libraries (myapp PRIVATE kompoz_sdl3) kompoz_copy_assets (myapp) # the fonts and the licenses next to the binary kompoz_copy_assets copies what every app ships, the bundled Noto Sans fonts and the licenses, next to your binary each time it is built. Your main.cpp defines kompoz::createApplication instead of main : void Counter () { auto count = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); Column ( Modifier (). padding ( 16 ), {.verticalArrangement = Arrangement :: spacedBy ( 8 )}, [&] { Text ( \"Count: \" + std:: to_string (count. get ()), {.font = typography ().titleLarge}); Button ([count] { count. set (count. get () + 1 ); }, [] { Text ( \"Add\" ); }); }); } Application kompoz:: createApplication ( int , char **) { return {.title = \"Demo\" , .content = [] { MaterialTheme ( lightColorScheme (), Counter ); }}; } All of Kompoz is in one header, #include <kompoz/kompoz.hpp> , and in the kompoz namespace."},{"p":"Setup","h":" The Application","u":"setup.html#the-application","x":"createApplication gets the command line and returns an Application : On the desktop the command line can carry a deep link that the app opens with, such as ./myapp kompoz://item/42 (see Navigation )."},{"p":"Setup ›  The Application","h":"title","u":"setup.html#the-application","x":"The window's title."},{"p":"Setup ›  The Application","h":"width , height","u":"setup.html#the-application","x":"The window's first size in dp, on the desktop."},{"p":"Setup ›  The Application","h":"content","u":"setup.html#the-application","x":"The composable that fills the window."},{"p":"Setup ›  The Application","h":"fontPath , boldFontPath , mediumFontPath","u":"setup.html#the-application","x":"Fonts to use instead of the bundled ones."},{"p":"Setup ›  The Application","h":"imageDiskCacheBytes","u":"setup.html#the-application","x":"How much of the images AsyncImage downloads is kept on disk (64 MB by default; 0 keeps none)."},{"p":"Setup","h":" Platforms","u":"setup.html#platforms","x":""},{"p":"Setup","h":" Linux","u":"setup.html#linux","x":"The build above is all it takes. Dark mode and the desktop's accent color come from the XDG desktop portal and follow it as it changes: isSystemInDarkTheme() and dynamicLightColorScheme() see them."},{"p":"Setup","h":" Windows","u":"setup.html#windows","x":"Windows builds are cross-compiled from Linux with MinGW-w64 (on Arch: pacman -S mingw-w64-gcc ). The dependencies are built from source and linked statically, so the program needs only Windows' own DLLs and runs on Windows 10 and later. scripts/fetch-deps.sh cmake -S . -B build-windows -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=cmake/mingw-w64-x86_64.cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release cmake --build build-windows -j Ship the executable with the fonts/ and licenses/ folders next to it. The tests also run under Wine: WINEPATH=/usr/x86_64-w64-mingw32/bin wine build-windows/kompoz_tests.exe ."},{"p":"Setup","h":" Android","u":"setup.html#android","x":"You need the Android SDK, NDK 29.0.14206865, the SDK's CMake 4.1.2 (from Android Studio's SDK Manager, or sdkmanager \"ndk;29.0.14206865\" \"cmake;4.1.2\" ) and JDK 17 or newer (Android Studio's bundled JBR will do). scripts/fetch-deps.sh cd examples/showcase/android && ./gradlew installDebug fetch-deps.sh downloads SDL3, FreeType and HarfBuzz into third_party/ at pinned versions, checks their SHA-256 sums and applies the patches in scripts/patches/ . Gradle builds Kompoz's CMake project with the NDK into libSDL3.so and your app's libmain.so . To make your own app, start from the showcase's android/ folder: it has only the manifest, a theme and a short MainActivity built on SDL's SDLActivity . The fonts go into the APK from Kompoz's assets/ folder. On Android, Kompoz supports the soft keyboard with suggestions, edge-to-edge insets, the back gesture and predictive back, the system's dark mode and font size, dynamic color on Android 12+ and TalkBack."},{"p":"Setup","h":" Web","u":"setup.html#web","x":"Kompoz builds for the browser with Emscripten (on Arch: pacman -S emscripten ). SDL3, FreeType and HarfBuzz are built from source; the fonts and images are packed into a .data file next to the page. scripts/fetch-deps.sh emcmake cmake -S . -B build-web -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DKOMPOZ_BUILD_TESTS=OFF cmake --build build-web -j python3 -m http.server 8765 --directory build-web Then open http://localhost:8765/showcase.html . There are no threads on the web, so work you would put on a thread runs in a coroutine instead. Characters the bundled font lacks (emoji, CJK, Arabic) are drawn with the browser's own fonts. For the web build of your own app, copy the Emscripten options from examples/showcase/CMakeLists.txt ."},{"p":"Setup","h":" macOS and iOS","u":"setup.html#macos-and-ios","x":"The code is portable, but neither has been built yet: both need a Mac."},{"p":"Setup","h":" Packaging","u":"setup.html#packaging","x":"scripts/package.sh package.sh packages the showcase for every platform whose toolchain it finds, into dist/ : a Linux .tar.gz , a Windows .zip , an Android .apk signed with the debug key, and a web .zip . Name the platforms to build only some: scripts/package.sh linux web . The packages carry the version from CMakeLists.txt , plus the commit when they are not built from the release's tag. Use the script as a model for packaging your own app."},{"p":"Setup","h":" See also","u":"setup.html#see-also","x":"Tutorial : your first app, step by step. Tools : rebuilding on save, previews, screenshots and profiling."},{"p":"Tutorial","h":"","u":"tutorial.html","x":"Build a small app step by step: text, layout, state, a scrolling list and Material 3 styling. Each step adds a little to the one before. This tutorial assumes you have built Kompoz once (see Setup ) and can run your own app with it. Every snippet is complete C++: copy it into your main.cpp ."},{"p":"Tutorial","h":" Step 1: a composable function","u":"tutorial.html#step-1-a-composable-function","x":"A Kompoz app is made of composable functions : plain C++ functions that describe part of the UI by calling other composables. Text is the simplest one. void Greeting ( const std::string& name) { Text ( \"Hello, \" + name + \"!\" ); } Application kompoz:: createApplication ( int , char **) { return {.title = \"Tutorial\" , .content = [] { Greeting ( \"Kompoz\" ); }}; } createApplication is the app's entry point: the platform backend owns main and calls it once. The Application it returns has the window's title, its size on the desktop, and content , the composable that fills the window. There is no XML, no view objects and no setText : to change what is on the screen, you call composables with different data, and Kompoz updates the screen to match."},{"p":"Tutorial","h":" Step 2: layout","u":"tutorial.html#step-2-layout","x":"Composables placed one after another are drawn on top of each other unless you say how to arrange them. Column puts its children in a column, Row in a row, Box on top of each other. struct Message { std::string author, body; }; void MessageCard ( const Message & msg) { Row ( Modifier (). padding ( 8 ), [&] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: AccountCircle , \"Profile picture\" , Modifier (). size ( 40 )); Spacer ( Modifier (). width ( 8 )); Column ([&] { Text (msg.author, {.font = typography ().titleSmall}); Spacer ( Modifier (). height ( 4 )); Text (msg.body, {.font = typography ().bodyMedium}); }); }); } The children go in a lambda, the last argument, like Compose's trailing lambda. Content lambdas run right away, while the parent is being composed, so capturing with [&] is safe there. Modifier decorates a composable: size, padding, background, clicks and much more. Modifiers chain, and their order matters ( padding then background is not the same as background then padding ). See Modifiers ."},{"p":"Tutorial","h":" Step 3: state","u":"tutorial.html#step-3-state","x":"A UI that never changes is not much of an app. Kompoz redraws a composable when a state it read changes. mutableStateOf makes one; remember keeps it across recompositions, so it isn't created anew each time the function runs. void Counter () { auto count = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); Column ( Modifier (). padding ( 16 ), {.verticalArrangement = Arrangement :: spacedBy ( 8 )}, [&] { Text ( \"You clicked \" + std:: to_string (count. get ()) + \" times\" ); Button ([count] { count. set (count. get () + 1 ); }, [] { Text ( \"Click me\" ); }); }); } count.get() reads the state. Because Counter read it, Counter runs again when it changes. count.set(...) writes it, here from the button's onClick . MutableState<T> is a small handle you can copy. The click callback runs later, after composition, so it captures count by value ( [count] ), never by reference. The second argument of Column is a style struct with designated initializers: C++'s answer to Compose's named parameters. See State for more."},{"p":"Tutorial","h":" Step 4: a scrolling list","u":"tutorial.html#step-4-a-scrolling-list","x":"A Column composes and lays out every child, even those out of view. For long lists use LazyColumn : it composes only the items on the screen, plus a small margin, as you scroll. void Conversation ( const std::vector< Message >& messages) { LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( LazyListScope & list) { list. items (messages, []( const Message & msg) { MessageCard (msg); }); }); } The lambda gets a LazyListScope that describes the items: item adds one, items a whole list. Item content is composed later, as each item comes into view, so the item lambda captures nothing by reference that might be gone by then. Here it only uses its argument. When items can be added, removed or moved, give each a stable key so its state follows it: LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( LazyListScope & list) { list. items (messages, []( const Message & msg) { return msg.id; }, []( const Message & msg) { MessageCard (msg); }); }); See Lists and grids for grids, pagers, sticky headers and scrolling from code."},{"p":"Tutorial","h":" Step 5: Material 3","u":"tutorial.html#step-5-material-3","x":"Kompoz implements Material 3, as Compose Material 3 does. MaterialTheme gives everything inside it a color scheme, a typography and shapes; Scaffold lays out a screen with its app bar, floating action button and snackbars. void ConversationScreen ( const std::vector< Message >& messages) { Scaffold ( Modifier (), {.topBar = [] { TopAppBar ({.title = [] { Text ( \"Messages\" ); }}); }}, [&]( PaddingValues padding) { Box ( Modifier (). padding (padding), [&] { Conversation (messages); }); }); } Application kompoz:: createApplication ( int , char **) { return {.title = \"Tutorial\" , .content = [] { static const std::vector< Message > messages = {{ \"Ada\" , \"Hello!\" }, { \"Linus\" , \"Hi Ada.\" }}; bool dark = isSystemInDarkTheme (); MaterialTheme (dark ? darkColorScheme () : lightColorScheme (), [] { ConversationScreen (messages); }); }}; } Scaffold passes its content the padding that keeps it clear of the bars and the system's insets (the status bar, a display cutout). isSystemInDarkTheme() follows the system's dark mode as it changes; on Android 12+ and on Linux desktops you can use the system's colors as well, with dynamicLightColorScheme() . See Theming ."},{"p":"Tutorial","h":" Step 6: a card that expands","u":"tutorial.html#step-6-a-card-that-expands","x":"Let's make each message a card that shows its whole text when clicked. The expanded flag is state that belongs to one card, so each card remembers its own. void MessageCard ( const Message & msg) { auto expanded = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); Card ([expanded] { expanded. set (!expanded. get ()); }, Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (). padding ( 8 , 4 ), [&] { Column ( Modifier (). padding ( 16 ). animateContentSize (), [&] { Text (msg.author, {.font = typography ().titleSmall, .color = colorScheme ().primary}); Spacer ( Modifier (). height ( 4 )); Text (msg.body, {.font = typography ().bodyMedium, .maxLines = expanded. get () ? 0 : 1 , .overflow = TextOverflow :: Ellipsis }); }); }); } Card(onClick, modifier, content) is a clickable card with Material's ripple. colorScheme() and typography() read the current theme's colors and type styles. maxLines = 0 means no limit; with one line the text ends in an ellipsis. animateContentSize() animates the column's size when the text grows or shrinks. Inside a lazy list, remember is per item: each card keeps its own expanded . With keys, it stays with the message when the list changes."},{"p":"Tutorial","h":" Where to go next","u":"tutorial.html#where-to-go-next","x":"Thinking in Kompoz : the ideas behind declarative UI and recomposition. Coming from Compose : how Kotlin's Compose maps to C++. Layouts , Material components and Animation for building real screens. Tools : previews and rebuilding on save while you work."},{"p":"Coming from Compose","h":"","u":"coming-from-compose.html","x":"If you know Jetpack Compose, you already know Kompoz: the same model, mostly the same names. This page shows how Kotlin's constructs map to C++. Kompoz follows Compose's behavior closely, down to Material 3's measurements and Android's fling curve. What changes is the language: C++ has no compiler plugin, no named arguments and no trailing lambdas outside the parentheses, so each of those has a C++ form. Once you know the handful of patterns below, Compose's documentation applies almost word for word."},{"p":"Coming from Compose","h":" Composable functions","u":"coming-from-compose.html#composable-functions","x":"In Kotlin, @Composable and the Compose compiler plugin give every call its identity. In C++ a composable is a plain function. Calling it from composition is enough: @ Composable fun Greeting (name: String ) { Text ( \"Hello, $name!\" ) } void Greeting ( const std::string& name) { Text ( \"Hello, \" + name + \"!\" ); } A plain function like this has no scope of its own: the state it reads belongs to whichever composable called it, and that composable runs again when the state changes. That is enough for most small helpers. For a component that should recompose on its own and be skipped when its arguments haven't changed, as a Kotlin composable is, write its body with composable and take a CallSite : void Greeting ( const std::string& name, CallSite site = CallSite :: current ()) { composable (site, name, [=] { Text ( \"Hello, \" + name + \"!\" ); }); } CallSite is std::source_location . Its default argument records where Greeting was called from; together with the call's position inside its parent, that is the call's identity, which is what the compiler plugin works out in Kotlin. The arguments before the lambda decide skipping: when they compare equal ( == ) to last time and nothing the body read has changed, the body doesn't run. The body captures by value ( [=] ), because it can run again later on its own, without its caller. Composables that take a content lambda, like Column and Card , are Compose's inline composables: they use ComposableScope scope(site); for an identity without a scope, and the state read in their content belongs to the caller. See Lifecycle and identity ."},{"p":"Coming from Compose","h":" Parameters","u":"coming-from-compose.html#parameters","x":"Kotlin's named and default parameters become C++ options structs with designated initializers: Text ( \"Title\" , fontSize = 24. sp, maxLines = 1 ) OutlinedTextField (value, onValueChange, label = { Text ( \"Email\" ) }, singleLine = true ) Text ( \"Title\" , Modifier (), {.fontSize = 24 , .maxLines = 1 }); OutlinedTextField (value, onValueChange, Modifier (), {.singleLine = true , .label = \"Email\" }); Designated initializers must follow the order the fields are declared in the struct. The headers list them in a sensible order, and your compiler tells you when you mix them up. Most components take, in this order: the required arguments, the Modifier , the options struct, then the content lambda. Overloads leave out what you don't need: Button(onClick, content) and Button(onClick, modifier, style, content) both exist."},{"p":"Coming from Compose","h":" Trailing lambdas","u":"coming-from-compose.html#trailing-lambdas","x":"Kotlin's trailing lambda goes inside the parentheses as the last argument: Column ( Modifier . padding ( 16. dp)) { Text ( \"One\" ) Text ( \"Two\" ) } Column ( Modifier (). padding ( 16 ), [&] { Text ( \"One\" ); Text ( \"Two\" ); }); Content lambdas run right away, during the call, so [&] is safe for them. Callbacks such as onClick run later, after composition, when the function that created them has returned: capture what they use by value . void LikeButton () { auto likes = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); Button ([likes] { likes. set (likes. get () + 1 ); }, [&] { // the callback: by value Text (std:: to_string (likes. get ()) + \" likes\" ); // the content: [&] is fine }); } Lambdas that receive a scope, such as LazyColumn 's, take it as a parameter instead of as a receiver: LazyColumn(modifier, [&](LazyListScope& list) { list.items(...); }) ."},{"p":"Coming from Compose","h":" State","u":"coming-from-compose.html#state","x":"var count by remember { mutableStateOf ( 0 ) } count++ auto count = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); count. set (count. get () + 1 ); C++ has no property delegates, so a state is read with get() and written with set() . MutableState<T> is a handle: copies share the same value, which is why callbacks can capture it by value. update changes the value in place: auto todos = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf (std::vector<std::string>{}); }); todos. update ([](std::vector<std::string>& list) { list. push_back ( \"Buy milk\" ); }); remember(key, init) , rememberSaveable , derivedStateOf , mutableStateListOf , rememberUpdatedState and produceState have their Compose names. See State ."},{"p":"Coming from Compose","h":" Modifiers","u":"coming-from-compose.html#modifiers","x":"Modifier . fillMaxWidth (). padding ( 16. dp). background ( Color . Red , RoundedCornerShape ( 8. dp)) Box ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (). padding ( 16 ). background ( Color :: hex (0xE53935), RoundedCornerShape ( 8 ))); A chain starts from Modifier() . Order matters as in Compose. See Modifiers ."},{"p":"Coming from Compose","h":" Units","u":"coming-from-compose.html#units","x":"Kotlin's 16.dp is just 16 : every length is a float in dp. Font sizes are TextUnit , which converts from a number of sp ( .fontSize = 24 ) or is written as TextUnit::sp(24) and TextUnit::em(1.5f) . Durations use std::chrono : delay(300ms) , debounce(300ms) . A Color holds floats from 0 to 1 ( Color{1, 0, 0, 1} ) or comes from a hex value ( Color::hex(0xE53935) , Color::hex(0x000000, 0.5f) ); colors::Black , White and Transparent are named, and color.copy(0.38f) changes the alpha like Compose's copy(alpha = …) ."},{"p":"Coming from Compose","h":" Coroutines","u":"coming-from-compose.html#coroutines","x":"Kotlin's coroutines become C++20 coroutines that return Task . They run on the UI thread, on the frame clock, as Compose's do. LaunchedEffect (userId) { delay ( 1000 ) user = repository. load (userId) } val scope = rememberCoroutineScope () Button (onClick = { scope.launch { listState. animateScrollToItem ( 0 ) } }) { Text ( \"Top\" ) } using namespace std::chrono_literals; auto user = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf (std:: string ()); }); LaunchedEffect (userId, [user, userId]() -> Task { co_await delay (1000ms); user. set ( \"user \" + std:: to_string (userId)); }); auto list = rememberLazyListState (); auto scope = rememberCoroutineScope (); Button ([scope, list] { scope. launch ([list]() -> Task { co_await list. animateScrollToItem ( 0 ); }); }, [] { Text ( \"Top\" ); }); suspend functions are coroutines you co_await . Flow , StateFlow and SharedFlow exist with their operators ( map , filter , debounce , flatMapLatest , stateIn …), and collectAsState() reads one in composition. See Side effects and Architecture ."},{"p":"Coming from Compose","h":" Composition locals","u":"coming-from-compose.html#composition-locals","x":"val color = LocalContentColor .current CompositionLocalProvider ( LocalContentColor provides Color . Red ) { … } Color color = LocalContentColor . current (); CompositionLocalProvider ({ LocalContentColor . provides ( Color :: hex (0xE53935))}, [&] { Text ( \"Red\" ); }); MaterialTheme.colorScheme , MaterialTheme.typography and MaterialTheme.motionScheme are colorScheme() , typography() and motionScheme() . There is no theme-wide Shapes : components have Material's shapes built in, and you pass your own where a component takes one ( RoundedCornerShape(12) , CircleShape ). See CompositionLocal ."},{"p":"Coming from Compose","h":" A quick reference","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"The README's longer table covers navigation, animation, gestures, text fields and more, and the API page lists every Compose API with its status in Kompoz."},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"@Composable fun F()","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"void F() , or void F(CallSite site = CallSite::current()) with composable(site, args…, [=] { … })"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"remember { x } , remember(key) { x }","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"remember([] { return x; }) , remember(key, [] { return x; })"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"var v by mutableStateOf(0)","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"auto v = mutableStateOf(0); v.get() , v.set(1)"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"key(id) { … }","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"key(id, [&] { … })"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"Modifier.padding(8.dp)","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"Modifier().padding(8)"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"Named arguments","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"Designated initializers: {.maxLines = 1}"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"LaunchedEffect(k) { … }","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"LaunchedEffect(k, [=]() -> Task { … })"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"scope.launch { … }","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"scope.launch([=]() -> Task { … })"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"DisposableEffect(k) { onDispose { … } }","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"DisposableEffect(k, [=] { return [=] { … }; })"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"LocalX.current","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"LocalX.current()"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"MaterialTheme.colorScheme.primary","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"colorScheme().primary"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"LazyColumn { items(list) { … } }","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"LazyColumn(Modifier(), [&](LazyListScope& l) { l.items(list, [](const T& x) { … }); })"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"val vm: MyViewModel = viewModel()","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"auto vm = viewModel<MyViewModel>();"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"@Preview","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"KOMPOZ_PREVIEW(Name) { … }"},{"p":"Coming from Compose ›  A quick reference","h":"createComposeRule()","u":"coming-from-compose.html#a-quick-reference","x":"kompoz::testing::ComposeTestRule"},{"p":"Coming from Compose","h":" What is different","u":"coming-from-compose.html#what-is-different","x":"No compiler plugin. Identity comes from call sites, so two calls on the same line are told apart by their order. In loops whose items move, use key (see Lifecycle ). No by delegation. States are read with get() and written with set() . Skipping needs == . A composable is skipped only if its arguments compare equal. std::function can't be compared, so a composable taking one is restartable but never skipped. Platforms. Kompoz draws through SDL3 on Linux, Windows, Android and the web; there are no Android Views to interoperate with."},{"p":"Thinking in Kompoz","h":"","u":"thinking.html","x":"Kompoz is declarative: you describe what the UI should look like for the current data, and Kompoz updates the screen when the data changes. This page explains that model and the rules that come with it."},{"p":"Thinking in Kompoz","h":" The declarative idea","u":"thinking.html#the-declarative-idea","x":"In an imperative toolkit you build a tree of widgets once and then change it as things happen: find the label, call setText , hide the button, add a row. Every change is code you write, and the more states a screen has, the easier it is to forget one transition and show something inconsistent. In Kompoz you write functions that turn data into UI. When the data changes, the functions run again with the new data, and Kompoz works out what changed on the screen: void Inbox ( int unread) { if (unread == 0 ) { Text ( \"No new messages\" ); } else { Text (std:: to_string (unread) + \" new messages\" , {.fontWeight = FontWeight :: Bold }); } } There is no step that \"updates\" the label: the function always describes the whole result, and the framework keeps the screen in step with it. This is the same model as Jetpack Compose, React or SwiftUI."},{"p":"Thinking in Kompoz","h":" Composable functions","u":"thinking.html#composable-functions","x":"A composable function is a C++ function that emits UI by calling other composables: It takes the data it shows as parameters. It returns nothing: it emits UI by calling Text , Row , Button and so on. It can be called from other composables, in loops and conditions, like any function. struct Contact { std::string name, phone; }; void ContactList ( const std::vector< Contact >& contacts) { Column ([&] { for ( const Contact & c : contacts) { Text (c.name, {.font = typography ().bodyLarge}); Text (c.phone, {.color = colorScheme ().onSurfaceVariant}); } }); } Composables run while Kompoz composes : when the app starts, and again when something they read changes. Calling one outside composition (from a click handler, say) does nothing useful; change state instead and let composition call it."},{"p":"Thinking in Kompoz","h":" Recomposition","u":"thinking.html#recomposition","x":"When data changes, Kompoz runs the composables that depend on it again: this is recomposition . It doesn't rebuild everything: A state remembers who read it. When it changes, only the composables that read it run again, not their parents. When a parent does run, a child whose arguments are equal to last time, and that read nothing that changed, is skipped : it doesn't run, and its part of the screen stays as it is. void NameTag ( const std::string& name, CallSite site = CallSite :: current ()) { composable (site, name, [=] { // skipped while `name` stays the same Text ( \"Hello, \" + name); }); } void Screen () { auto clicks = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); Column ([&] { NameTag ( \"Ada\" ); // not run again when clicks change Text ( \"Clicks: \" + std:: to_string (clicks. get ())); // Screen read clicks Button ([clicks] { clicks. set (clicks. get () + 1 ); }, [] { Text ( \"Click\" ); }); }); } Screen read clicks , so it runs again on every click. NameTag is a scope of its own whose argument didn't change, so it is skipped. See Lifecycle and identity for how scopes and skipping work in C++."},{"p":"Thinking in Kompoz","h":" Rules that follow","u":"thinking.html#rules-that-follow","x":"Because Kompoz decides when and how often your composables run, a few rules keep the results right."},{"p":"Thinking in Kompoz","h":" Composables can run often","u":"thinking.html#composables-can-run-often","x":"A composable may run on every frame of an animation. Keep its body fast: no file reading, no network calls, no heavy computation. Do expensive work once with remember , or outside composition in a coroutine (see Side effects ), and keep the result in state."},{"p":"Thinking in Kompoz","h":" Composables should be free of side effects","u":"thinking.html#composables-should-be-free-of-side-effects","x":"Running a composable should only describe UI. Writing a file, sending a request or changing a variable from inside its body happens again every time it recomposes, and in an order you don't control. void Bad ( int & counter) { counter++; // runs on every recomposition: don't Text (std:: to_string (counter)); } Effects belong in event handlers ( onClick runs once per click) or in effect APIs such as LaunchedEffect and DisposableEffect , which run at defined times."},{"p":"Thinking in Kompoz","h":" Composables can be skipped","u":"thinking.html#composables-can-be-skipped","x":"Don't rely on a composable running because its parent did. If it read no state that changed and its arguments are equal, it won't. Anything that must happen belongs in state, events or effects."},{"p":"Thinking in Kompoz","h":" Composables don't run in a fixed order","u":"thinking.html#composables-dont-run-in-a-fixed-order","x":"Write each composable so that it doesn't depend on another having run first. Sharing data through global variables written during composition breaks as soon as one of them is skipped."},{"p":"Thinking in Kompoz","h":" State drives the UI","u":"thinking.html#state-drives-the-ui","x":"Since the UI is a function of data, changing the UI means changing data. Kompoz watches state , values made with mutableStateOf and the other state holders, and recomposes what read it: void Toggle () { auto on = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); Row ( Modifier (), {.verticalAlignment = Alignment :: CenterVertically }, [&] { Switch (on. get (), [on]( bool v) { on. set (v); }); Text (on. get () ? \"On\" : \"Off\" ); }); } The data flows down as arguments, and events flow up as callbacks: Switch doesn't change anything itself, it tells its caller what the user did, and the caller decides what the state becomes. This unidirectional data flow is the basis of how Kompoz apps are structured. See State and Architecture ."},{"p":"Thinking in Kompoz","h":" See also","u":"thinking.html#see-also","x":"State : holding, remembering and hoisting state. Lifecycle and identity : how composables enter and leave the composition, and what makes them skip. Phases : composition, layout and drawing."},{"p":"State","h":"","u":"state.html","x":"State is any value that can change over time and that the UI shows. This page covers how Kompoz observes state, how to keep it across recompositions and restarts, and where it should live."},{"p":"State","h":" Observable state","u":"state.html#observable-state","x":"Kompoz recomposes when observable state changes. mutableStateOf makes the basic kind, a MutableState<T> : auto name = mutableStateOf (std:: string ( \"Ada\" )); Text (name. get ()); // reading it subscribes the running composable name. set ( \"Grace\" ); // writing it recomposes the readers get() reads the value and records that the running composable depends on it. set(v) stores a new value. If T can be compared with == and the value is the same, nothing happens; otherwise every composable that read it recomposes. update(f) changes the value in place, for containers: todos.update([](auto& v) { v.push_back(item); }) . peek() reads without subscribing, for code that must not recompose when the value changes. A plain variable is not observable: changing it doesn't recompose anything, and a local variable is gone the moment the composable returns. MutableState<T> is a handle to a shared value. Copies are cheap and all see the same value, so you pass states around and capture them by value in callbacks: void NameField () { auto name = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf (std:: string ()); }); OutlinedTextField (name. get (), [name](std::string v) { name. set (std:: move (v)); }); }"},{"p":"State","h":" remember","u":"state.html#remember","x":"A composable's body runs on every recomposition, so a state created in it would start over each time. remember keeps a value across recompositions: void Counter () { auto count = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); // made once Button ([count] { count. set (count. get () + 1 ); }, [&] { Text (std:: to_string (count. get ())); }); } The lambda runs the first time the composable is composed; after that, remember returns the stored value. The value is forgotten when the composable leaves the composition (an if turns false, a list item is removed), and made again if it comes back. remember works for any value, not only states. Use it to keep an expensive object, or one whose identity matters, such as a MutableInteractionSource or a FocusRequester ."},{"p":"State","h":" Remembering with a key","u":"state.html#remembering-with-a-key","x":"remember(key, init) computes the value again when key changes: void Greeting ( const User & user) { auto fullName = remember (user.first + \" \" + user.last, [&] { return \"Dear \" + user.first + \" \" + user.last; }); Text (fullName); } The key can be any value that compares with == ; for several, use a std::tuple or a struct."},{"p":"State","h":" rememberSaveable","u":"state.html#remembersaveable","x":"remember keeps a value while the composable stays in the composition. rememberSaveable also keeps it: when the composable leaves and comes back inside a SaveableStateHolder , as the screens of a NavHost do when you go back to them; when Android ends the app's process in the background and brings it back, and on the desktop across runs with KOMPOZ_STATE_FILE . auto query = rememberSaveable ([] { return mutableStateOf (std:: string ()); }); To survive the process ending, the value must be saveable . Numbers, bool , enums, strings, and std::vector and std::optional of saveable types are; so are MutableState of a saveable value and the library's own state objects (scroll and lazy list states, TextFieldState , NavController ). For your own type, specialize Saver : struct Point { float x = 0 , y = 0 ; }; template <> struct kompoz:: Saver < Point > { static SavedValue save ( const Point & p) { return SavedValue :: List {p.x, p.y}; } static std::optional< Point > restore ( const SavedValue & v) { auto * list = v. list (); if (!list || list-> size () != 2 ) return std::nullopt; auto x = (*list)[ 0 ]. number (), y = (*list)[ 1 ]. number (); if (!x || !y) return std::nullopt; return Point { float (*x), float (*y)}; } }; void Pin () { auto where = rememberSaveable ([] { return mutableStateOf ( Point {}); }); Text (std:: to_string (where. get ().x)); } Or pass a saver made with listSaver or mapSaver , as Compose's rememberSaveable(saver = …) : struct City { std::string name, country; }; const auto citySaver = mapSaver< City >( []( const City & c) { return SavedValue :: Map {{ \"name\" , c.name}, { \"country\" , c.country}}; }, []( const SavedValue :: Map & m) -> std::optional< City > { auto name = m. at ( \"name\" ). string (), country = m. at ( \"country\" ). string (); return name && country ? std:: optional ( City {*name, *country}) : std::nullopt; }); void CityScreen () { auto city = rememberSaveable (citySaver, [] { return mutableStateOf ( City { \"Madrid\" , \"Spain\" }); }); Text (ci"},{"p":"State","h":" Other state holders","u":"state.html#other-state-holders","x":""},{"p":"State","h":" derivedStateOf","u":"state.html#derivedstateof","x":"When a state is computed from others and changes less often than they do, wrap the computation in derivedStateOf . Its readers recompose only when the result changes, not on every change of its inputs: void ScrollToTop () { auto list = rememberLazyListState (); auto showButton = remember ([list] { return derivedStateOf ([list] { return list. firstVisibleItemIndex () > 0 ; }); }); Box ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&] { LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), {.state = list}, []( LazyListScope & l) { l. items ( 100 , []( int i) { Text ( \"Row \" + std:: to_string (i)); }); }); if (showButton. get ()) Text ( \"Back to top\" ); }); } Without derivedStateOf , reading firstVisibleItemIndex() directly would recompose on every item scrolled past. Create a derived state inside remember , so it is made once and can observe its inputs."},{"p":"State","h":" State lists and maps","u":"state.html#state-lists-and-maps","x":"mutableStateListOf and mutableStateMapOf are observable containers, like Compose's SnapshotStateList and SnapshotStateMap . Reading one subscribes; adding, removing or changing items recomposes the readers: void Tags () { auto tags = remember ([] { return mutableStateListOf<std::string>({ \"red\" , \"green\" }); }); Column ([&] { for ( const std::string& tag : tags) Text (tag); Button ([tags] { tags. add ( \"blue\" ); }, [] { Text ( \"Add\" ); }); }); } A list has add , set , removeAt , removeIf , clear and update ; a map has put , remove , getOrDefault and the like. A MutableState<std::vector<T>> changed with update works just as well."},{"p":"State","h":" rememberUpdatedState","u":"state.html#rememberupdatedstate","x":"A long-running effect that must not restart, but should use the latest value of a parameter, reads it through rememberUpdatedState . See Side effects ."},{"p":"State","h":" Flows","u":"state.html#flows","x":"StateFlow and other flows are observable too: collectAsState() turns one into a value that recomposes when it changes. See Architecture ."},{"p":"State","h":" State hoisting","u":"state.html#state-hoisting","x":"A composable that holds its own state is easy to use but hard to control from outside: its caller can't read the state, set it, or share it with others. Hoisting moves the state up to the caller: the composable takes the value and a callback instead. // Stateless: shows `name`, reports edits. Easy to reuse, preview and test. void NameInput ( const std::string& name, std::function< void (std::string)> onNameChange) { OutlinedTextField (name, std:: move (onNameChange), Modifier (), {.label = \"Name\" }); } // Stateful: owns the state and hands it down. void Profile () { auto name = rememberSaveable ([] { return mutableStateOf (std:: string ()); }); Column ([&] { NameInput (name. get (), [name](std::string v) { name. set (std:: move (v)); }); Text ( \"Hello, \" + name. get ()); }); } The rule of thumb, as in Compose: Hoist state to the lowest common ancestor of everything that reads or writes it. Pass state down as values, and events up as callbacks ( unidirectional data flow ). Offer a stateless version of reusable components; add a stateful wrapper when it is convenient."},{"p":"State","h":" State holders","u":"state.html#state-holders","x":"When a screen's state and logic grow, gather them in a class: a state holder . A plain class kept with remember suits UI logic (which panel is open, a form's fields); a ViewModel suits state that outlives the screen and talks to the rest of the app. Kompoz's own components do this: LazyListState , DrawerState and SnackbarHostState are state holders made by rememberLazyListState() and friends. class SearchState { public : MutableState <std::string> query = mutableStateOf (std:: string ()); MutableState < bool > expanded = mutableStateOf ( false ); void clear () const { query. set ( \"\" ); expanded. set ( false ); } }; void SearchScreen () { auto & search = remember ([] { return SearchState (); }); Text (search.query. get ()); } See Architecture for ViewModel s."},{"p":"State","h":" See also","u":"state.html#see-also","x":"Side effects : work that runs in response to state, and coroutines. Lifecycle and identity : when remembered values are kept or forgotten. Architecture : ViewModels and flows."},{"p":"Lifecycle and identity","h":"","u":"lifecycle.html","x":"A composable enters the composition, recomposes zero or more times, and leaves. This page explains that lifecycle, how Kompoz tells one call from another without a compiler plugin, and what makes a composable skip."},{"p":"Lifecycle and identity","h":" The lifecycle of a composable","u":"lifecycle.html#the-lifecycle-of-a-composable","x":"When Kompoz first runs your UI, it records every composable that was called in a tree: the composition . From then on, each call in that tree has a lifecycle: It enters the composition the first time it is called. Its remember blocks compute their values, its effects are scheduled. It recomposes whenever a state it read changes, or when its parent runs and its arguments have changed. Remembered values are kept. It leaves the composition when it is no longer called: an if became false, a list item went away, the screen was navigated away from. Its remembered values are forgotten, its DisposableEffect clean-ups run, its coroutines are cancelled. void Details ( bool show) { if (show) { // Enters when show becomes true, leaves when it becomes false. auto opened = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); DisposableEffect ( 0 , [] { // here: it entered return [] { /* here: it left */ }; }); Text ( \"Details\" ); } } A composable that leaves and comes back starts fresh: remember runs its lambda again. To keep a value across that, use rememberSaveable inside a SaveableStateHolder (navigation does this for its screens) or hoist the state above the if ."},{"p":"Lifecycle and identity","h":" Identity: where a call comes from","u":"lifecycle.html#identity-where-a-call-comes-from","x":"To know which remembered values belong to which call, Kompoz needs to identify each call across recompositions. Jetpack Compose does it with a compiler plugin. Kompoz does it at run time, with std::source_location : Every composable takes a last parameter CallSite site = CallSite::current() . The default argument is evaluated where the composable is called , so site is the file, line and column of the call. Inside a parent, calls from the same site are told apart by their order: the first call from a site, the second call from it, and so on. You don't pass site yourself: the default does it. You only write it when you define a composable that needs its own identity: void Badge ( int count, CallSite site = CallSite :: current ()) { composable (site, count, [=] { Text (std:: to_string (count)); }); } remember takes a CallSite the same way, so two remember calls on different lines are different values."},{"p":"Lifecycle and identity","h":" What a plain function is","u":"lifecycle.html#what-a-plain-function-is","x":"A plain C++ function that calls composables, without composable or ComposableScope , has no identity of its own. The calls inside it are identified by their own call sites, inside the composable that called the function. That is fine, and most small helpers are written that way: void Header ( const std::string& title) { // a plain function Text (title, {.font = typography ().headlineSmall}); HorizontalDivider (); } What a plain function doesn't get is a scope : the states it reads belong to the nearest enclosing scope, which recomposes when they change, and it is never skipped on its own."},{"p":"Lifecycle and identity","h":" Scopes","u":"lifecycle.html#scopes","x":"composable(site, args…, body) makes a restartable and skippable scope, what an ordinary @Composable function is in Kotlin: Restartable : when a state the body read changes, only this body runs again, not the parent. Skippable : when the parent runs and args… compare equal to last time, the body doesn't run. Because the body can run on its own later, it must capture by value: [=] . ComposableScope scope(site); gives a composable an identity without making it a scope. Components that take a content lambda use it, the way Column and Row are inline functions in Compose: their content lambdas capture by reference and can't be run again on their own, so the state they read belongs to the caller. void Section ( const std::string& title, const Content & content, CallSite site = CallSite :: current ()) { ComposableScope scope (site); Column ( Modifier (). padding ( 16 ), [&] { Text (title, {.font = typography ().titleMedium}); content (); }); }"},{"p":"Lifecycle and identity","h":" Skipping","u":"lifecycle.html#skipping","x":"A scope is skipped when its parent runs, its arguments compare equal to the previous call, and nothing it read has changed since. For that, arguments need == : Numbers, strings, enums and your own structs with operator== (or = default ) compare by value. A MutableState compares by identity: the same state is equal even if its value changed. The scope still recomposes when the value changes, because it read it. A Modifier compares by the values of its elements. std::function can't be compared. A composable that takes one is restartable but never skipped. struct Todo { int id = 0 ; std::string title; bool done = false ; friend bool operator ==( const Todo &, const Todo &) = default ; }; void TodoRow ( const Todo & todo, MutableState <std::vector< Todo >> todos, CallSite site = CallSite :: current ()) { composable (site, todo, todos, [=] { // skipped while todo and todos are the same Row ([&] { Checkbox (todo.done, [todos, id = todo.id]( bool done) { todos. update ([&](std::vector< Todo >& list) { for ( Todo & t : list) if (t.id == id) t.done = done; }); }); Text (todo.title); }); }); } When one todo changes, the list recomposes, and every other TodoRow is skipped. Text is skippable too. Most components that take content lambdas are not scopes and run with their caller; that is cheap, because what they lay out only changes when their inputs do."},{"p":"Lifecycle and identity","h":" Calls in loops","u":"lifecycle.html#calls-in-loops","x":"Inside a loop, every iteration calls from the same site, so the calls are identified by their order. That works while the list only grows at the end. When items are inserted, removed or reordered, state would stay with the position , not the item: for ( const Todo & todo : todos. get ()) { TodoRow (todo, todos); // insert at the front, and every row's remembered state shifts by one } key gives each call an identity of your choosing: void TodoList ( MutableState <std::vector< Todo >> todos) { Column ([&] { for ( const Todo & todo : todos. get ()) { key (todo.id, [&] { TodoRow (todo, todos); }); } }); } Now each row's remembered values, effects and nodes follow its id . Lazy lists take keys in their items calls for the same reason (see Lists and grids )."},{"p":"Lifecycle and identity","h":" Moving content","u":"lifecycle.html#moving-content","x":"Calling the same composable from a different place in the tree is a different call: its state starts over. movableContentOf makes content whose state moves with it, as in Compose: void Player ( bool wide) { auto video = remember ([] { return movableContentOf ([] { auto position = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); // kept when it moves Text ( \"Playing at \" + std:: to_string (position. get ())); }); }); if (wide) { Row ([&] { video (); Text ( \"Comments\" ); }); } else { Column ([&] { video (); Text ( \"Comments\" ); }); } } When wide changes, the content's remembered values, nodes and running effects move from the column to the row instead of being made again."},{"p":"Lifecycle and identity","h":" See also","u":"lifecycle.html#see-also","x":"State : remember , rememberSaveable and state holders. Side effects : effects that start when a composable enters and stop when it leaves. Phases : what happens after composition."},{"p":"Side effects","h":"","u":"side-effects.html","x":"A side effect is a change to the world outside a composable: starting a timer, registering a listener, loading data. Composables run at times you don't control, so effects go through APIs that run them at defined moments. This page covers those APIs and Kompoz's coroutines."},{"p":"Side effects","h":" Coroutines","u":"side-effects.html#coroutines","x":"Kompoz's asynchronous code uses C++20 coroutines. A coroutine here is a lambda that returns Task and uses co_await where it waits: using namespace std::chrono_literals; auto seconds = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); LaunchedEffect ( 0 , [seconds]() -> Task { while ( true ) { co_await delay (1s); seconds. set (seconds. get () + 1 ); } }); Text (std:: to_string (seconds. get ()) + \" s\" ); Coroutines run on the UI thread, driven by the frame clock, so they can read and write state directly. They are cancelled when their scope ends: the coroutine is destroyed at the co_await where it waits, running the destructors of what it holds. That is why the loop above can be endless: it stops when the composable leaves. Things you can wait for: A coroutine that gives back a value returns TaskOf<T> and uses co_return ; another coroutine awaits it, and it runs as part of the caller and is cancelled with it: TaskOf <std::string> loadName ( int id) { co_await delay (std::chrono:: milliseconds ( 300 )); co_return \"User \" + std:: to_string (id); } void UserName ( int id) { auto name = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf (std:: string ( \"…\" )); }); LaunchedEffect (id, [id, name]() -> Task { name. set ( co_await loadName (id)); }); Text (name. get ()); } A coroutine's captures are kept alive while it runs, so capture by value . A lambda that captures by reference outlives the composable that made it, and its references dangle."},{"p":"Side effects ›  Coroutines","h":"co_await delay(300ms)","u":"side-effects.html#coroutines","x":"delay(300) For a time, on the frame clock."},{"p":"Side effects ›  Coroutines","h":"co_await awaitFrame()","u":"side-effects.html#coroutines","x":"withFrameNanos { } Until the next frame; returns its time in nanoseconds."},{"p":"Side effects ›  Coroutines","h":"co_await withBackground(work)","u":"side-effects.html#coroutines","x":"withContext(Dispatchers.IO) { } While work runs on a worker thread; returns what it returned."},{"p":"Side effects ›  Coroutines","h":"co_await anim.animateTo(1.f)","u":"side-effects.html#coroutines","x":"animateTo(1f) Until an animation ends (see Animation )."},{"p":"Side effects ›  Coroutines","h":"co_await f() , where f returns TaskOf<T>","u":"side-effects.html#coroutines","x":"a suspend fun Until another coroutine gives its result."},{"p":"Side effects","h":" Heavy work","u":"side-effects.html#heavy-work","x":"Coroutines run on the UI thread: a long computation in one freezes the UI just as it would anywhere else. Put reading files, decoding and heavy sums on a worker thread with withBackground , and come back to the UI thread with the result: void FileView ( const std::string& path) { auto text = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf (std:: string ()); }); LaunchedEffect (path, [path, text]() -> Task { text. set ( co_await withBackground ([path] { return readFile (path); })); }); Text (text. get ()); } The work on the worker thread mustn't touch composition state; it returns its result, and the coroutine, back on the UI thread, sets the state. If the coroutine is cancelled meanwhile, the work still runs to its end and its result is dropped. On the web there are no threads, so the work runs right away."},{"p":"Side effects","h":" LaunchedEffect","u":"side-effects.html#launchedeffect","x":"LaunchedEffect(key, coroutine) runs a coroutine when the composable enters the composition, and again whenever key changes, cancelling the previous run first. It is cancelled when the composable leaves. void Greeting ( const std::string& user, SnackbarHostState snackbar) { LaunchedEffect (user, [user, snackbar]() -> Task { co_await snackbar. showSnackbarAsync ( \"Welcome back, \" + user); }); } Use a key that changes exactly when the effect should restart: an id, a URL. For an effect that should run once, use a constant ( 0 ), like Compose's LaunchedEffect(Unit) . The key must compare with == . For several, use a std::tuple ."},{"p":"Side effects","h":" rememberCoroutineScope","u":"side-effects.html#remembercoroutinescope","x":"LaunchedEffect starts work from composition. To start work from an event, such as a click, use a CoroutineScope from rememberCoroutineScope . Its coroutines are cancelled when the composable that made it leaves. void ScrollUpButton ( LazyListState list) { auto scope = rememberCoroutineScope (); Button ([scope, list] { scope. launch ([list]() -> Task { co_await list. animateScrollToItem ( 0 ); }); }, [] { Text ( \"Top\" ); }); }"},{"p":"Side effects","h":" DisposableEffect","u":"side-effects.html#disposableeffect","x":"For effects that need cleaning up, such as registering and unregistering a listener, DisposableEffect(key, effect) runs effect after composition when it enters or when key changes. The function it returns runs when it leaves, or before it runs again for a new key: void Compass () { auto heading = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0.f ); }); DisposableEffect ( 0 , [heading] { sensor (). subscribe ([heading]( float h) { heading. set (h); }); return [] { sensor (). unsubscribe (); }; // like Compose's onDispose { } }); Text (std:: to_string ( int (heading. get ())) + \"°\" ); }"},{"p":"Side effects","h":" SideEffect","u":"side-effects.html#sideeffect","x":"SideEffect(effect) runs after every successful composition of the enclosing scope. Use it to hand state to something that isn't managed by Kompoz, such as an analytics library, after the UI it describes was composed: void Shop ( const std::string& userType) { SideEffect ([userType] { analytics (). setUserType (userType); }); Text ( \"Shop\" ); }"},{"p":"Side effects","h":" rememberUpdatedState","u":"side-effects.html#rememberupdatedstate","x":"A long-running effect keyed to a constant doesn't restart when the composable's parameters change, so it would keep using the values it captured first. rememberUpdatedState holds the latest value for it to read: void SplashScreen (std::function< void ()> onTimeout) { auto currentOnTimeout = rememberUpdatedState (onTimeout); LaunchedEffect ( 0 , [currentOnTimeout]() -> Task { // not restarted when onTimeout changes co_await delay (std::chrono:: seconds ( 3 )); currentOnTimeout. get ()(); // the latest one }); Text ( \"Loading…\" ); }"},{"p":"Side effects","h":" produceState","u":"side-effects.html#producestate","x":"produceState(initial, key, producer) turns the result of a coroutine into a value that composition reads. The coroutine gets a MutableState to set, and is restarted when key changes: void Downloads ( const std::string& url) { int count = produceState ( 0 , url, [url]( MutableState < int > state) -> Task { state. set ( co_await fetchCount (url)); }); Text (std:: to_string (count) + \" files\" ); }"},{"p":"Side effects","h":" snapshotFlow","u":"side-effects.html#snapshotflow","x":"snapshotFlow(block) watches states from inside a coroutine: it calls you with the block's value now, and again each time a state the block read changes it to a different value. It goes on until the coroutine is cancelled. void ScrollLogger ( LazyListState list) { LaunchedEffect ( 0 , [list]() -> Task { co_await snapshotFlow ([list] { return list. firstVisibleItemIndex (); }). collect ([]( int first) { std:: printf ( \"first visible: %d\\n\" , first); }); }); } Use it to react to state changes with effects (logging, saving, analytics) without recomposing."},{"p":"Side effects","h":" Choosing an API","u":"side-effects.html#choosing-an-api","x":""},{"p":"Side effects ›  Choosing an API","h":"Start async work when something appears or a value changes","u":"side-effects.html#choosing-an-api","x":"LaunchedEffect"},{"p":"Side effects ›  Choosing an API","h":"Start async work from a click or another event","u":"side-effects.html#choosing-an-api","x":"rememberCoroutineScope"},{"p":"Side effects ›  Choosing an API","h":"Register something and unregister it later","u":"side-effects.html#choosing-an-api","x":"DisposableEffect"},{"p":"Side effects ›  Choosing an API","h":"Push state to code outside Kompoz after each composition","u":"side-effects.html#choosing-an-api","x":"SideEffect"},{"p":"Side effects ›  Choosing an API","h":"Use the latest callback inside a long-running effect","u":"side-effects.html#choosing-an-api","x":"rememberUpdatedState"},{"p":"Side effects ›  Choosing an API","h":"Turn a coroutine's result into a value","u":"side-effects.html#choosing-an-api","x":"produceState"},{"p":"Side effects ›  Choosing an API","h":"React to state changes inside a coroutine","u":"side-effects.html#choosing-an-api","x":"snapshotFlow"},{"p":"Side effects","h":" See also","u":"side-effects.html#see-also","x":"State : what effects usually change. Architecture : flows, ViewModel s and their viewModelScope() . Lifecycle and identity : when effects start and stop."},{"p":"Phases","h":"","u":"phases.html","x":"Each frame goes through three phases: composition decides what to show, layout decides where , drawing decides how it looks . Knowing which phase reads a state tells you how much work a change costs."},{"p":"Phases","h":" The three phases","u":"phases.html#the-three-phases","x":"Composition runs your composable functions and turns them into a tree of layout nodes, each with its modifiers. Layout measures each node with the constraints its parent gives it and places its children. A node is measured once per pass. Drawing draws each node, its modifiers' backgrounds, borders and custom drawing, in order. Then the frame is presented. A frame in which nothing visible changed is neither drawn nor presented. The phases normally go in that order, but each can be skipped. When a state changes, Kompoz does only the phases that depend on it:"},{"p":"Phases ›  The three phases","h":"In a composable's body","u":"phases.html#the-three-phases","x":"Composition of the scopes that read it, then layout and drawing of what changed."},{"p":"Phases ›  The three phases","h":"While measuring or placing ( Modifier().offset([] { … }) , a Layout 's measure policy)","u":"phases.html#the-three-phases","x":"Layout and drawing; no composition."},{"p":"Phases ›  The three phases","h":"While drawing ( graphicsLayer([] (GraphicsLayerScope&) { … }) , drawBehind , a Canvas )","u":"phases.html#the-three-phases","x":"Drawing; a layout pass looks, but measures nothing."},{"p":"Phases","h":" Deferring state reads","u":"phases.html#deferring-state-reads","x":"The later the phase that reads a changing value, the less work each change costs. This matters for values that change every frame: animations, scroll positions, drags. Reading an animated value in composition recomposes on every frame: void Pulse () { auto pulse = rememberInfiniteTransition (). animateFloatState ( 0.8f , 1.f , infiniteRepeatable ( tween ( 600 ), RepeatMode :: Reverse )); // read while drawing: only drawn again each frame, never recomposed or measured Box ( Modifier (). size ( 48 ). graphicsLayer ([pulse]( GraphicsLayerScope & l) { l.scaleX = l.scaleY = pulse. get (); }) . background ( colorScheme ().primary, CircleShape )); } The lambda form of graphicsLayer is evaluated while drawing, so the scale changes without composition or layout. The value form, graphicsLayer({.alpha = 0.5f}) , is read in composition, which is fine for values that don't change often. The same goes for moving something. offset(x, y) takes values read in composition; offset([] { … }) takes a lambda read during layout: void DraggableBall () { auto x = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0.f ); }); Box ( Modifier (). offset ([x] { return Offset {x. get (), 0 }; }) // read during layout . draggable ( rememberDraggableState ([x]( float delta) { x. set (x. get () + delta); }), Orientation :: Horizontal ) . size ( 56 ) . background ( colorScheme ().tertiary, CircleShape )); } Dragging the ball changes x on every pointer move. Because only the layout phase read it, nothing recomposes: the ball is placed again and drawn."},{"p":"Phases","h":" Incremental layout","u":"phases.html#incremental-layout","x":"Layout in Kompoz is incremental, as in Compose. A node measured again with the same constraints as last time, with nothing changed in it or below it, keeps its size and its children's positions without measuring them. Only nodes that were composed again, or that read a state that changed while they were measured, are measured again, along with the parents whose size depends on them. You don't need to do anything for this. It does mean a change deep in a large tree costs little, as long as it doesn't change the size of what is around it. KOMPOZ_LAYOUT_CHECK=1 checks each incremental layout against a full one, and logs any difference; the tests always run with it."},{"p":"Phases","h":" Drawing only what changed","u":"phases.html#drawing-only-what-changed","x":"Kompoz records each frame as a list of drawing commands and compares it with the frame before. Only the parts of the screen that changed are drawn again; the rest of the pixels are kept. You can see this with KOMPOZ_DAMAGE_SHOW=1 , which outlines what was redrawn. See Tools ."},{"p":"Phases","h":" Performance tips","u":"phases.html#performance-tips","x":""},{"p":"Phases","h":" Do expensive work once","u":"phases.html#do-expensive-work-once","x":"Anything a composable computes runs again on each recomposition. Compute it once with remember , keyed to its inputs: void Contacts ( const std::vector< Contact >& contacts) { auto sorted = remember (contacts, [&] { // sorted again only when the contacts change std::vector< Contact > s = contacts; std::ranges:: sort (s, {}, & Contact ::name); return s; }); LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( LazyListScope & list) { list. items (sorted, []( const Contact & c) { Text (c.name); }); }); } Better still, keep the sorted list in state or in a ViewModel , so it is sorted when it changes, not when the UI recomposes."},{"p":"Phases","h":" Use keys in lazy lists","u":"phases.html#use-keys-in-lazy-lists","x":"Without keys, a lazy list ties item state to positions, and inserting at the top recomposes every item. With keys, items that moved keep their composition. See Lists and grids ."},{"p":"Phases","h":" Use derivedStateOf for values that change less often than their inputs","u":"phases.html#use-derivedstateof-for-values-that-change-less-often-than-their-inputs","x":"A scroll position changes on every frame of a scroll, but \"is the list scrolled at all\" changes once. Reading the position recomposes on every frame; reading a derivedStateOf recomposes once. See State ."},{"p":"Phases","h":" Let skipping work","u":"phases.html#let-skipping-work","x":"A scope is skipped only when its arguments compare equal. Pass values that compare cheaply and correctly: structs with operator== , states (which compare by identity) rather than copies of big containers. A composable that takes a std::function is never skipped."},{"p":"Phases","h":" Don't write state you just read","u":"phases.html#dont-write-state-you-just-read","x":"Writing, during composition, a state that this composition already read makes Kompoz compose again at once, and doing it on every pass never settles. Update state in callbacks and effects, or compute the value instead of storing it."},{"p":"Phases","h":" See also","u":"phases.html#see-also","x":"Lifecycle and identity : scopes and skipping. Custom layouts : measuring and placing yourself. Graphics : drawing and graphicsLayer . Tools : the profiler and the recomposition highlighter."},{"p":"CompositionLocal","h":"","u":"composition-local.html","x":"A CompositionLocal hands a value down the composition tree without passing it through every function in between. The theme's colors, the text style and the layout direction reach every component this way."},{"p":"CompositionLocal","h":" Reading a local","u":"composition-local.html#reading-a-local","x":"Every local has a value at every point in the tree: what the nearest provider above gives, or else its default. current() reads it: void Caption ( const std::string& text) { Color color = LocalContentColor . current (); // the color text and icons use here Text (text, {.font = typography ().labelSmall, .color = color}); } Reading a local subscribes the running scope, like reading a state: when a provider above starts giving a different value, the scopes that read it recompose, even if everything between them was skipped. The theme accessors are locals underneath: colorScheme() is LocalColorScheme.current() , typography() is LocalTypography.current() , isSystemInDarkTheme() reads LocalSystemDarkTheme ."},{"p":"CompositionLocal","h":" Providing a value","u":"composition-local.html#providing-a-value","x":"CompositionLocalProvider gives one or more locals a value for everything inside its content: void Warning ( const std::string& message) { CompositionLocalProvider ({ LocalContentColor . provides ( colorScheme ().error)}, [&] { Row ([&] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Info , \"\" ); // tinted with the error color Text (message); // so is the text }); }); } The value only applies inside the content lambda; outside it, the local has its previous value. Providers nest: an inner one overrides an outer one for its own content. providesDefault(value) gives a value only where no provider above has given one: a library's way of setting a default that an app can still override."},{"p":"CompositionLocal","h":" Defining your own","u":"composition-local.html#defining-your-own","x":"A local is identified by its address, so define it once, as a global, with its default: struct Spacing { float small = 4 , medium = 8 , large = 16 ; friend bool operator ==( const Spacing &, const Spacing &) = default ; }; inline const CompositionLocal < Spacing > LocalSpacing { Spacing {}}; void Card1 ( const std::string& title) { Column ( Modifier (). padding ( LocalSpacing . current ().large), [&] { Text (title); }); } void Compact ( const Content & content) { CompositionLocalProvider ({ LocalSpacing . provides ( Spacing { 2 , 4 , 8 })}, content); } When there is no sensible default, make the local with compositionLocalOf , whose default is made only when it is read without a provider; it can throw to say that the local must be provided: struct User { std::string name; friend bool operator ==( const User &, const User &) = default ; }; inline const auto LocalUser = compositionLocalOf< User >([]() -> User { throw std:: logic_error ( \"LocalUser was not provided\" ); }); staticCompositionLocalOf exists for code coming from Compose; in Kompoz both kinds recompose only the scopes that read the local. Give a local's type operator== : when a provider gives an equal value, nothing recomposes."},{"p":"CompositionLocal","h":" Built-in locals","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":""},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalColorScheme , LocalTypography , LocalMotionScheme","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"The Material theme (read with colorScheme() , typography() , motionScheme() )."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalContentColor","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"The color for text and icons here; Material containers set it to their content color."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalTextStyle","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"The font Text uses when none is given."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalLayoutDirection","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"Left to right or right to left; from the system language at the root."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalDensity","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"The screen's density and the system's font scale."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalWindowSize , LocalWindowInfo","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"The window's size in dp, and whether it has focus."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalSystemDarkTheme","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"Whether the system is in dark mode (read with isSystemInDarkTheme() )."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalWindowInsets , LocalSystemInsets","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"The system bars', cutout's and keyboard's insets."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalViewConfiguration","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"Touch slop, long press timeout and the platform's other gesture thresholds."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalIndication","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"What clickables draw when pressed: Material's ripple by default."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalMinimumInteractiveComponentSize","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"The smallest touch target components keep (48 dp)."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalTextSelectionColors","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"The colors of selected text and its handles."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalScrollbarStyle","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"How scrollbars look."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalStrings","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"The library's own texts, in English or Turkish (see Resources )."},{"p":"CompositionLocal ›  Built-in locals","h":"LocalUiMediaScope","u":"composition-local.html#built-in-locals","x":"The window's size, pointer and keyboard, for mediaQuery ."},{"p":"CompositionLocal","h":" When to use one","u":"composition-local.html#when-to-use-one","x":"A local is implicit: a function that reads one depends on something its signature doesn't show. Use them sparingly: Good uses are values that much of the tree needs and that rarely change: a theme, a design system's tokens, a text direction, the current user's settings. Avoid them for data only a few composables need, and for state that changes often. Pass those as parameters, so the dependency is visible and the composable is easy to preview and test. A local isn't a replacement for state hoisting: it hands values down, not events up."},{"p":"CompositionLocal","h":" Passing locals to another composition","u":"composition-local.html#passing-locals-to-another-composition","x":"Content composed elsewhere, such as in a popup, already gets the locals of the place it is called from. If you need to carry them by hand, currentCompositionLocalContext() captures the locals at a point and CompositionLocalProvider can provide them again."},{"p":"CompositionLocal","h":" See also","u":"composition-local.html#see-also","x":"Theming : Material's theme is built on locals. State : explicit data flow with parameters and hoisting."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":"","u":"modifiers.html","x":"A Modifier decorates or augments a composable: its size and padding, its background, what happens when it is clicked, what it tells accessibility services. Modifiers chain, and the order of the chain matters."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Using modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#using-modifiers","x":"Most composables take a Modifier as a parameter. A chain starts from Modifier() and each call adds an element: void ArtistCard ( const std::string& name) { Row ( Modifier () . fillMaxWidth () . clickable ([] { /* open the artist */ }) . padding ( 16 ), [&] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Person , \"\" , Modifier (). size ( 40 )); Spacer ( Modifier (). width ( 12 )); Text (name, {.font = typography ().titleMedium}); }); } Modifiers are plain values: you can store them in variables, pass them around and build them conditionally. Modifier base = Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (). padding ( 8 ); Box (selected ? base. background ( colorScheme ().secondaryContainer, 12 ) : base);"},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Order matters","u":"modifiers.html#order-matters","x":"Each element wraps everything after it in the chain. The first element is the outermost: it is measured first and draws around the rest. So the same elements in a different order give a different result: // The whole 72 dp area is clickable, and the padding is inside the click area. Box ( Modifier (). clickable ([] {}). padding ( 16 ). size ( 40 )); // Only the 40 dp box is clickable; the padding is outside it. Box ( Modifier (). padding ( 16 ). clickable ([] {}). size ( 40 )); The same goes for backgrounds: Box ( Modifier (). background ( colorScheme ().primaryContainer). padding ( 16 ). size ( 40 )); // padding is colored Box ( Modifier (). padding ( 16 ). background ( colorScheme ().primaryContainer). size ( 40 )); // padding is not Read a chain from left to right as \"from the outside in\"."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Common modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#common-modifiers","x":""},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Size","u":"modifiers.html#size","x":"All lengths are in dp."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Size","h":"size(s) , size(w, h) , width(w) , height(h)","u":"modifiers.html#size","x":"A preferred size, within the parent's constraints."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Size","h":"requiredSize(…) , requiredWidth(w) , requiredHeight(h)","u":"modifiers.html#size","x":"The size even if the parent's constraints say otherwise."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Size","h":"widthIn(min, max) , heightIn(min, max) , sizeIn(…)","u":"modifiers.html#size","x":"Bounds on the size."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Size","h":"defaultMinSize(w, h)","u":"modifiers.html#size","x":"A minimum the content may override."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Size","h":"fillMaxWidth(f) , fillMaxHeight(f) , fillMaxSize(f)","u":"modifiers.html#size","x":"All of the space the parent offers (or a fraction of it)."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Size","h":"wrapContentSize(align) , wrapContentWidth , wrapContentHeight","u":"modifiers.html#size","x":"The content's own size, aligned in what the parent forces."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Size","h":"aspectRatio(r)","u":"modifiers.html#size","x":"A width-to-height ratio within the constraints."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Size","h":"width(IntrinsicSize::Min) , height(IntrinsicSize::Max)","u":"modifiers.html#size","x":"The content's intrinsic size (see Custom layouts )."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Padding and position","u":"modifiers.html#padding-and-position","x":""},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Padding and position","h":"padding(all) , padding(h, v) , padding(start, top, end, bottom) , padding(PaddingValues)","u":"modifiers.html#padding-and-position","x":"Space around the content. start and end swap in right-to-left layouts."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Padding and position","h":"absolutePadding(left, top, right, bottom)","u":"modifiers.html#padding-and-position","x":"Padding that doesn't mirror."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Padding and position","h":"offset(x, y)","u":"modifiers.html#padding-and-position","x":"Moves the content without changing its layout size."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Padding and position","h":"offset([] { return Offset{…}; })","u":"modifiers.html#padding-and-position","x":"The same, read during layout: for offsets that change often (see Phases )."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Padding and position","h":"windowInsetsPadding(insets) , safeDrawingPadding() , systemBarsPadding() , imePadding() …","u":"modifiers.html#padding-and-position","x":"Keeps content clear of the system bars, the display cutout or the keyboard."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Drawing","u":"modifiers.html#drawing","x":""},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Drawing","h":"background(color, shape) , background(brush, shape)","u":"modifiers.html#drawing","x":"Fills behind the content."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Drawing","h":"border(width, color, shape)","u":"modifiers.html#drawing","x":"Draws an outline."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Drawing","h":"clip(shape)","u":"modifiers.html#drawing","x":"Clips the content to a shape."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Drawing","h":"shadow(elevation, shape) , dropShadow , innerShadow","u":"modifiers.html#drawing","x":"Shadows."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Drawing","h":"alpha(a) , scale(s) , rotate(degrees) , graphicsLayer(…) , blur(r)","u":"modifiers.html#drawing","x":"Changes how the content is drawn, not its layout."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Drawing","h":"drawBehind(…) , drawWithContent(…) , drawWithCache(…)","u":"modifiers.html#drawing","x":"Your own drawing (see Graphics )."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Interaction","u":"modifiers.html#interaction","x":""},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Interaction","h":"clickable(onClick) , combinedClickable(onClick, onLongClick)","u":"modifiers.html#interaction","x":"Clicks and taps, with a ripple, keyboard activation and accessibility."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Interaction","h":"selectable(selected, onClick, role) , toggleable(value, onChange, role)","u":"modifiers.html#interaction","x":"Selection and toggling, for rows that act as a radio button or a checkbox."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Interaction","h":"draggable , anchoredDraggable , transformable , pointerInput","u":"modifiers.html#interaction","x":"Gestures (see Gestures )."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Interaction","h":"verticalScroll(state) , horizontalScroll(state) , scrollable","u":"modifiers.html#interaction","x":"Scrolling (see Lists and grids )."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Interaction","h":"focusable() , focusRequester(r) , onKeyEvent(…)","u":"modifiers.html#interaction","x":"Keyboard focus and keys."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Interaction","h":"pointerHoverIcon(PointerIcon::Hand)","u":"modifiers.html#interaction","x":"The mouse cursor over it."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Accessibility and tests","u":"modifiers.html#accessibility-and-tests","x":""},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Accessibility and tests","h":"semantics({…}) , clearAndSetSemantics({…})","u":"modifiers.html#accessibility-and-tests","x":"What accessibility services are told (see Accessibility )."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Accessibility and tests","h":"testTag(\"name\")","u":"modifiers.html#accessibility-and-tests","x":"A name tests can find the node by (see Testing )."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Layout callbacks","u":"modifiers.html#layout-callbacks","x":""},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Layout callbacks","h":"onSizeChanged([](Size s) { … })","u":"modifiers.html#layout-callbacks","x":"Called when the node's size changes."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Layout callbacks","h":"onGloballyPositioned([](const LayoutCoordinates& c) { … })","u":"modifiers.html#layout-callbacks","x":"Called when its position in the window changes."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Layout callbacks","h":"onVisibilityChanged([](bool visible) { … })","u":"modifiers.html#layout-callbacks","x":"Called when it becomes visible or stops being."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Layout callbacks","h":"zIndex(z)","u":"modifiers.html#layout-callbacks","x":"Draws it above or below its siblings."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Scope modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#scope-modifiers","x":"Some modifiers only make sense inside a particular parent, like Compose's scoped modifiers ( RowScope.weight ): In C++ these are ordinary Modifier methods: the compiler won't stop you from using weight outside a Row or a Column , it just has no effect there. Row ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), [&] { Text ( \"Left\" , Modifier (). weight ( 1 )); Text ( \"Right\" ); });"},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Scope modifiers","h":"weight(w)","u":"modifiers.html#scope-modifiers","x":"Row , Column Shares the space left over among weighted children."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Scope modifiers","h":"align(Alignment::…)","u":"modifiers.html#scope-modifiers","x":"Box Aligns this child in the box."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Scope modifiers","h":"align(Alignment::CenterHorizontally)","u":"modifiers.html#scope-modifiers","x":"Column Aligns this child across the column."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Scope modifiers","h":"align(Alignment::CenterVertically) , alignByBaseline()","u":"modifiers.html#scope-modifiers","x":"Row Aligns this child across the row."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Scope modifiers","h":"matchParentSize()","u":"modifiers.html#scope-modifiers","x":"Box As big as the box, without making it bigger."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Scope modifiers","h":"fillParentMaxWidth() , fillParentMaxHeight()","u":"modifiers.html#scope-modifiers","x":"Lazy list items A share of the list's viewport."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Scope modifiers","h":"animateItem()","u":"modifiers.html#scope-modifiers","x":"Lazy list items Animates the item when it appears, moves or goes away."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Scope modifiers","h":"flex(config)","u":"modifiers.html#scope-modifiers","x":"FlexBox How the child grows and shrinks."},{"p":"Modifiers ›  Scope modifiers","h":"gridItem(row, column, …)","u":"modifiers.html#scope-modifiers","x":"Grid Where the child goes in the grid."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Writing your own","u":"modifiers.html#writing-your-own","x":""},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Combining modifiers","u":"modifiers.html#combining-modifiers","x":"C++ has no extension functions, so a modifier of your own is a function that returns a chain. Join it to another chain with then : Modifier cardSurface () { return Modifier (). shadow ( 2 , RoundedCornerShape ( 16 )). background ( colorScheme ().surfaceContainerLow, RoundedCornerShape ( 16 )). padding ( 16 ); } void Note ( const std::string& text) { Box ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (). then ( cardSurface ()), [&] { Text (text); }); } A function that takes the chain to extend reads naturally, too: Modifier card(const Modifier& m = {}) { return m.padding(…); } ."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Changing measurement","u":"modifiers.html#changing-measurement","x":"layout measures the content through measure with the constraints you choose and returns this node's size; placeAt says where the content goes inside it: // The content centered in a square as big as its longer side. Modifier squareAround () { return Modifier (). layout ([]( const MeasureNext & measure, const Constraints & c, Offset & placeAt) { Size content = measure (c. loosen ()); float side = std:: max (content.width, content.height); placeAt = {(side - content.width) / 2 , (side - content.height) / 2 }; return c. constrain ( Size {side, side}); }); }"},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Custom drawing","u":"modifiers.html#custom-drawing","x":"drawBehind draws behind the content, drawWithContent around it. See Graphics . Modifier underline ( Color color) { return Modifier (). drawBehind ([color]( DrawScope & d) { d. drawLine (color, { 0 , d. size ().height}, {d. size ().width, d. size ().height}, 2 ); }); }"},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" Modifier elements","u":"modifiers.html#modifier-elements","x":"Underneath, a modifier is a list of ModifierElement s. An element can take part in measuring, drawing and input; then(std::make_shared<MyElement>(…)) adds one. This is a lower-level API, used by the library's own modifiers; the functions above cover most needs. If you write an element, implement equals so that a composable called with an equal modifier can still be skipped."},{"p":"Modifiers","h":" See also","u":"modifiers.html#see-also","x":"Layouts : the parents scope modifiers work in. Phases : modifiers that read state while laying out or drawing. Gestures and Graphics for the interaction and drawing modifiers."},{"p":"Layouts","h":"","u":"layouts.html","x":"Kompoz's layouts arrange children in columns, rows, stacks, wrapping lines and grids, and adapt to the window's size. This page covers the standard layouts; Custom layouts shows how to write your own."},{"p":"Layouts","h":" How layout works","u":"layouts.html#how-layout-works","x":"Layout happens in one pass down and up the tree: A parent gives each child constraints : a minimum and maximum width and height. Each child measures itself within them, measuring its own children first, and reports its size. The parent places its children and reports its own size. Each node is measured once per pass, so layout stays fast however deep the tree is. All sizes are in dp."},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Column, Row and Box","u":"layouts.html#column-row-and-box","x":"The three basic layouts: Column places its children one under another. Row places them side by side, from the start (left, or right in a right-to-left layout). Box stacks them on top of each other. void ProfileHeader ( const std::string& name, const std::string& handle) { Row ( Modifier (). padding ( 16 ), {.verticalAlignment = Alignment :: CenterVertically }, [&] { Box ([&] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: AccountCircle , \"\" , Modifier (). size ( 56 )); Box ( Modifier (). size ( 14 ). align ( Alignment :: BottomEnd ). background ( Color :: hex (0x2E7D32), CircleShape )); }); Spacer ( Modifier (). width ( 12 )); Column ([&] { Text (name, {.font = typography ().titleMedium}); Text (handle, {.font = typography ().bodyMedium, .color = colorScheme ().onSurfaceVariant}); }); }); }"},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Arrangement and alignment","u":"layouts.html#arrangement-and-alignment","x":"A Column 's style arranges children along its height and aligns them across its width; a Row 's does the opposite: Column ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), {.verticalArrangement = Arrangement :: SpaceBetween , .horizontalAlignment = Alignment :: CenterHorizontally }, [&] { Text ( \"Top\" ); Text ( \"Middle\" ); Text ( \"Bottom\" ); }); A single child can override its alignment: Modifier().align(Alignment::End) in a Column , Modifier().align(Alignment::CenterVertically) in a Row , Modifier().align(Alignment::BottomEnd) in a Box . A Box aligns all its children with its contentAlignment argument: Box(modifier, Alignment::Center, content) ."},{"p":"Layouts ›  Arrangement and alignment","h":"Arrangement::Start , Top","u":"layouts.html#arrangement-and-alignment","x":"Packed at the start."},{"p":"Layouts ›  Arrangement and alignment","h":"Arrangement::End , Bottom","u":"layouts.html#arrangement-and-alignment","x":"Packed at the end."},{"p":"Layouts ›  Arrangement and alignment","h":"Arrangement::Center","u":"layouts.html#arrangement-and-alignment","x":"Packed in the middle."},{"p":"Layouts ›  Arrangement and alignment","h":"Arrangement::SpaceBetween","u":"layouts.html#arrangement-and-alignment","x":"Spread out, with no space at the ends."},{"p":"Layouts ›  Arrangement and alignment","h":"Arrangement::SpaceAround","u":"layouts.html#arrangement-and-alignment","x":"Spread out, with half the space at the ends."},{"p":"Layouts ›  Arrangement and alignment","h":"Arrangement::SpaceEvenly","u":"layouts.html#arrangement-and-alignment","x":"Spread out, with equal space everywhere."},{"p":"Layouts ›  Arrangement and alignment","h":"Arrangement::spacedBy(8)","u":"layouts.html#arrangement-and-alignment","x":"Packed with 8 dp between each; spacedBy(8, Arrangement::Kind::Center) packs them in the middle."},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Weight","u":"layouts.html#weight","x":"weight shares the space a Row or Column has left, after the unweighted children are measured, among the weighted ones in proportion: Row ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (). height ( 48 ), [&] { Box ( Modifier (). weight ( 2 ). fillMaxHeight (). background ( colorScheme ().primary)); // two thirds Box ( Modifier (). weight ( 1 ). fillMaxHeight (). background ( colorScheme ().secondary)); // one third }); weight(w, false) lets the child be smaller than its share."},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Spacer","u":"layouts.html#spacer","x":"Spacer(Modifier().width(8)) is empty space; Spacer(Modifier().weight(1)) pushes what comes after it to the end."},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Wrapping lines","u":"layouts.html#wrapping-lines","x":"FlowRow places children in a row and starts a new row when one is full, as chips or tags need; FlowColumn does the same in columns. FlowRow ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), {.horizontalSpacing = 8 , .verticalSpacing = 8 }, [&] { for ( const std::string& tag : tags) AssistChip ([] {}, [&] { Text (tag); }); }); maxItemsInEachLine limits how many go on one line."},{"p":"Layouts","h":" FlexBox","u":"layouts.html#flexbox","x":"FlexBox is CSS's flexbox, as in Compose 1.12: a superset of Row , Column and the flow layouts. Children go along the main axis ( direction ), wrap or not ( wrap ), are spread along it ( justifyContent ) and lined up across their line ( alignItems ). Each child takes its share with Modifier().flex({.grow, .shrink, .basis}) : FlexBox ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), {.wrap = FlexWrap :: Wrap , .justifyContent = FlexJustifyContent :: SpaceBetween , .alignItems = FlexAlignItems :: Center , .rowGap = 8 , .columnGap = 8 }, [&] { OutlinedTextField ( \"\" , [](std::string) {}, Modifier (). flex ({.grow = 1 }), {.label = \"Search\" }); Button ([] {}, [] { Text ( \"Go\" ); }); });"},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Grid","u":"layouts.html#grid","x":"Grid lays children out in rows and columns, as CSS grid and Compose 1.12's Grid . You give the column and row tracks; each child goes in the next free cell, or where Modifier().gridItem(row, column, rowSpan, columnSpan) puts it: Grid ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), {.columns = { GridTrackSize :: Fixed ( 96 ), GridTrackSize :: Flex ( 1 )}, .rowGap = 8 , .columnGap = 16 }, [&] { Text ( \"Name\" ); Text ( \"Ada Lovelace\" ); Text ( \"Born\" ); Text ( \"10 December 1815\" ); Text ( \"Known for\" , Modifier (). gridItem ( 3 , 1 )); Text ( \"The first published algorithm\" , Modifier (). gridItem ( 3 , 2 )); }); Track sizes: Fixed(dp) , Percentage(fraction) , Flex(weight) (a share of the room left), MinMax(minDp, weight) , MinContent() , MaxContent() and Auto() . Named areas in the config let children say Modifier().gridItem(\"header\") ."},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Scrolling","u":"layouts.html#scrolling","x":"A layout doesn't scroll by itself. verticalScroll and horizontalScroll make a Column or Row scroll when its content is bigger than the space it has: Column ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (). verticalScroll ( rememberScrollState ()), [&] { for ( int i = 0 ; i < 50 ; ++i) Text ( \"Line \" + std:: to_string (i)); }); This composes every child. For long or endless content, use a lazy list instead: see Lists and grids ."},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Adapting to the window","u":"layouts.html#adapting-to-the-window","x":""},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Size classes","u":"layouts.html#size-classes","x":"Lay out by the window's size class rather than by exact sizes. currentWindowAdaptiveInfo() gives the window's width and height classes, as Compose's adaptive library does: Compact (narrower than 600 dp), Medium (narrower than 840 dp) or Expanded. void MailScreen () { WindowAdaptiveInfo info = currentWindowAdaptiveInfo (); if (info.windowSizeClass.windowWidthSizeClass == WindowWidthSizeClass :: Expanded ) { Row ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&] { Box ( Modifier (). weight ( 1 ), [] { ListPane (); }); Box ( Modifier (). weight ( 2 ), [] { DetailPane (); }); }); } else { ListPane (); } } It recomposes when the window changes class. NavigationSuiteScaffold uses it to switch between a navigation bar and a rail (see Material components )."},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Media queries","u":"layouts.html#media-queries","x":"mediaQuery asks about what the app runs on: the window's size, the most precise pointer, whether there is a keyboard. derivedMediaQuery gives a state that changes only when the answer does, which is what you want for the window's size, since that changes at every step of a resize: void Adaptive () { auto wide = derivedMediaQuery ([]( const UiMediaScope & m) { return m. windowWidth () >= 600 ; }); if (wide. get ()) TwoPanes (); else OnePane (); if ( mediaQuery ([]( const UiMediaScope & m) { return m. pointerPrecision () == PointerPrecision :: Fine ; })) Text ( \"Hover for details\" ); }"},{"p":"Layouts","h":" BoxWithConstraints","u":"layouts.html#boxwithconstraints","x":"BoxWithConstraints gives its content the constraints the box is laid out with, so the content can choose a layout by the space it actually has, not by the window's: BoxWithConstraints ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), [&]( const Constraints & c) { if (c.maxWidth > 400 ) Text ( \"A wide layout\" ); else Text ( \"A narrow layout\" ); }); The very first composition sees unbounded constraints; the content recomposes in the same frame once they are known."},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Window insets","u":"layouts.html#window-insets","x":"On phones, the system bars, the display cutout and the keyboard cover parts of the window. Insets tell you how much, so content can stay clear of them: Scaffold passes its content padding that already keeps clear of the bars and the system insets. Modifier().safeDrawingPadding() , systemBarsPadding() , statusBarsPadding() , navigationBarsPadding() and imePadding() pad by one kind of inset. windowInsetsPadding(WindowInsets::systemBars()) takes any WindowInsets . Insets paddings don't add up: an inner one pads only by what outer ones didn't already take. In a Scaffold with a text field, pad the content as Compose does: Scaffold ( Modifier (), {}, [&]( PaddingValues padding) { Column ( Modifier (). padding (padding). consumeWindowInsets (padding). imePadding (), [&] { OutlinedTextField ( \"\" , [](std::string) {}); }); });"},{"p":"Layouts","h":" Right to left","u":"layouts.html#right-to-left","x":"When the system language is written right to left (Arabic, Hebrew, Persian…), the layout direction is right to left: Row s start at the right, start and end padding swap, and horizontal lists scroll from the right. CompositionLocalProvider({LocalLayoutDirection.provides(LayoutDirection::Rtl)}, …) changes it for part of the tree, and KOMPOZ_LAYOUT_DIRECTION=rtl for the whole app. Drawing, absoluteOffset and absolutePadding don't mirror."},{"p":"Layouts","h":" See also","u":"layouts.html#see-also","x":"Modifiers : size, padding and the scope modifiers. Custom layouts : Layout , intrinsics and SubcomposeLayout . Lists and grids : lazy layouts for long content."},{"p":"Custom layouts","h":"","u":"custom-layouts.html","x":"When Row , Column , Box and the other layouts don't fit, measure and place children yourself with Layout . This page also covers the layout modifier, intrinsic sizes, SubcomposeLayout and animating layout changes."},{"p":"Custom layouts","h":" Constraints","u":"custom-layouts.html#constraints","x":"A parent measures each child with Constraints : a minimum and maximum width and height, in dp. A child picks a size within them. Some helpers:"},{"p":"Custom layouts ›  Constraints","h":"Constraints::fixed(w, h)","u":"custom-layouts.html#constraints","x":"Exactly this size."},{"p":"Custom layouts ›  Constraints","h":"c.loosen()","u":"custom-layouts.html#constraints","x":"The same maximums, with minimums of zero: the child may be smaller."},{"p":"Custom layouts ›  Constraints","h":"c.constrain(size)","u":"custom-layouts.html#constraints","x":"size clamped into the constraints."},{"p":"Custom layouts ›  Constraints","h":"c.hasBoundedWidth() , c.hasBoundedHeight()","u":"custom-layouts.html#constraints","x":"Whether the maximum is finite: inside a vertical scroll, the height isn't."},{"p":"Custom layouts ›  Constraints","h":"c.deflate(h, v)","u":"custom-layouts.html#constraints","x":"The constraints with h and v dp taken off, for padding."},{"p":"Custom layouts","h":" Layout","u":"custom-layouts.html#layout","x":"Layout(modifier, content, measurePolicy) composes content and gives each of its children to measurePolicy as a Measurable . The policy measures them, places them and returns the layout's own size: // Children one under another, each pushed right by `indent` dp more than the one before. void Staircase ( float indent, const Content & content) { Layout ( Modifier (), content, [indent]( const std::vector< Measurable >& children, const Constraints & c) { float x = 0 , y = 0 , width = 0 ; for ( const Measurable & child : children) { Placeable p = child. measure (c. loosen ()); p. place (x, y); width = std:: max (width, x + p.width); x += indent; y += p.height; } return c. constrain ( Size {width, y}); }); } void Steps () { Staircase ( 24 , [] { Text ( \"Wake up\" ); Text ( \"Make coffee\" ); Text ( \"Write code\" ); }); } measure(constraints) measures a child and returns a Placeable with its width and height . Measure each child once per pass. place(x, y) puts it at that offset from the layout's start and top; an optional third argument is its z-index. In a right-to-left layout Kompoz mirrors what you placed, as Compose's placeRelative does: write the policy as if the layout were left to right. Return a size within the constraints you were given; c.constrain makes sure of it. The policy runs during layout, not composition. A state it reads lays the layout out again when it changes, without recomposing."},{"p":"Custom layouts","h":" Telling children apart","u":"custom-layouts.html#telling-children-apart","x":"A policy sees its children in order. To tell them apart by role, tag them with Modifier().layoutId(\"name\") and read child.layoutId() : void LabeledValue ( const std::string& label, const std::string& value) { Layout ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), [&] { Text (label, Modifier (). layoutId ( \"label\" )); Text (value, Modifier (). layoutId ( \"value\" ), {.fontWeight = FontWeight :: Bold }); }, []( const std::vector< Measurable >& children, const Constraints & c) { float height = 0 ; for ( const Measurable & child : children) { Placeable p = child. measure (c. loosen ()); // The label at the start, the value at the end. p. place (child. layoutId () == \"value\" ? c.maxWidth - p.width : 0 , 0 ); height = std:: max (height, p.height); } return c. constrain ( Size {c.maxWidth, height}); }); }"},{"p":"Custom layouts","h":" Alignment lines","u":"custom-layouts.html#alignment-lines","x":"A Placeable knows where its text baselines are: p[AlignmentLine::FirstBaseline] is the first baseline's distance from its top, or NaN if it has none. Use it to line up text of different sizes. The alignByBaseline() and paddingFromBaseline(top, bottom) modifiers cover the common cases without a custom layout."},{"p":"Custom layouts","h":" The layout modifier","u":"custom-layouts.html#the-layout-modifier","x":"To change how a single composable is measured or placed, use Modifier().layout instead of wrapping it in a Layout . It measures the content through measure , returns the node's size, and sets placeAt to where the content goes inside it: // At least `min` dp tall, the content centered vertically in that. Modifier atLeastHeight ( float min) { return Modifier (). layout ([min]( const MeasureNext & measure, const Constraints & c, Offset & placeAt) { Size content = measure (c); float height = std:: max (content.height, std:: min (min, c.maxHeight)); placeAt = { 0 , (height - content.height) / 2 }; return Size {content.width, height}; }); }"},{"p":"Custom layouts","h":" Intrinsic sizes","u":"custom-layouts.html#intrinsic-sizes","x":"Layout measures each child once, so a parent can't measure a child twice to find out what size it would like. Intrinsic sizes answer that question without measuring for real: how tall would this content be at its natural size? The typical use is making siblings as tall as the tallest one: Row ( Modifier (). height ( IntrinsicSize :: Min ), [&] { Text ( \"A short label\" , Modifier (). weight ( 1 )); VerticalDivider ( Modifier (). fillMaxHeight ()); // as tall as the taller text Text ( \"A label that wraps onto more than one line\" , Modifier (). weight ( 1 )); }); height(IntrinsicSize::Min) and width(IntrinsicSize::Min) (or Max ) size the node to its content's intrinsic size, and children that fill it then fill that. Note: Kompoz's intrinsics are an approximation of Compose's: both Min and Max measure the content with that axis unbounded, and a Layout can't override how its intrinsic sizes are computed. For a min intrinsic width, text breaks at every space, so it comes out as wide as its widest word."},{"p":"Custom layouts","h":" SubcomposeLayout","u":"custom-layouts.html#subcomposelayout","x":"Sometimes what to compose depends on a measurement: a header sized to match a body, a different layout for narrow constraints, a badge sized by the text it decorates. SubcomposeLayout composes parts of its content while it measures: // The content, with a line under it exactly as wide as the content turned out to be. void Underlined ( const Content & content) { SubcomposeLayout ( Modifier (), [content]( SubcomposeMeasureScope & s, const Constraints & c) { float width = 0 , height = 0 ; for ( const Measurable & m : s. subcompose (std:: string ( \"main\" ), content)) { Placeable p = m. measure (c. loosen ()); p. place ( 0 , height); width = std:: max (width, p.width); height += p.height; } // Composed with the width it has to match; composed again when that changes. for ( const Measurable & m : s. subcompose (std:: string ( \"line\" ), width, [width] { Box ( Modifier (). size (width, 2 ). background ( colorScheme ().primary)); })) { Placeable line = m. measure (c. loosen ()); line. place ( 0 , height); height += line.height; } return c. constrain ( Size {width, height}); }); } subcompose(slotId, content) composes a slot and returns its measurables. A slot is composed the first time it is asked for, and the layout runs again in the same frame. subcompose(slotId, dependency, content) composes the slot again when dependency changes. The content runs later, during composition, so capture by value. SubcomposeLayout costs more than Layout ; use it only when composition really depends on measurement. BoxWithConstraints is the common case of it, ready-made."},{"p":"Custom layouts","h":" Animating layout changes","u":"custom-layouts.html#animating-layout-changes","x":"Modifier().animateContentSize() animates a node's size when its content grows or shrinks. For a node that moves or resizes because the layout around it changed, Modifier().animateBounds() makes it glide from where it was drawn to where it is now, as Compose's animateBounds . Its position is measured from the nearest LookaheadScope : void Chips ( bool wide) { LookaheadScope ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), [&] { FlowRow ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), {.horizontalSpacing = 8 , .maxItemsInEachLine = wide ? 4 : 2 }, [&] { for ( const char * name : { \"One\" , \"Two\" , \"Three\" , \"Four\" }) AssistChip ([] {}, [&] { Text (name); }, {}, Modifier (). animateBounds ()); }); }); } Only the drawing moves; taps land where the node is laid out. skipToLookaheadSize() lays content out at the size it is going to rather than the sizes along the way, and approachLayout lets you write the in-between steps yourself."},{"p":"Custom layouts","h":" See also","u":"custom-layouts.html#see-also","x":"Layouts : the built-in layouts. Phases : what reading state during layout costs. Modifiers : the modifier chain that wraps every layout node."},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":"","u":"lists.html","x":"Lazy lists and grids compose only the items in view, so they scroll through thousands of items as smoothly as through ten. This page covers them, along with pagers, carousels, scroll modifiers, nested scrolling and pull to refresh."},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Scrolling a column","u":"lists.html#scrolling-a-column","x":"For a handful of items, a Column with verticalScroll is enough: Column ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (). verticalScroll ( rememberScrollState ()). padding ( 16 ), [&] { for ( int i = 0 ; i < 20 ; ++i) Text ( \"Paragraph \" + std:: to_string (i)); }); It composes and lays out every child, whether it is on the screen or not. For long lists, and lists whose length you don't know, use a lazy list."},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Lazy lists","u":"lists.html#lazy-lists","x":"LazyColumn and LazyRow compose items as they come into view, plus a small margin, and drop the ones that leave it. Instead of content, they take a lambda that describes the items through a LazyListScope : struct Message { int id; std::string author, body; }; void Conversation ( const std::vector< Message >& messages) { LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( LazyListScope & list) { list. item ([] { Text ( \"Today\" , {.font = typography ().labelLarge}); }); list. items (messages, []( const Message & m) { return m.id; }, []( const Message & m) { ListItem ({.headline = [&] { Text (m.author); }, .supporting = [&] { Text (m.body); }}); }); list. item ([] { Text ( \"No more messages\" ); }); }); } Item content runs later, when the item comes into view, not while the scope lambda runs. Capture what the item needs by value, or use the arguments it is given: the list you pass is read by the items, so it must outlive the composition that uses it, as a state's value or a member of a ViewModel does."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Lazy lists","h":"item(content) , item(key, content)","u":"lists.html#lazy-lists","x":"One item."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Lazy lists","h":"items(count, content)","u":"lists.html#lazy-lists","x":"count items; content gets the index."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Lazy lists","h":"items(list, content)","u":"lists.html#lazy-lists","x":"One item per element; content gets the element."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Lazy lists","h":"items(list, key, content)","u":"lists.html#lazy-lists","x":"The same, with a key per element."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Lazy lists","h":"items(list, key, contentType, content)","u":"lists.html#lazy-lists","x":"The same, with a content type (reported in layoutInfo() )."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Lazy lists","h":"itemsIndexed(list, content)","u":"lists.html#lazy-lists","x":"One item per element; content gets the index and the element."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Lazy lists","h":"stickyHeader(content)","u":"lists.html#lazy-lists","x":"A header that stays at the top while the items after it scroll under it."},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Keys","u":"lists.html#keys","x":"Without keys, an item's state is tied to its position. Insert an item at the top, and every remembered value shifts down by one. Give items a stable, unique key, and their state follows them: LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( LazyListScope & list) { list. items (messages, []( const Message & m) { return m.id; }, []( const Message & m) { auto expanded = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); // stays with the message Text (m.body, Modifier (). clickable ([expanded] { expanded. set (!expanded. get ()); }), {.maxLines = expanded. get () ? 0 : 1 }); }); }); A key can be anything hashable: an id, a string. Keys must be unique within the list."},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Options","u":"lists.html#options","x":"The options struct sets padding, spacing and direction: LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), {.contentPadding = PaddingValues ( 16 ), .spacing = 8 }, [&]( LazyListScope & list) { list. items ( 50 , []( int i) { Text ( \"Item \" + std:: to_string (i)); }); });"},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Options","h":"state","u":"lists.html#options","x":"A LazyListState to read or control the scroll position."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Options","h":"contentPadding","u":"lists.html#options","x":"Padding around the items, inside the scrolling area: items scroll into it."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Options","h":"spacing","u":"lists.html#options","x":"Space between items."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Options","h":"arrangement","u":"lists.html#options","x":"Instead of spacing : how items are arranged when they all fit ( Arrangement::Bottom , Center …)."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Options","h":"reverseLayout","u":"lists.html#options","x":"Item 0 at the bottom (a LazyRow 's end), as in a chat."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Options","h":"flingBehavior","u":"lists.html#options","x":"How a fling ends: rememberSnapFlingBehavior(state) snaps to an item."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Options","h":"userScrollEnabled","u":"lists.html#options","x":"false : scrolled only from code."},{"p":"Lists and grids ›  Options","h":"crossAxisBias","u":"lists.html#options","x":"Where items narrower than the list sit: start (-1), center (0) or end (1)."},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Animating items","u":"lists.html#animating-items","x":"Modifier().animateItem() on an item's root animates it when it appears (fading in), moves to a new position, or goes away (fading out). It needs keys, so that Kompoz can tell which item moved: LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( LazyListScope & list) { list. items (names, []( const std::string& n) { return n; }, []( const std::string& n) { Text (n, Modifier (). animateItem (). padding ( 16 )); }); });"},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Controlling the scroll position","u":"lists.html#controlling-the-scroll-position","x":"rememberLazyListState() gives a LazyListState to pass in the options. Read where the list is with firstVisibleItemIndex() (observable) and layoutInfo() ; move it with scrollToItem or, in a coroutine, animateScrollToItem : void Messages ( const std::vector<std::string>& messages) { auto list = rememberLazyListState (); auto scope = rememberCoroutineScope (); auto showTop = remember ([list] { return derivedStateOf ([list] { return list. firstVisibleItemIndex () > 3 ; }); }); Box ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&] { LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), {.state = list}, [&]( LazyListScope & l) { l. items (messages, []( const std::string& m) { Text (m, Modifier (). padding ( 16 )); }); }); if (showTop. get ()) { SmallFloatingActionButton ([scope, list] { scope. launch ([list]() -> Task { co_await list. animateScrollToItem ( 0 ); }); }, [] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: KeyboardArrowUp , \"Back to top\" ); }, Modifier (). align ( Alignment :: BottomEnd ). padding ( 16 )); } }); } The list's state is saveable: kept with rememberSaveable , or inside a navigation destination, the position comes back. To load more as the user nears the end, watch the last visible item: auto list = rememberLazyListState (); auto nearEnd = remember ([list, total] { return derivedStateOf ([list, total] { auto visible = list. layoutInfo ().visibleItemsInfo; return !visible. empty () && visible. back ().index >= total - 5 ; }); }); LaunchedEffect (nearEnd. get (), [nearEnd, loadMore]() -> Task { if (nearEnd. get ()) loadMore (); co_return ; });"},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Lazy grids","u":"lists.html#lazy-grids","x":"LazyVerticalGrid and LazyHorizontalGrid lay items out in a grid, composing rows (or columns) as they come into view: LazyVerticalGrid ( GridCells :: Adaptive ( 120 ), Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), {.contentPadding = PaddingValues ( 8 ), .verticalSpacing = 8 , .horizontalSpacing = 8 }, [&]( LazyGridScope & grid) { grid. item ([] { Text ( \"Photos\" , {.font = typography ().titleLarge}); }, LazyGridScope :: FullLine ); grid. items (photos, []( const Photo & p) { return p.id; }, []( const Photo & p) { AsyncImage (p.url, \"\" , Modifier (). aspectRatio ( 1 ), {.contentScale = ContentScale :: Crop }); }); }); GridCells::Fixed(3) gives three columns; GridCells::Adaptive(120) as many columns of at least 120 dp as fit. An item can span several cells: item(content, span) , with LazyGridScope::FullLine for a header across the grid. rememberLazyGridState() works like the list's state. LazyVerticalStaggeredGrid and LazyHorizontalStaggeredGrid place items of different sizes in columns (or rows) of their own, like a masonry layout: each item goes in the shortest column. LazyVerticalStaggeredGrid ( StaggeredGridCells :: Fixed ( 2 ), Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), {.verticalItemSpacing = 8 }, [&]( LazyGridScope & grid) { grid. items (notes, []( const std::string& note) { OutlinedCard ( Modifier (). padding ( 4 ), [&] { Text (note, Modifier (). padding ( 12 )); }); }); });"},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Pagers","u":"lists.html#pagers","x":"HorizontalPager and VerticalPager show one page at a time and snap to pages as the user swipes, like Compose's pagers: void Onboarding () { auto pager = rememberPagerState ( 0 , [] { return 3 ; }); auto scope = rememberCoroutineScope (); Column ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&] { HorizontalPager (pager, Modifier (). weight ( 1 ), []( int page) { Box ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), Alignment :: Center , [&] { Text ( \"Page \" + std:: to_string (page + 1 )); }); }); Button ([scope, pager] { scope. launch ([pager]() -> Task { co_await pager. animateScrollToPage (pager. currentPage () + 1 ); }); }, [] { Text ( \"Next\" ); }); }); } currentPage() , settledPage() and targetPage() are observable. getOffsetDistanceInPages(page) , read while drawing, gives how far a page is from the center, for pages that shrink or fade as they move."},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Carousels","u":"lists.html#carousels","x":"Material 3's carousels show a row of items of changing sizes: HorizontalMultiBrowseCarousel (large items with smaller ones beside them), HorizontalUncontainedCarousel (items of one size) and HorizontalCenteredHeroCarousel : HorizontalMultiBrowseCarousel ( rememberCarouselState ( 0 , [&] { return int (images. size ()); }), 186 , Modifier (). height ( 221 ), {.itemSpacing = 8 }, [&]( int i) { AsyncImage (images[ size_t (i)], \"\" , Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), {.contentScale = ContentScale :: Crop }); });"},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Scrollbars","u":"lists.html#scrollbars","x":"On the desktop, show a scrollbar next to a scrolling container with VerticalScrollbar or HorizontalScrollbar and an adapter for its state: auto scroll = rememberScrollState (); Box ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&] { Column ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (). verticalScroll (scroll), [&] { for ( int i = 0 ; i < 100 ; ++i) Text ( \"Line \" + std:: to_string (i)); }); VerticalScrollbar ( rememberScrollbarAdapter (scroll), Modifier (). align ( Alignment :: CenterEnd ). fillMaxHeight ()); }); rememberScrollbarAdapter takes a ScrollState or a LazyListState . The thumb can be dragged, and clicking the track pages there."},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Nested scrolling","u":"lists.html#nested-scrolling","x":"A scrollable inside another, such as a list under a collapsing app bar, shares scroll deltas with its parents through nested scrolling . Most of the time it just works: a LargeTopAppBar with a scroll behavior collapses as the list under it scrolls: auto behavior = TopAppBarDefaults :: exitUntilCollapsedScrollBehavior (); Scaffold ( Modifier (). nestedScroll (behavior.nestedScrollConnection), {.topBar = [&] { LargeTopAppBar ({.title = [] { Text ( \"Inbox\" ); }, .scrollBehavior = behavior}); }}, [&]( PaddingValues padding) { LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), {.contentPadding = padding}, []( LazyListScope & l) { l. items ( 50 , []( int i) { Text ( \"Message \" + std:: to_string (i), Modifier (). padding ( 16 )); }); }); }); To take part yourself, give Modifier().nestedScroll a connection. Its onPreScroll sees each delta before the child scrolls and returns what it consumed; onPostScroll sees what the child left: void CollapsingHeader () { auto height = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 160.f ); }); auto connection = rememberNestedScrollConnection ({.onPreScroll = [height]( Offset available, NestedScrollSource ) { float next = std:: clamp (height. get () + available.y, 56.f , 160.f ); float used = next - height. get (); height. set (next); return Offset { 0 , used}; }}); Column ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (). nestedScroll (connection), [&] { Box ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (). height (height. get ()). background ( colorScheme ().primaryContainer)); LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), []( LazyListScope & l) { l. items ( 50 , []( int i) { Text ( \"Row \" + std:: to_string (i), Modifier (). padding ( 16 )); }); }); }); } Note: unlike Compose's, onPreFling and onPostFling don't suspend: a connection that animates a fling launches its own coroutine."},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" Pull to refresh","u":"lists.html#pull-to-refresh","x":"PullToRefreshBox adds Material's pull-to-refresh indicator to a scrolling container: void News () { auto items = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf (std::vector<std::string>{ \"First\" }); }); auto refreshing = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); auto scope = rememberCoroutineScope (); PullToRefreshBox (refreshing. get (), [=] { refreshing. set ( true ); scope. launch ([items, refreshing]() -> Task { items. set ( co_await fetchNews ()); refreshing. set ( false ); }); }, Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&] { LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( LazyListScope & l) { l. items (items. get (), []( const std::string& s) { Text (s, Modifier (). padding ( 16 )); }); }); }); }"},{"p":"Lists and grids","h":" See also","u":"lists.html#see-also","x":"Layouts : Column , Row and the other non-lazy layouts. State : derivedStateOf for values computed from the scroll position. Gestures : scrollable , dragging and flinging of your own."},{"p":"Material components","h":"","u":"material.html","x":"Kompoz implements Material 3 as Compose Material 3 1.4 does, Expressive components included: the same components, the same measurements and the same defaults. This page is a tour of the main ones; the API page lists them all with their status. Material components read their colors, type and motion from MaterialTheme , so wrap your app in one. See Theming ."},{"p":"Material components","h":" Scaffold and app bars","u":"material.html#scaffold-and-app-bars","x":"Scaffold lays out a screen: a top bar, a bottom bar, a floating action button and a snackbar host around the content. The content gets the padding that keeps it clear of the bars and the system's insets. void Inbox () { auto snackbars = rememberSnackbarHostState (); Scaffold ( Modifier (), {.topBar = [] { TopAppBar ({.title = [] { Text ( \"Inbox\" ); }, .navigationIcon = [] { IconButton ([] {}, [] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Menu , \"Menu\" ); }); }, .actions = [] { IconButton ([] {}, [] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Search , \"Search\" ); }); }}); }, .floatingActionButton = [snackbars] { FloatingActionButton ([snackbars] { snackbars. showSnackbar ( \"Draft saved\" ); }, [] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Edit , \"Compose\" ); }); }, .snackbarHost = [snackbars] { SnackbarHost (snackbars); }}, []( PaddingValues padding) { LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), {.contentPadding = padding}, []( LazyListScope & l) { l. items ( 30 , []( int i) { ListItem ({.headline = [i] { Text ( \"Message \" + std:: to_string (i)); }}); }); }); }); } Top app bars come in the sizes Material defines: TopAppBar , CenterAlignedTopAppBar , MediumTopAppBar and LargeTopAppBar . With a scroll behavior ( TopAppBarDefaults::enterAlwaysScrollBehavior() , exitUntilCollapsedScrollBehavior() , pinnedScrollBehavior() ) and the behavior's nestedScrollConnection on the Scaffold , they collapse and change color as the content scrolls (see Lists and grids ). BottomAppBar and BottomSheetScaffold exist too."},{"p":"Material components","h":" Navigation","u":"material.html#navigation","x":"NavigationSuiteScaffold is usually the one you want: it shows a bar on a phone and a rail on a tablet or desktop window, as Compose's adaptive navigation suite does. void App () { auto tab = rememberSaveable ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); NavigationSuiteScaffold ([&]( NavigationSuiteScope & nav) { nav. item (tab. get () == 0 , [tab] { tab. set ( 0 ); }, Icons :: Filled :: Home , \"Home\" ); nav. item (tab. get () == 1 , [tab] { tab. set ( 1 ); }, Icons :: Filled :: Search , \"Search\" ); nav. item (tab. get () == 2 , [tab] { tab. set ( 2 ); }, Icons :: Filled :: Settings , \"Settings\" ); }, Modifier (), {}, [&] { Text ( \"Tab \" + std:: to_string (tab. get ())); }); } For moving between screens with a back stack, see Navigation ."},{"p":"Material components ›  Navigation","h":"NavigationBar + NavigationBarItem","u":"material.html#navigation","x":"Three to five destinations at the bottom of a phone screen."},{"p":"Material components ›  Navigation","h":"ShortNavigationBar","u":"material.html#navigation","x":"M3 Expressive's shorter bar."},{"p":"Material components ›  Navigation","h":"NavigationRail , WideNavigationRail","u":"material.html#navigation","x":"Destinations at the side of a wider window."},{"p":"Material components ›  Navigation","h":"ModalNavigationDrawer , DismissibleNavigationDrawer , PermanentNavigationDrawer","u":"material.html#navigation","x":"A drawer of destinations."},{"p":"Material components ›  Navigation","h":"NavigationSuiteScaffold","u":"material.html#navigation","x":"Picks a bar, a rail or a drawer by the window's size."},{"p":"Material components ›  Navigation","h":"PrimaryTabRow , SecondaryTabRow , PrimaryScrollableTabRow + Tab","u":"material.html#navigation","x":"Tabs."},{"p":"Material components","h":" Buttons","u":"material.html#buttons","x":"Row ( Modifier (), {.horizontalArrangement = Arrangement :: spacedBy ( 8 )}, [&] { Button ([] {}, [] { Text ( \"Save\" ); }); OutlinedButton ([] {}, [] { Text ( \"Cancel\" ); }); IconButton ([] {}, [] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Favorite , \"Like\" ); }); });"},{"p":"Material components ›  Buttons","h":"Button","u":"material.html#buttons","x":"Filled: the main action."},{"p":"Material components ›  Buttons","h":"FilledTonalButton","u":"material.html#buttons","x":"Tonal: an important action that isn't the main one."},{"p":"Material components ›  Buttons","h":"ElevatedButton , OutlinedButton","u":"material.html#buttons","x":"Medium emphasis."},{"p":"Material components ›  Buttons","h":"TextButton","u":"material.html#buttons","x":"Low emphasis, in dialogs and cards."},{"p":"Material components ›  Buttons","h":"IconButton , FilledIconButton , OutlinedIconButton , IconToggleButton …","u":"material.html#buttons","x":"An icon on its own."},{"p":"Material components ›  Buttons","h":"FloatingActionButton , SmallFloatingActionButton , LargeFloatingActionButton , ExtendedFloatingActionButton","u":"material.html#buttons","x":"The screen's primary action."},{"p":"Material components ›  Buttons","h":"SingleChoiceSegmentedButtonRow + SegmentedButton","u":"material.html#buttons","x":"A choice among a few options."},{"p":"Material components ›  Buttons","h":"ButtonGroup , SplitButtonLayout , ToggleButton","u":"material.html#buttons","x":"M3 Expressive's groups, split buttons and toggles."},{"p":"Material components","h":" Cards and lists","u":"material.html#cards-and-lists","x":"Card , ElevatedCard and OutlinedCard group related content; each also comes in a clickable form that takes onClick first. ListItem is a one-, two- or three-line row with optional leading and trailing content: OutlinedCard ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), [&] { ListItem ({.headline = [] { Text ( \"Wi-Fi\" ); }, .supporting = [] { Text ( \"Connected\" ); }, .leading = [] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Settings , \"\" ); }, .trailing = [] { Switch ( true , []( bool ) {}); }}); });"},{"p":"Material components","h":" Selection controls","u":"material.html#selection-controls","x":"Checkbox , TriStateCheckbox , RadioButton , Switch , Slider and RangeSlider . They are stateless: they show the value you pass and report changes through a callback. void Volume () { auto volume = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0.5f ); }); auto muted = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); Column ([&] { Slider (volume. get (), [volume]( float v) { volume. set (v); }); Row ( Modifier (). toggleable (muted. get (), [muted]( bool v) { muted. set (v); }, SemanticsRole :: Checkbox ), {.verticalAlignment = Alignment :: CenterVertically }, [&] { Checkbox (muted. get (), {}); // the whole row toggles it Text ( \"Mute\" ); }); }); } Pass an empty callback to a control inside a row that handles the click itself, as above, so that the row is one touch target and one item for accessibility."},{"p":"Material components","h":" Chips","u":"material.html#chips","x":"AssistChip , FilterChip , InputChip and SuggestionChip : FilterChip (selected. get (), [selected] { selected. set (!selected. get ()); }, [] { Text ( \"Unread\" ); });"},{"p":"Material components","h":" Text fields","u":"material.html#text-fields","x":"TextField and OutlinedTextField , with labels, placeholders, icons, prefixes, suffixes, supporting text and errors; SearchBar and DockedSearchBar for search. See Text ."},{"p":"Material components","h":" Dialogs, sheets and menus","u":"material.html#dialogs-sheets-and-menus","x":"These show above the rest of the UI. Each is composed where you call it, so it keeps its state and composition locals, and Kompoz places it on top. Show one by calling it while a state says so: void DeleteButton (std::function< void ()> onDelete) { auto open = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); TextButton ([open] { open. set ( true ); }, [] { Text ( \"Delete\" ); }); if (open. get ()) { AlertDialog ([open] { open. set ( false ); }, {.confirmButton = [open, onDelete] { TextButton ([open, onDelete] { open. set ( false ); onDelete (); }, [] { Text ( \"Delete\" ); }); }, .dismissButton = [open] { TextButton ([open] { open. set ( false ); }, [] { Text ( \"Cancel\" ); }); }, .title = [] { Text ( \"Delete this message?\" ); }, .text = [] { Text ( \"It will be gone for good.\" ); }}); } }"},{"p":"Material components ›  Dialogs, sheets and menus","h":"AlertDialog , BasicAlertDialog , Dialog","u":"material.html#dialogs-sheets-and-menus","x":"Asks for a decision."},{"p":"Material components ›  Dialogs, sheets and menus","h":"ModalBottomSheet","u":"material.html#dialogs-sheets-and-menus","x":"Content that slides up from the bottom, dismissed by dragging it down."},{"p":"Material components ›  Dialogs, sheets and menus","h":"DropdownMenu + DropdownMenuItem","u":"material.html#dialogs-sheets-and-menus","x":"A menu anchored to what opened it."},{"p":"Material components ›  Dialogs, sheets and menus","h":"ExposedDropdownMenuBox","u":"material.html#dialogs-sheets-and-menus","x":"A text field with a menu of choices."},{"p":"Material components ›  Dialogs, sheets and menus","h":"DatePicker , DateRangePicker , TimePicker , TimeInput","u":"material.html#dialogs-sheets-and-menus","x":"Dates and times, alone or in DatePickerDialog / TimePickerDialog ."},{"p":"Material components ›  Dialogs, sheets and menus","h":"TooltipBox + PlainTooltip / RichTooltip","u":"material.html#dialogs-sheets-and-menus","x":"A hint on long press or hover."},{"p":"Material components","h":" Snackbars","u":"material.html#snackbars","x":"A SnackbarHost shows the messages of a SnackbarHostState . Show one from a callback with showSnackbar , or wait for how it went in a coroutine: auto snackbars = rememberSnackbarHostState (); auto scope = rememberCoroutineScope (); Button ([scope, snackbars, undo] { scope. launch ([snackbars, undo]() -> Task { if ( co_await snackbars. showSnackbarAsync ( \"Message deleted\" , \"Undo\" ) == SnackbarResult :: ActionPerformed ) undo (); }); }, [] { Text ( \"Delete\" ); }); SnackbarHost (snackbars);"},{"p":"Material components","h":" Progress","u":"material.html#progress","x":"CircularProgressIndicator and LinearProgressIndicator , with a progress value or without one (indeterminate); M3 Expressive's LoadingIndicator , ContainedLoadingIndicator and the wavy indicators. Indeterminate indicators animate in the draw phase, so they don't recompose anything. CircularProgressIndicator (); // indeterminate LinearProgressIndicator (progress); // 40 %"},{"p":"Material components","h":" Other components","u":"material.html#other-components","x":"Badges ( BadgedBox , Badge ), dividers ( HorizontalDivider , VerticalDivider ), carousels (see Lists and grids ), pull to refresh, swipe to dismiss ( SwipeToDismissBox ), floating toolbars, and more. The API page has the complete list."},{"p":"Material components","h":" See also","u":"material.html#see-also","x":"Theming : colors, typography and dark theme. Navigation : screens and the back stack. Text : text fields in depth."},{"p":"Theming","h":"","u":"theming.html","x":"MaterialTheme gives everything inside it Material 3's color scheme, typography and motion. This page covers color schemes, dark theme, dynamic color from the wallpaper or the desktop's accent color, typography, and building a design system of your own."},{"p":"Theming","h":" MaterialTheme","u":"theming.html#materialtheme","x":"Wrap your app's content in MaterialTheme , usually once at the top: Application kompoz:: createApplication ( int , char **) { return {.title = \"Themed\" , .content = [] { bool dark = isSystemInDarkTheme (); MaterialTheme (dark ? darkColorScheme () : lightColorScheme (), App ); }}; } Components read the theme through composition locals. Read it yourself with colorScheme() , typography() and motionScheme() : void Tag ( const std::string& text) { Text (text, Modifier (). background ( colorScheme ().secondaryContainer, 8 ). padding ( 8 , 4 ), {.font = typography ().labelMedium, .color = colorScheme ().onSecondaryContainer}); } MaterialExpressiveTheme is the same with M3 Expressive's springier motion scheme."},{"p":"Theming","h":" Color schemes","u":"theming.html#color-schemes","x":"A ColorScheme has all of Material 3's color roles, as Compose 1.4's: primary , onPrimary , primaryContainer , secondary , tertiary , error , the surfaces ( surface , surfaceContainerLowest … surfaceContainerHighest , surfaceBright , surfaceDim ), outline , inverseSurface , the fixed colors and more. lightColorScheme() and darkColorScheme() are Material's baseline purple schemes. Each on… color is the color for content on the matching container: onPrimary on primary , onSurface on surface . contentColorFor(background) finds it for you. Material containers ( Surface , Card , buttons) set LocalContentColor to their content color, so Text and Icon inside them get the right color without being told. A scheme of your own is a ColorScheme with its fields set. Start from a baseline and change what you need: ColorScheme brandColors ( bool dark) { ColorScheme cs = dark ? darkColorScheme () : lightColorScheme (); cs.primary = dark ? Color :: hex (0x9ECAFF) : Color :: hex (0x0061A4); cs.onPrimary = dark ? Color :: hex (0x003258) : colors:: White ; return cs; }"},{"p":"Theming","h":" From one color","u":"theming.html#from-one-color","x":"TonalPalette::fromSeed(color) makes the five tonal palettes Material You derives from one color, the way Android 13 does from the wallpaper (its \"tonal spot\" style), in Material's HCT color space. dynamicLightColorScheme(palette) and dynamicDarkColorScheme(palette) turn them into schemes with Material's tone mapping: void BrandTheme ( const Content & content) { static const TonalPalette palette = TonalPalette :: fromSeed ( Color :: hex (0x00796B)); bool dark = isSystemInDarkTheme (); MaterialTheme (dark ? dynamicDarkColorScheme (palette) : dynamicLightColorScheme (palette), content); } This is the easiest way to a complete, consistent scheme from a brand color. Hct::fromColor(c) and Hct{hue, chroma, tone}.toColor() work in the HCT color space directly."},{"p":"Theming","h":" Dark theme","u":"theming.html#dark-theme","x":"isSystemInDarkTheme() follows the system's dark mode, and changes while the app runs: from Android's setting, from the XDG desktop portal on Linux, from the browser on the web. Pick the scheme by it, as above. On Android, tell the system bars whether to draw light or dark icons with SystemBarsAppearance : dark icons over a light theme. void Root () { bool dark = isSystemInDarkTheme (); SystemBarsAppearance (!dark); MaterialTheme (dark ? darkColorScheme () : lightColorScheme (), App ); } To let the user choose, keep the choice in state and fall back to the system's: enum class ThemeChoice { System , Light , Dark }; void Root ( ThemeChoice choice) { bool dark = choice == ThemeChoice :: System ? isSystemInDarkTheme () : choice == ThemeChoice :: Dark ; MaterialTheme (dark ? darkColorScheme () : lightColorScheme (), App ); }"},{"p":"Theming","h":" Dynamic color","u":"theming.html#dynamic-color","x":"On Android 12 and later, the system derives a palette from the wallpaper (\"Material You\"). On Linux, Kompoz reads the desktop's accent color from the XDG desktop portal and turns it into a palette the same way. dynamicLightColorScheme() and dynamicDarkColorScheme() give the schemes, and update when the wallpaper or the accent color changes: void Root () { bool dark = isSystemInDarkTheme (); ColorScheme scheme = isDynamicColorAvailable () ? (dark ? dynamicDarkColorScheme () : dynamicLightColorScheme ()) : (dark ? darkColorScheme () : lightColorScheme ()); MaterialTheme (scheme, App ); } Where there is no system palette (Windows, the web, older Android), isDynamicColorAvailable() is false and the dynamic schemes are the baseline ones. KOMPOZ_NO_DESKTOP_ACCENT=1 ignores the Linux accent color, for screenshots that are the same on every machine."},{"p":"Theming","h":" Animating a theme change","u":"theming.html#animating-a-theme-change","x":"ColorScheme::map applies a function to every color. With animateColorAsState , each color animates to its new value when the scheme changes: ColorScheme animateColorScheme ( const ColorScheme & target, CallSite site = CallSite :: current ()) { ComposableScope scope (site); // a stable identity for the animations inside return target. map ([]( Color c) { return animateColorAsState (c, 400 ); }); } void Root ( bool dark) { MaterialTheme ( animateColorScheme (dark ? darkColorScheme () : lightColorScheme ()), App ); } Each animateColorAsState call inside map runs in the same order every time, so each color keeps its own animation."},{"p":"Theming","h":" Typography","u":"theming.html#typography","x":"Typography holds Material 3's type scale: displayLarge , displayMedium , displaySmall , headlineLarge … headlineSmall , titleLarge … titleSmall , bodyLarge … bodySmall and labelLarge … labelSmall . Each is a Font : a size in sp, a weight, a letter spacing and a line height. Use them through typography() : Text ( \"Settings\" , {.font = typography ().headlineSmall}); Text ( \"Notifications are on\" , {.font = typography ().bodyMedium}); To change the type scale, provide your own Typography through LocalTypography : void BrandTypography ( const Content & content) { static const FontFamily lora = FontFamily :: fromFiles ({{ \"fonts/Lora-Regular.ttf\" }, { \"fonts/Lora-Bold.ttf\" , FontWeight :: Bold }}); Typography type; for ( Font * f : {&type.displayLarge, &type.displayMedium, &type.displaySmall, &type.headlineLarge, &type.headlineMedium, &type.headlineSmall}) f->family = lora.id; CompositionLocalProvider ({ LocalTypography . provides (type)}, content); } Font files are assets (see Resources ). The generic families FontFamily::Serif and FontFamily::Monospace use the system's fonts where there are some. Text sizes follow the system's font scale."},{"p":"Theming","h":" Shapes","u":"theming.html#shapes","x":"Components have Material's shapes built in: a button's full rounding, a card's 12 dp corners, a sheet's 28 dp top corners. There is no theme-wide Shapes object to change them all at once; pass a shape where a component takes one, and use RoundedCornerShape , CutCornerShape , CircleShape or the Material shape library ( MaterialShapes ) for your own."},{"p":"Theming","h":" A design system of your own","u":"theming.html#a-design-system-of-your-own","x":"When your app doesn't follow Material, build your own theme the same way MaterialTheme is built: put the tokens in a struct, provide it through a CompositionLocal , and read it through a small accessor: struct BrandColors { Color accent, background, text; friend bool operator ==( const BrandColors &, const BrandColors &) = default ; }; inline const CompositionLocal < BrandColors > LocalBrandColors { BrandColors { Color :: hex (0xFF5722), colors:: White , Color :: hex (0x212121)}}; inline const BrandColors & brandColors () { return LocalBrandColors . current (); } void BrandTheme ( BrandColors colors, const Content & content) { CompositionLocalProvider ({ LocalBrandColors . provides (colors), LocalContentColor . provides (colors.text)}, content); } void BrandButton ( const std::string& label, std::function< void ()> onClick) { Box ( Modifier (). clickable (std:: move (onClick)). background ( brandColors ().accent, 8 ). padding ( 16 , 10 ), [&] { Text (label, {.color = brandColors ().background}); }); } You can mix both: keep MaterialTheme for Material components and add your own locals for what Material doesn't cover."},{"p":"Theming","h":" See also","u":"theming.html#see-also","x":"CompositionLocal : how themes reach components. Material components : what the theme styles. Text : fonts and text styles."},{"p":"Text","h":"","u":"text.html","x":"Showing text, styling it, letting the user select it, and taking text input. Kompoz shapes text with HarfBuzz, lays out right-to-left scripts, and falls back to the system's fonts for characters the bundled font lacks."},{"p":"Text","h":" Showing text","u":"text.html#showing-text","x":"Text shows a string with the current style ( LocalTextStyle ) in the current content color: Text ( \"Hello, world\" ); Text ( \"A title\" , {.font = typography ().titleLarge}); Text ( \"A note\" , Modifier (). padding ( 8 ), {.color = colorScheme ().onSurfaceVariant}); Its overloads take the text, then optionally a Modifier , then a TextStyle . Text sizes are in sp, scaled by the system's font size."},{"p":"Text","h":" Styling text","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"TextStyle 's fields, in the order you write them: Designated initializers go in this order; skip the ones you don't need. Text (article, Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), {.fontWeight = FontWeight :: Medium , .textAlign = TextAlign :: Justify , .maxLines = 3 , .overflow = TextOverflow :: Ellipsis });"},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"font","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"A whole Font : Material's type styles ( typography().bodyLarge ) are fonts."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"fontSize","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"The size: a number of sp, or TextUnit::em(1.2f) relative to the current size."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"fontWeight","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"FontWeight::Normal , Medium , Bold , or W100 … W900 ."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"color","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"The color; unset, LocalContentColor ."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"textAlign","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"TextAlign::Start , Center , End , Justify ."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"textDecoration","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"TextDecoration::Underline , LineThrough ."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"maxLines , minLines","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"How many lines it may take (0: no limit), and at least."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"fontStyle","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"FontStyle::Italic ."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"fontFamily","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"FontFamily::Serif , Monospace , or one made from files."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"letterSpacing","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"Extra space after each character."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"fontFeatureSettings","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"OpenType features as CSS writes them: \"tnum\" , \"smcp\" ."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"overflow","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"What the last line shows when cut off: TextOverflow::Clip , Ellipsis , StartEllipsis , MiddleEllipsis ."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"softWrap","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"false : one line per paragraph."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"autoSize","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"The font size that fits the space: TextAutoSize::StepBased(12, 48) ."},{"p":"Text ›  Styling text","h":"lineHeight","u":"text.html#styling-text","x":"sp from one baseline to the next."},{"p":"Text","h":" Several styles in one text","u":"text.html#several-styles-in-one-text","x":"An AnnotatedString carries styles over parts of its text, as Compose's does. Build one with buildAnnotatedString : void Terms () { Text ( buildAnnotatedString ([]( AnnotatedString :: Builder & b) { b. append ( \"By continuing you accept the \" ); b. withLink ([] { openTerms (); }, [&] { b. append ( \"terms\" ); }); b. append ( \". \" ); b. withStyle ({.fontWeight = FontWeight :: Bold }, [&] { b. append ( \"No refunds.\" ); }); })); } withStyle(SpanStyle, content) styles what content appends; spans nest, inner ones win. SpanStyle has fontSize , fontWeight , fontStyle , fontFamily , color , textDecoration , background , letterSpacing , brush … withStyle(ParagraphStyle, content) makes a paragraph with its own alignment, indent and line height. withLink(onClick, content) makes what content appends clickable, underlined in the primary color; LinkAnnotation::Url(url) opens a web address. pushStyle / pop , addStyle(style, start, end) and string annotations work as in Compose. AnnotatedString::fromHtml turns simple HTML ( <b> , <i> , <u> , <a href> , <br> , <p> , lists) into an annotated string."},{"p":"Text","h":" Fonts","u":"text.html#fonts","x":"Kompoz bundles Noto Sans in three weights (regular, medium and bold) and uses the system's fonts for everything it lacks: symbols, emoji, CJK, other scripts. FontFamily::Serif and FontFamily::Monospace use the system's where it has them. Your own fonts are asset files: void Quote ( const std::string& text) { static const FontFamily lora = FontFamily :: fromFiles ({{ \"fonts/Lora-Regular.ttf\" }, { \"fonts/Lora-Bold.ttf\" , FontWeight :: Bold }}); Text (text, {.fontSize = 20 , .fontStyle = FontStyle :: Italic , .fontFamily = lora}); } A weight without a file of its own is made from the nearest one (bold ones emboldened); a missing italic is slanted. A variable font can be listed several times with FontVariation settings for each weight. See Resources for where asset files go."},{"p":"Text","h":" Selecting text","u":"text.html#selecting-text","x":"Text isn't selectable by default. Inside a SelectionContainer , it is: one selection across all its texts, with handles on touch screens, a Copy action, mouse dragging and Ctrl+C: SelectionContainer ( Modifier (), [&] { Column ([&] { Text ( \"Order number\" ); Text ( \"KMP-2026-0042\" , {.fontWeight = FontWeight :: Bold }); DisableSelection ([] { Text ( \"(not selectable)\" ); }); }); });"},{"p":"Text","h":" Text fields","u":"text.html#text-fields","x":"TextField (filled) and OutlinedTextField are Material's text fields. As in Compose, their text is either hoisted, with a value and a callback, or kept in a TextFieldState ."},{"p":"Text","h":" With a value and a callback","u":"text.html#with-a-value-and-a-callback","x":"void EmailField () { auto email = rememberSaveable ([] { return mutableStateOf (std:: string ()); }); OutlinedTextField (email. get (), [email](std::string v) { email. set (std:: move (v)); }, Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), {.singleLine = true , .label = \"Email\" , .supportingText = \"We never share it\" , .keyboardOptions = {.keyboardType = KeyboardType :: Email , .imeAction = ImeAction :: Next }}); }"},{"p":"Text","h":" With a TextFieldState","u":"text.html#with-a-textfieldstate","x":"A TextFieldState keeps the text and its selection, and an undo history: void Composer () { auto message = rememberTextFieldState (); Row ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (), {.verticalAlignment = Alignment :: CenterVertically }, [&] { OutlinedTextField (message, Modifier (). weight ( 1 ), {.maxLines = 4 , .placeholder = \"Message\" }); IconButton ([message] { send (message. text ()); message. clearText (); }, [] { Icon ( Icons :: AutoMirrored :: Filled :: Send , \"Send\" ); }); }); } text() and selection() are observable. edit([](TextFieldBuffer& b) { … }) changes both at once; undoState().undo() undoes, and the field also undoes with Ctrl+Z. InputTransformation::maxLength(10) limits what can be typed, and an OutputTransformation changes how the text is shown without changing it."},{"p":"Text","h":" Options","u":"text.html#options","x":"BasicTextField is a field with no decoration, for designs of your own; its decorator option draws around the text. On Android, the soft keyboard shows suggestions and autocorrects, and its type and action key follow keyboardOptions . On desktops, fields have the usual keyboard editing: Ctrl+arrows by word, Home and End, Shift to select, double click for a word."},{"p":"Text ›  Options","h":"singleLine , minLines , maxLines","u":"text.html#options","x":"One line (Enter runs the keyboard action), or how many lines it shows before scrolling."},{"p":"Text ›  Options","h":"label , placeholder","u":"text.html#options","x":"A label that floats above the text, and a hint shown while it is empty."},{"p":"Text ›  Options","h":"prefix , suffix","u":"text.html#options","x":"Text before and after the input, such as a currency or a unit."},{"p":"Text ›  Options","h":"leadingIcon , trailingIcon","u":"text.html#options","x":"Composables at the ends."},{"p":"Text ›  Options","h":"supportingText , isError","u":"text.html#options","x":"A line under the field, and the error colors."},{"p":"Text ›  Options","h":"keyboardOptions","u":"text.html#options","x":"The keyboard's type ( Email , Number , Password …), its action key ( Next , Done , Search …), capitalization and autocorrect."},{"p":"Text ›  Options","h":"keyboardActions , onSubmit","u":"text.html#options","x":"What the action key does."},{"p":"Text ›  Options","h":"visualTransformation","u":"text.html#options","x":"VisualTransformation::Password shows dots."},{"p":"Text ›  Options","h":"enabled , readOnly","u":"text.html#options","x":"Disabled, or focusable and selectable but not editable."},{"p":"Text","h":" Focus","u":"text.html#focus","x":"A FocusRequester moves focus to a field from code, and focusManager() clears it: void Search () { auto query = rememberTextFieldState (); auto focus = remember ([] { return FocusRequester (); }); LaunchedEffect ( 0 , [focus]() -> Task { focus. requestFocus (); // focus the field when the screen opens co_return ; }); OutlinedTextField (query, Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (). focusRequester (focus), {.singleLine = true , .label = \"Search\" }); }"},{"p":"Text","h":" Measuring text","u":"text.html#measuring-text","x":"To draw text on a Canvas , or to know how a text was laid out, use a text measurer: void Gauge ( int percent) { auto measurer = rememberTextMeasurer (); Canvas ( Modifier (). size ( 120 ), [=]( DrawScope & d) { d. drawCircle ( colorScheme ().primary. copy ( 0.2f )); TextLayoutResult label = measurer. measure (std:: to_string (percent) + \"%\" , {.fontSize = 24 }); d. drawText (label, {d. center ().x - label. size ().width / 2 , d. center ().y - label. size ().height / 2 }); }); } Text can also tell you how it was laid out, through an onTextLayout callback: how many lines it took, whether it was cut off. void Excerpt ( const std::string& article) { auto cut = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); Column ([&] { Text (article, Modifier (), {.maxLines = 3 , .overflow = TextOverflow :: Ellipsis }, [cut]( const TextLayoutResult & r) { cut. set (r. hasVisualOverflow ()); }); if (cut. get ()) TextButton ([] {}, [] { Text ( \"Read more\" ); }); }); } Offsets into text are UTF-8 byte offsets everywhere in Kompoz."},{"p":"Text","h":" Right-to-left and mixed text","u":"text.html#right-to-left-and-mixed-text","x":"Text in Arabic, Hebrew or Persian is laid out right to left, and mixed text is ordered by the Unicode bidirectional algorithm. A paragraph's direction comes from its first letter that has one; text without one (digits, punctuation) goes the layout's way."},{"p":"Text","h":" See also","u":"text.html#see-also","x":"Resources : font files and the library's own texts. Theming : the type scale. Accessibility : how text is read out."},{"p":"Graphics","h":"","u":"graphics.html","x":"Drawing your own graphics with Canvas and the drawing modifiers, brushes and paths, clipping and shadows, graphicsLayer , and showing images and vectors."},{"p":"Graphics","h":" Canvas","u":"graphics.html#canvas","x":"Canvas is a composable that draws whatever you tell it in a DrawScope . Give it a size with its modifier: void Target () { Color ring = colorScheme ().primary, center = colorScheme ().error; Canvas ( Modifier (). size ( 160 ), [=]( DrawScope & d) { d. drawCircle (ring. copy ( 0.2f )); // fills the canvas d. drawCircle (ring, d. size ().width / 3 , std::nullopt, Stroke ( 6 )); d. drawCircle (center, 12 ); // at the center }); } The draw lambda runs while drawing, not while composing. Read the colors you need from the theme in composition, as above, and capture them by value."},{"p":"Graphics","h":" What a DrawScope can draw","u":"graphics.html#what-a-drawscope-can-draw","x":"DrawStyle is Fill (the default) or Stroke(width, cap, join) . Coordinates are in dp from the canvas's top-left corner; d.size() and d.center() give its size and center."},{"p":"Graphics ›  What a DrawScope can draw","h":"drawRect , drawRoundRect , drawCircle , drawOval , drawArc","u":"graphics.html#what-a-drawscope-can-draw","x":"Shapes, filled or stroked, with a color or a brush."},{"p":"Graphics ›  What a DrawScope can draw","h":"drawLine(color, start, end, width)","u":"graphics.html#what-a-drawscope-can-draw","x":"A line; with a StrokeCap and a dash PathEffect ."},{"p":"Graphics ›  What a DrawScope can draw","h":"drawPoints(points, mode, color, width)","u":"graphics.html#what-a-drawscope-can-draw","x":"Points, lines between pairs, or a polyline."},{"p":"Graphics ›  What a DrawScope can draw","h":"drawPath(path, color or brush, style)","u":"graphics.html#what-a-drawscope-can-draw","x":"Any path."},{"p":"Graphics ›  What a DrawScope can draw","h":"drawText(layout, topLeft)","u":"graphics.html#what-a-drawscope-can-draw","x":"Text measured with a text measurer (see Text )."},{"p":"Graphics ›  What a DrawScope can draw","h":"drawImage(image, topLeft, size)","u":"graphics.html#what-a-drawscope-can-draw","x":"A bitmap."},{"p":"Graphics ›  What a DrawScope can draw","h":"drawVector(vector, color, topLeft, size)","u":"graphics.html#what-a-drawscope-can-draw","x":"An ImageVector , tinted."},{"p":"Graphics","h":" Transforming","u":"graphics.html#transforming","x":"translate , rotate , scale , inset , clipRect and clipPath take a block: what the block draws is moved, turned, scaled or clipped. withTransform does several at once. Rotating and scaling go through an offscreen layer of the drawing's area. void Clock ( float minutes) { Color hand = colorScheme ().onSurface; Canvas ( Modifier (). size ( 120 ), [=]( DrawScope & d) { d. drawCircle (hand, std::nullopt, std::nullopt, Stroke ( 2 )); d. rotate (minutes * 6 , [&]( DrawScope & r) { // 6° per minute, around the center r. drawLine (hand, r. center (), {r. center ().x, 12 }, 3 , StrokeCap :: Round ); }, d. center ()); }); }"},{"p":"Graphics","h":" Drawing modifiers","u":"graphics.html#drawing-modifiers","x":"You don't need a Canvas to draw on an existing composable. These modifiers draw on its node: drawBehind(onDraw) draws behind the content. drawWithContent(onDraw) draws around it: call drawContent() where the content goes, and draw before or after it. drawWithCache(build) builds objects once per size (paths, brushes) and draws with them every frame. void Highlighted ( const std::string& text) { Color marker = Color :: hex (0xFFEB3B, 0.6f ); Text (text, Modifier (). drawBehind ([marker]( DrawScope & d) { d. drawRect (marker, { 0 , d. size ().height * 0.55f }, Size {d. size ().width, d. size ().height * 0.4f }); })); } void Faded ( const Content & content) { Box ( Modifier (). drawWithContent ([]( ContentDrawScope & d) { d. drawContent (); d. drawRect ( Brush :: verticalGradient ({colors:: Transparent , colors:: White })); // a fade over the bottom }), content); }"},{"p":"Graphics","h":" Brushes","u":"graphics.html#brushes","x":"A Brush paints with a solid color or a gradient, wherever a color would go: drawing calls, background , text. Box ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (). height ( 120 ). background ( Brush :: linearGradient ({ Color :: hex (0x6750A4), Color :: hex (0xB58392)}), RoundedCornerShape ( 16 ))); Brush::solid , linearGradient , horizontalGradient , verticalGradient , radialGradient and sweepGradient are there, with color stops and tile modes."},{"p":"Graphics","h":" Paths","u":"graphics.html#paths","x":"A Path describes any outline with lines, curves and arcs: Path heart ( Size s) { Path p; float w = s.width, h = s.height; p. moveTo (w / 2 , h * 0.9f ); p. cubicTo (w * 0.1f , h * 0.6f , 0 , h * 0.25f , w * 0.25f , h * 0.1f ); p. cubicTo (w * 0.4f , 0 , w / 2 , h * 0.15f , w / 2 , h * 0.25f ); p. cubicTo (w / 2 , h * 0.15f , w * 0.6f , 0 , w * 0.75f , h * 0.1f ); p. cubicTo (w, h * 0.25f , w * 0.9f , h * 0.6f , w / 2 , h * 0.9f ); p. close (); return p; } void Heart () { Color red = Color :: hex (0xE53935); Box ( Modifier (). size ( 64 ). drawWithCache ([red]( CacheDrawScope & cache) { Path path = heart (cache. size ()); // built once per size return cache. onDrawBehind ([path, red]( DrawScope & d) { d. drawPath (path, red); }); })); } moveTo , lineTo , quadraticTo , cubicTo , their relative… forms, arcTo , addRect , addOval , addRoundRect and close build it. PathMeasure measures its length and gives segments of it, for drawing a path progressively."},{"p":"Graphics","h":" Shapes and clipping","u":"graphics.html#shapes-and-clipping","x":"A Shape is the outline used by backgrounds, borders, clips and shadows: RectangleShape , CircleShape , RoundedCornerShape(16) (or one radius per corner), CutCornerShape(8) , and Material 3 Expressive's shapes ( MaterialShapes::Cookie9Sided() and others, with toShape ). Box ( Modifier (). size ( 96 ). clip ( CircleShape ). background ( colorScheme ().tertiaryContainer)); Box ( Modifier (). size ( 96 ). border ( 2 , colorScheme ().outline, RoundedCornerShape ( 12 , 12 , 0 , 0 ))); clip(shape) clips the content to the shape; the content is drawn through a layer so the edge is smooth."},{"p":"Graphics","h":" Shadows","u":"graphics.html#shadows","x":"shadow(elevation, shape) draws a Material-style shadow under a node. dropShadow(shape, Shadow{…}) and innerShadow(shape, Shadow{…}) draw shadows with your own color, offset, blur radius and spread: Box ( Modifier (). size ( 120 ) . dropShadow ( RoundedCornerShape ( 24 ), Shadow {.color = Color :: hex (0x6750A4, 0.4f ), .offset = { 0 , 8 }, .blurRadius = 24 }) . background ( colorScheme ().surface, RoundedCornerShape ( 24 )));"},{"p":"Graphics","h":" graphicsLayer","u":"graphics.html#graphicslayer","x":"graphicsLayer draws the content scaled, rotated, moved or faded, through an offscreen layer, without changing its layout: Box ( Modifier (). size ( 80 ). graphicsLayer ({.scaleX = 1.2f , .scaleY = 1.2f , .alpha = 0.8f , .rotationZ = 15 }) . background ( colorScheme ().primary, RoundedCornerShape ( 16 ))); The form that takes a lambda is evaluated while drawing, so animated values read there change without recomposing or laying out again. Use it for anything that changes every frame (see Phases ): void Spinner () { auto angle = rememberInfiniteTransition (). animateFloatState ( 0 , 360 , infiniteRepeatable ( tween ( 1200 , 0 , LinearEasing ))); Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Refresh , \"Loading\" , Modifier (). graphicsLayer ([angle]( GraphicsLayerScope & l) { l.rotationZ = angle. get (); })); } alpha(a) , scale(s) and rotate(degrees) are shorthands for the common cases, and blur(radius) blurs the content."},{"p":"Graphics","h":" Images","u":"graphics.html#images","x":"Image shows a bitmap; imageResource(path) loads one from the app's assets, once: Image ( imageResource ( \"images/cover.jpg\" ), \"Album cover\" , Modifier (). size ( 160 ). clip ( RoundedCornerShape ( 12 )), ContentScale :: Crop ); AsyncImage loads an image from the assets or from the web ( http:// , https:// ), decodes it at the size it is shown at, and keeps downloads on disk. It shows a placeholder while loading and something else if it fails: AsyncImage ( \"https://example.com/photo.webp\" , \"A photo\" , Modifier (). size ( 200 ), {.placeholder = [] { CircularProgressIndicator (); }, .error = [] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Info , \"Couldn't load\" ); }, .contentScale = ContentScale :: Crop , .crossfadeMillis = 200 }); JPEG, PNG and WebP are supported. ContentScale fits the image into its box as Compose's does: Fit , Crop , FillBounds , FillWidth , FillHeight , Inside , None ."},{"p":"Graphics","h":" Vectors and icons","u":"graphics.html#vectors-and-icons","x":"Icon draws an ImageVector tinted with the content color, at 24 dp by default. Kompoz has Material icons under Icons::Filled and Icons::AutoMirrored::Filled (the latter mirror in right-to-left layouts): Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Favorite , \"Favorite\" , Modifier (), Color :: hex (0xE53935)); Your own vectors come from SVG path data, from Android vector drawable files, or built in code: const ImageVector & sparkle () { static const ImageVector v = ImageVector :: fromSvgPath ( \"M12,2l2.4,7.6L22,12l-7.6,2.4L12,22l-2.4-7.6L2,12l7.6-2.4z\" ); return v; } void Sparkle () { Icon ( sparkle (), \"\" ); Image ( rememberVectorPainter ( vectorResource ( \"vectors/logo.xml\" )), \"Logo\" , Modifier (). size ( 48 )); } vectorResource reads groups, paths, gradients and clip paths from an Android <vector> file, in its own colors when drawn with Image , or tinted with Icon ."},{"p":"Graphics","h":" See also","u":"graphics.html#see-also","x":"Phases : drawing without recomposing. Animation : animating what you draw. Resources : where image and vector files go."},{"p":"Animation","h":"","u":"animation.html","x":"Kompoz has Compose's animation APIs: values that animate to a target, content that animates in and out, transitions of several values together, and low-level Animatable for gestures and coroutines."},{"p":"Animation","h":" Choosing an API","u":"animation.html#choosing-an-api","x":""},{"p":"Animation ›  Choosing an API","h":"Animate one value when its target changes","u":"animation.html#choosing-an-api","x":"animateFloatAsState , animateColorAsState"},{"p":"Animation ›  Choosing an API","h":"Show or hide content with an animation","u":"animation.html#choosing-an-api","x":"AnimatedVisibility"},{"p":"Animation ›  Choosing an API","h":"Switch between pieces of content","u":"animation.html#choosing-an-api","x":"AnimatedContent , Crossfade"},{"p":"Animation ›  Choosing an API","h":"Animate a size change","u":"animation.html#choosing-an-api","x":"Modifier().animateContentSize()"},{"p":"Animation ›  Choosing an API","h":"Animate several values from one state","u":"animation.html#choosing-an-api","x":"updateTransition"},{"p":"Animation ›  Choosing an API","h":"Animate forever (pulses, spinners)","u":"animation.html#choosing-an-api","x":"rememberInfiniteTransition()"},{"p":"Animation ›  Choosing an API","h":"Drive an animation from a coroutine or a gesture","u":"animation.html#choosing-an-api","x":"Animatable"},{"p":"Animation ›  Choosing an API","h":"Move an element from one screen to another","u":"animation.html#choosing-an-api","x":"SharedTransitionLayout and sharedElement"},{"p":"Animation","h":" Animating a value","u":"animation.html#animating-a-value","x":"animate*AsState returns a value that animates to its target whenever the target changes: void LikeButton () { auto liked = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); float scale = animateFloatAsState (liked. get () ? 1.2f : 1.f , spring ( Spring :: DampingRatioMediumBouncy )); Color tint = animateColorAsState (liked. get () ? Color :: hex (0xE53935) : colorScheme ().onSurfaceVariant, 300 ); IconButton ([liked] { liked. set (!liked. get ()); }, [&] { Icon (liked. get () ? Icons :: Filled :: Favorite : Icons :: Filled :: FavoriteBorder , \"Like\" , Modifier (). scale (scale), tint); }); } Only the scope that reads the value recomposes on each frame of the animation, not its parents. For something that changes every frame, read the value while drawing instead, so nothing recomposes (see Phases ). The second argument is an animation spec, or a duration in milliseconds for a tween."},{"p":"Animation","h":" Animation specs","u":"animation.html#animation-specs","x":"Material components use motionScheme() 's springs, so they move the way Material 3 (and M3 Expressive with MaterialExpressiveTheme ) says."},{"p":"Animation ›  Animation specs","h":"spring(dampingRatio, stiffness)","u":"animation.html#animation-specs","x":"Physics-based. The default; it keeps its velocity when the target changes mid-way. Spring::DampingRatioMediumBouncy , Spring::StiffnessLow and the other constants are Compose's."},{"p":"Animation ›  Animation specs","h":"tween(durationMillis, delayMillis, easing)","u":"animation.html#animation-specs","x":"A fixed duration along an easing curve: FastOutSlowInEasing (the default), LinearOutSlowInEasing , FastOutLinearInEasing , LinearEasing ."},{"p":"Animation ›  Animation specs","h":"keyframes(durationMillis, {{atMillis, value}, …})","u":"animation.html#animation-specs","x":"Values at given times."},{"p":"Animation ›  Animation specs","h":"repeatable(iterations, spec, mode) , infiniteRepeatable(spec, mode)","u":"animation.html#animation-specs","x":"A spec repeated, restarting or reversing ( RepeatMode::Reverse )."},{"p":"Animation ›  Animation specs","h":"snap(delayMillis)","u":"animation.html#animation-specs","x":"Jumps to the target."},{"p":"Animation","h":" AnimatedVisibility","u":"animation.html#animatedvisibility","x":"AnimatedVisibility animates its content in when visible becomes true and out when it becomes false. Once hidden, the content leaves the composition. void Details () { auto open = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); Column ([&] { TextButton ([open] { open. set (!open. get ()); }, [&] { Text (open. get () ? \"Less\" : \"More\" ); }); AnimatedVisibility (open. get (), Modifier (), fadeIn () + expandVertically (), fadeOut () + shrinkVertically (), [] { Text ( \"Here are the details.\" , Modifier (). padding ( 16 )); }); }); } Enter transitions are fadeIn , slideInHorizontally , slideInVertically , scaleIn , expandIn , expandHorizontally and expandVertically ; exit transitions are their fadeOut , slideOut… , scaleOut and shrink… counterparts. Combine them with + . EnterTransition::None and ExitTransition::None animate nothing, for when the children animate themselves with animateEnterExit from the content's AnimatedVisibilityScope ."},{"p":"Animation","h":" AnimatedContent and Crossfade","u":"animation.html#animatedcontent-and-crossfade","x":"AnimatedContent animates between contents when its target state changes: the old content leaves while the new one enters, and the size animates between them. void Counter () { auto count = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); Row ( Modifier (), {.verticalAlignment = Alignment :: CenterVertically }, [&] { IconButton ([count] { count. set (count. get () - 1 ); }, [] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Remove , \"Less\" ); }); AnimatedContent < int >(count. get (), []( const int & n) { Text (std:: to_string (n), {.font = typography ().headlineMedium}); }, Modifier (), []( const int & from, const int & to) { // up when counting up, down when counting down return togetherWith ( slideInVertically (to > from ? 1 : - 1 ), slideOutVertically (to > from ? - 1 : 1 )); }); IconButton ([count] { count. set (count. get () + 1 ); }, [] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Add , \"More\" ); }); }); } The content lambda gets the state it shows, which for leaving content is the old one: draw from that argument, not from the state variable. togetherWith(enter, exit) pairs the two transitions; .withSizeTransform(SizeTransform{.clip = false}) changes how the size animates. Crossfade is the simple case: it fades between contents. enum class Page { Home , Settings }; void PageContent ( Page page) { Crossfade < Page >(page, []( const Page & p) { if (p == Page :: Home ) Text ( \"Home\" ); else Text ( \"Settings\" ); }); }"},{"p":"Animation","h":" animateContentSize","u":"animation.html#animatecontentsize","x":"Modifier().animateContentSize() animates a node's size whenever its content makes it grow or shrink: void Expandable ( const std::string& text) { auto expanded = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); Text (text, Modifier (). clickable ([expanded] { expanded. set (!expanded. get ()); }). animateContentSize (), {.maxLines = expanded. get () ? 0 : 2 , .overflow = TextOverflow :: Ellipsis }); } Put it before size modifiers in the chain, so it sees the size change."},{"p":"Animation","h":" updateTransition","u":"animation.html#updatetransition","x":"When several values animate together towards one state, a transition keeps them in step and tells you when they have all arrived: enum class BoxState { Collapsed , Expanded }; void TransitionBox () { auto state = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( BoxState :: Collapsed ); }); auto t = updateTransition (state. get ()); float size = t. animateFloat ([]( BoxState s) { return s == BoxState :: Expanded ? 200.f : 80.f ; }); Color color = t. animateColor ([]( BoxState s) { return s == BoxState :: Expanded ? Color :: hex (0x6750A4) : Color :: hex (0xB58392); }); Box ( Modifier (). size (size). background (color, 16 ). clickable ([state] { state. set (state. get () == BoxState :: Expanded ? BoxState :: Collapsed : BoxState :: Expanded ); })); } t.currentState() catches up with t.targetState() once every value has arrived, and t.isRunning() says whether any is still moving."},{"p":"Animation","h":" Infinite animations","u":"animation.html#infinite-animations","x":"rememberInfiniteTransition() animates values for as long as they are in the composition: void Pulse () { auto pulse = rememberInfiniteTransition (). animateFloatState ( 0.85f , 1.f , infiniteRepeatable ( tween ( 800 ), RepeatMode :: Reverse )); Box ( Modifier (). size ( 64 ) . graphicsLayer ([pulse]( GraphicsLayerScope & l) { l.scaleX = l.scaleY = pulse. get (); }) . background ( colorScheme ().primary, CircleShape )); } animateFloatState and animateColorState return a state to read where it is needed: read while drawing, as here, it is only drawn again each frame. animateFloat and animateColor return the value directly, recomposing the reader each frame."},{"p":"Animation","h":" Animatable","u":"animation.html#animatable","x":"Animatable<T> is a value you animate from coroutines: animateTo suspends until the animation ends, snapTo jumps, stop stops it. A new animateTo takes over from a running one, keeping its velocity, which makes it the tool for animations driven by gestures: void Shake ( bool error) { auto offset = remember ([] { return Animatable < float >( 0.f ); }); LaunchedEffect (error, [offset, error]() -> Task { if (!error) co_return ; for ( float x : {- 12.f , 12.f , - 8.f , 8.f , 0.f }) co_await offset. animateTo (x, tween ( 50 )); }); Text ( \"Wrong password\" , Modifier (). offset ([offset] { return Offset {offset. value (), 0 }; }), {.color = colorScheme ().error}); } animateTo(target, spec, initialVelocity) starts from a fling's velocity, so a released drag springs on naturally. animateDecay(velocity, splineBasedDecay()) slows down from a velocity until it stops, like a fling. updateBounds(lower, upper) keeps it within limits. value() is observable; read it during layout or drawing for smooth motion. See Gestures for combining it with dragging."},{"p":"Animation","h":" Shared elements","u":"animation.html#shared-elements","x":"A shared element moves and resizes from its place in one piece of content to its place in another, as in Compose: void Gallery () { auto details = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); SharedTransitionLayout ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( const SharedTransitionScope & shared) { AnimatedContent < bool >(details. get (), [&, details]( const AnimatedContentScope & scope, const bool & open) { Box ( Modifier () . size (open ? 280 : 72 ) . sharedElement (shared. rememberSharedContentState ( \"photo\" ), scope) . background ( colorScheme ().tertiary, 16 ) . clickable ([details] { details. set (!details. get ()); })); }); }); } Elements with the same key in the leaving and the entering content become one element that animates between them, drawn above everything else. sharedBounds is for elements whose contents differ: both are shown, cross-fading, in the moving bounds. With navigation, shared elements move between screens."},{"p":"Animation","h":" See also","u":"animation.html#see-also","x":"Phases : animating without recomposing. Gestures : animations that follow the finger. Graphics : graphicsLayer for animated transforms."},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":"","u":"gestures.html","x":"Handling taps, clicks, drags, swipes, pinches, the keyboard, focus, hover and drag and drop. Kompoz takes touch, mouse, touchpad and keyboard input on every platform."},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":" Clicks","u":"gestures.html#clicks","x":"Modifier().clickable(onClick) is the simplest gesture: a tap or a click, with Material's ripple, keyboard activation (Enter and Space when focused) and an accessibility action. Text ( \"Open\" , Modifier (). clickable ([] { /* … */ }). padding ( 16 )); combinedClickable(onClick, onLongClick) also takes a long press (and, through its options, a double tap). ClickableOptions turns the click off ( enabled ), shares an interactionSource , or changes the indication : false for none, ripple(false, 24) for an unbounded ripple. Components that act on clicks ( Button , Card , ListItem ) take an onClick of their own; you don't add clickable to them."},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":" Scrolling and dragging","u":"gestures.html#scrolling-and-dragging","x":"For scrolling content, use the scroll modifiers or a lazy list (see Lists and grids ): they handle drags, flings with Android's curve, the overscroll stretch, the mouse wheel and touchpads. Modifier().draggable(state, orientation) reports drags along one axis, as deltas in dp. It doesn't move anything itself: move what you want, typically with an offset read during layout: void Slider1D () { auto x = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0.f ); }); Box ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (). height ( 56 ), [&] { Box ( Modifier () . offset ([x] { return Offset {x. get (), 0 }; }) . draggable ( rememberDraggableState ([x]( float delta) { x. set (std:: max ( 0.f , x. get () + delta)); }), Orientation :: Horizontal ) . size ( 56 ) . background ( colorScheme ().primary, CircleShape )); }); } draggable2D reports drags in both directions. scrollable(state, orientation) is lower level than the scroll modifiers: it reports scroll deltas and flings, and you decide how much of each you consume."},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":" Snapping between positions","u":"gestures.html#snapping-between-positions","x":"anchoredDraggable drags something between named positions and settles at one of them when let go, with a fling's velocity: the building block of bottom sheets, drawers and swipe-to-reveal. enum class DragValue { Start , End }; void Toggle () { auto state = rememberAnchoredDraggableState ( DragValue :: Start ); state. updateAnchors ({{ DragValue :: Start , 0 }, { DragValue :: End , 120 }}); Box ( Modifier (). width ( 176 ). height ( 56 ). background ( colorScheme ().surfaceContainerHigh, CircleShape ), [&] { Box ( Modifier () . offset ([state] { return Offset {state. requireOffset (), 0 }; }) . anchoredDraggable (state, Orientation :: Horizontal ) . size ( 56 ) . background ( colorScheme ().primary, CircleShape )); }); } SwipeToDismissBox builds on it for list items that are swiped away."},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":" Pinch, zoom and rotate","u":"gestures.html#pinch-zoom-and-rotate","x":"transformable turns multi-touch gestures into zoom, pan and rotation changes. Ctrl+wheel and touchpad pinches zoom too: void ZoomableImage () { auto scale = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 1.f ); }); auto state = rememberTransformableState ([scale]( float zoom, Offset , float ) { scale. set (std:: clamp (scale. get () * zoom, 1.f , 5.f )); }); Image ( imageResource ( \"images/map.png\" ), \"Map\" , Modifier (). fillMaxSize () . graphicsLayer ([scale]( GraphicsLayerScope & l) { l.scaleX = l.scaleY = scale. get (); }) . transformable (state)); }"},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":" pointerInput","u":"gestures.html#pointerinput","x":"For gestures of your own, Modifier().pointerInput(key, block) runs a coroutine that receives the node's pointer events. The gesture detectors run inside it: void TapTarget () { auto last = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf (std:: string ( \"Nothing yet\" )); }); Box ( Modifier (). size ( 200 ). background ( colorScheme ().secondaryContainer) . pointerInput ( 0 , [last]( PointerInputScope & s) -> Task { co_await detectTapGestures (s, {.onTap = [last]( Offset ) { last. set ( \"Tap\" ); }, .onDoubleTap = [last]( Offset ) { last. set ( \"Double tap\" ); }, .onLongPress = [last]( Offset ) { last. set ( \"Long press\" ); }}); }), Alignment :: Center , [&] { Text (last. get ()); }); } The block restarts when key changes, so give it a key that captures what it depends on. For lower-level work, co_await s.awaitPointerEvent() gives each event with every pointer's change; consume a change ( change.consume() ) so that scrolling and clicks around it leave it alone. awaitEachGesture and waitForUpOrCancellation help structure a gesture, and VelocityTracker measures how fast a finger moved when it lifted, for a fling. void Drawing () { auto points = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf (std::vector< Offset >{}); }); Color ink = colorScheme ().onSurface; Canvas ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (). pointerInput ( 0 , [points]( PointerInputScope & s) -> Task { co_await detectDragGestures (s, {.onDragStart = [points]( Offset at) { points. update ([&](std::vector< Offset >& v) { v. assign ( 1 , at); }); }, .onDrag = [points]( PointerInputChange & change, Offset ) { points. update ([&](std::vector< Offset >& v) { v. push_back (change.position); }); }}); }), [=]( DrawScope & d) { d. drawPoints (points. get (), PointMode :: Polygon , ink, 3 ); }); }"},{"p":"Gestures and input ›  pointerInput","h":"detectTapGestures(s, {.onPress, .onTap, .onDoubleTap, .onLongPress})","u":"gestures.html#pointerinput","x":"Taps, double taps, long presses."},{"p":"Gestures and input ›  pointerInput","h":"detectDragGestures(s, {.onDragStart, .onDrag, .onDragEnd})","u":"gestures.html#pointerinput","x":"Drags in any direction, after the touch slop."},{"p":"Gestures and input ›  pointerInput","h":"detectHorizontalDragGestures , detectVerticalDragGestures","u":"gestures.html#pointerinput","x":"Drags along one axis."},{"p":"Gestures and input ›  pointerInput","h":"detectDragGesturesAfterLongPress","u":"gestures.html#pointerinput","x":"A drag that starts with a long press, to pick something up."},{"p":"Gestures and input ›  pointerInput","h":"detectTransformGestures(s, onGesture)","u":"gestures.html#pointerinput","x":"Pinch, pan and rotate with several fingers."},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":" Keyboard","u":"gestures.html#keyboard","x":"onKeyEvent handles keys on a focused node; return true when you handled one. onPreviewKeyEvent sees keys before the focused node does. Box ( Modifier () . onKeyEvent ([submit]( const KeyEvent & e) { if (e.type == KeyEventType :: KeyDown && e.key == Key :: Enter && e. isCtrlPressed ()) { submit (); return true ; } return false ; }) . focusable ());"},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":" Focus","u":"gestures.html#focus","x":"Tab and Shift+Tab move keyboard focus between focusable nodes, and the arrow keys move it in a direction. Clickable components and text fields are focusable already; Modifier().focusable() makes any node so. FocusRequester moves focus from code: remember one, attach it with Modifier().focusRequester(r) , call r.requestFocus() . focusManager().clearFocus() takes focus away, which also hides the soft keyboard. onFocusChanged([](FocusState s) { … }) says when a node gains or loses focus. focusProperties({.canFocus = false}) takes a node out of the order; its next and the other directions change where focus goes. focusGroup() and focusRestorer() keep focus moving sensibly through groups, such as a row of chips. Keyboard focus is shown, as in Compose Material 3, by the component's focus state layer."},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":" Hover and the mouse","u":"gestures.html#hover-and-the-mouse","x":"void HoverCard () { auto source = remember ([] { return MutableInteractionSource (); }); bool hovered = source. collectIsHoveredAsState (); Card ( Modifier (). hoverable (source). pointerHoverIcon ( PointerIcon :: Hand ), [&] { Text (hovered ? \"Hovering\" : \"Point at me\" , Modifier (). padding ( 24 )); }); } hoverable(source) reports the mouse coming over a node and leaving. pointerHoverIcon(PointerIcon::Hand) changes the cursor. Text fields and selections show a context menu on a right click."},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":" Interactions","u":"gestures.html#interactions","x":"A MutableInteractionSource is told of the presses, hovers, focus and drags of the components it is given to. Read whether one is going on with collectIsPressedAsState() , collectIsHoveredAsState() , collectIsFocusedAsState() and collectIsDraggedAsState() ; they recompose when it changes. void PressToRecord () { auto source = remember ([] { return MutableInteractionSource (); }); bool pressed = source. collectIsPressedAsState (); Button ([] {}, Modifier (), {.interactionSource = source}, [&] { Text (pressed ? \"Recording…\" : \"Hold to record\" ); }); } interactions() gives the stream of interactions as a flow. Modifier().indication(source, ripple()) draws an indication for interactions that happen somewhere else."},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":" Drag and drop","u":"gestures.html#drag-and-drop","x":"dragAndDropSource starts a drag with data; dragAndDropTarget accepts drops, from inside the app or, on the desktop, files from outside it: void DropZone () { auto dropped = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf (std:: string ( \"Drop text here\" )); }); Column ([&] { Text ( \"Drag me\" , Modifier (). padding ( 16 ). dragAndDropSource ([]( Offset ) { return std:: optional ( DragAndDropTransferData {.text = \"Hello\" }); })); Box ( Modifier (). size ( 200 ). border ( 2 , colorScheme ().outline, 12 ) . dragAndDropTarget ([]( const DragAndDropEvent & e) { return e. hasMimeType ( \"text/plain\" ); }, {.onDrop = [dropped]( const DragAndDropEvent & e) { dropped. set (e. text ()); return true ; }}), Alignment :: Center , [&] { Text (dropped. get ()); }); }); }"},{"p":"Gestures and input","h":" See also","u":"gestures.html#see-also","x":"Lists and grids : scrolling, flinging and nested scrolling. Animation : Animatable for gestures that animate when let go. Accessibility : making custom gestures reachable without them."},{"p":"Architecture","h":"","u":"architecture.html","x":"How to structure an app: unidirectional data flow, state holders, ViewModel s, flows, saved state and the lifecycle. The patterns are the ones Android recommends for Compose apps, in C++."},{"p":"Architecture","h":" Unidirectional data flow","u":"architecture.html#unidirectional-data-flow","x":"State flows down, events flow up: The UI shows state it is given. When the user does something, the UI reports an event (a callback) rather than changing the state itself. Whoever owns the state decides how the event changes it, and the new state flows back down. struct TodoItem { int id; std::string title; bool done; friend bool operator ==( const TodoItem &, const TodoItem &) = default ; }; // Stateless: shows the items, reports what the user did. void TodoList ( const std::vector< TodoItem >& items, std::function< void ( int id, bool done)> onToggle) { LazyColumn ( Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( LazyListScope & list) { list. items (items, []( const TodoItem & t) { return t.id; }, [onToggle]( const TodoItem & t) { ListItem (t.done, [onToggle, id = t.id]( bool done) { onToggle (id, done); }, {.headline = [&] { Text (t.title); }, .leading = [&] { Checkbox (t.done, {}); }}); }); }); } This makes composables easy to reuse, preview and test, and leaves one place where each piece of state changes."},{"p":"Architecture","h":" Where state lives","u":"architecture.html#where-state-lives","x":""},{"p":"Architecture ›  Where state lives","h":"UI element state that nothing else needs (is this menu open?)","u":"architecture.html#where-state-lives","x":"remember in the composable."},{"p":"Architecture ›  Where state lives","h":"State several composables share","u":"architecture.html#where-state-lives","x":"Hoisted to their lowest common ancestor (see State )."},{"p":"Architecture ›  Where state lives","h":"A screen's UI logic (form fields, what is selected)","u":"architecture.html#where-state-lives","x":"A plain state holder class kept with remember ."},{"p":"Architecture ›  Where state lives","h":"Screen state that outlives the UI, and business logic","u":"architecture.html#where-state-lives","x":"A ViewModel ."},{"p":"Architecture ›  Where state lives","h":"Data shared across screens (a repository, a cache)","u":"architecture.html#where-state-lives","x":"Your own objects, passed to the ViewModel s that need them."},{"p":"Architecture","h":" ViewModel","u":"architecture.html#viewmodel","x":"A ViewModel holds a screen's state and does its work, as in Jetpack. It lives in the nearest owner: a navigation entry, cleared when the entry is popped for good, or else the app. So it survives the composables that show it coming and going. class TodoViewModel : public ViewModel { public : MutableStateFlow <std::vector< TodoItem >> items{std::vector< TodoItem >{{ 1 , \"Write docs\" , false }, { 2 , \"Ship\" , false }}}; void toggle ( int id, bool done) { items. update ([&]( const std::vector< TodoItem >& list) { std::vector< TodoItem > next = list; for ( TodoItem & t : next) if (t.id == id) t.done = done; return next; }); } protected : void onCleared () override { /* cancel subscriptions, close files */ } }; void TodoScreen () { auto vm = viewModel< TodoViewModel >(); // the same one while its owner lives std::vector< TodoItem > items = vm->items. collectAsState (); TodoList (items, [vm]( int id, bool done) { vm-> toggle (id, done); }); } viewModel<T>() returns a std::shared_ptr<T> , made the first time and the same one afterwards. Capture it by value in callbacks. viewModelScope() is a CoroutineScope cancelled when the ViewModel is cleared; launch its work there. onCleared() runs when its owner goes away. Screens of a nested navigation graph can share one through the graph's entry: viewModel<T>(*entry.parent()) (see Navigation ). To give a ViewModel dependencies, use the overload that takes a factory: viewModel<T>(key, [&] { return std::make_shared<T>(repository); }) ."},{"p":"Architecture","h":" Flows","u":"architecture.html#flows","x":"A ViewModel exposes its state as a StateFlow , and the UI reads it with collectAsState() , which recomposes when the value changes. MutableStateFlow<T> holds a value. setValue(v) and update(f) can be called from any thread , which makes it the bridge between worker threads and the UI. value() reads the current value; collect(f) follows it in a coroutine. It is conflated, as in Kotlin: setting the value it already has does nothing. Flow<T> is a cold stream, produced by a coroutine for each collector, with Kotlin's operators: map , filter , take , debounce , distinctUntilChanged , flatMapLatest , combine and more. stateIn turns one into a StateFlow kept by a scope: class SearchViewModel : public ViewModel { public : explicit SearchViewModel ( Repository & repo) : results (query. debounce (std::chrono:: milliseconds ( 300 )) . flatMapLatest ([&repo]( const std::string& q) { return repo. search (q); }) . stateIn ( viewModelScope (), std::vector<std::string>{})) {} MutableStateFlow <std::string> query{std:: string ()}; StateFlow <std::vector<std::string>> results; }; MutableSharedFlow<T> is a hot stream for one-off events (show a message, navigate), and tryEmit works from any thread. shareIn and stateIn take SharingStarted::Eagerly , Lazily or WhileSubscribed(5000ms) , which collects the upstream only while someone is collecting. Making a flow by hand: Flow < int > ticks () { return flow< int >([]( FlowCollector < int > emit) -> Task { for ( int i = 0 ;; ++i) { co_await emit (i); co_await delay (std::chrono:: seconds ( 1 )); } }); } Everything runs on the UI thread, in the collecting coroutine. Cancelling the coroutine stops the producer too."},{"p":"Architecture","h":" Saved state","u":"architecture.html#saved-state","x":"Android may end an app's process in the background and bring it back later, expecting it to look as it did. Two tools cover this: rememberSaveable in composables (see State ). SavedStateHandle in ViewModel s: a ViewModel whose constructor takes one gets it, with the values it had, when it is made again. class SearchScreenViewModel : public ViewModel { public : explicit SearchScreenViewModel ( SavedStateHandle state) : state_ (state), query (state.getStateFlow<std::string>( \"query\" , \"\" )) {} void setQuery ( const std::string& q) { state_. set ( \"query\" , q); } StateFlow <std::string> query; private : SavedStateHandle state_; }; viewModel<SearchScreenViewModel>() passes the handle. Values must be saveable types. On the desktop, KOMPOZ_STATE_FILE=file saves and restores across runs, so you can try this without a phone."},{"p":"Architecture","h":" Lifecycle","u":"architecture.html#lifecycle","x":"The app, and each navigation entry, has a Lifecycle that moves through Created , Started and Resumed as it comes to the front and back. Effects can follow it, as Jetpack's do: void Video (std::shared_ptr< Player > player) { LifecycleResumeEffect ( 0 , [player] { player-> play (); return [player] { player-> pause (); }; // when it is no longer resumed }); } LifecycleStartEffect , LifecycleEventEffect(LifecycleEvent::OnStart, …) and currentLifecycleOwner() exist too. collectAsStateWithLifecycle(flow) collects a flow only while the lifecycle is at least started, so in the background it stops counting as a subscriber."},{"p":"Architecture","h":" See also","u":"architecture.html#see-also","x":"State : local state and hoisting. Side effects : coroutines and effects. Navigation : screens, each with its own ViewModel s. Testing : testing stateless composables and ViewModel s."},{"p":"Navigation","h":"","u":"navigation.html","x":"Moving between screens with a back stack, as androidx.navigation.compose does: a NavController keeps the stack, a NavHost shows its top entry and animates between entries."},{"p":"Navigation","h":" A NavHost","u":"navigation.html#a-navhost","x":"void App () { auto nav = rememberNavController (); NavHost (nav, \"home\" , Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( NavGraphBuilder & graph) { graph. composable ( \"home\" , [nav]( const NavBackStackEntry &) { Button ([nav] { nav. navigate ( \"detail/42\" ); }, [] { Text ( \"Open item 42\" ); }); }); graph. composable ( \"detail/{id}\" , [nav]( const NavBackStackEntry & entry) { int id = entry. arguments (). getInt ( \"id\" ). value_or ( 0 ); Column ([&] { Text ( \"Item \" + std:: to_string (id)); TextButton ([nav] { nav. popBackStack (); }, [] { Text ( \"Back\" ); }); }); }); }); } rememberNavController() makes the controller. It is saveable: the back stack comes back after the process was ended. NavHost(nav, startDestination, modifier, builder) declares the destinations and shows the current one. graph.composable(route, content) adds a destination. Its content gets the NavBackStackEntry . nav.navigate(path) pushes a destination; nav.popBackStack() goes back. The system back button and gesture (and Escape on the desktop) pop the stack while there is something to go back to."},{"p":"Navigation","h":" Routes and arguments","u":"navigation.html#routes-and-arguments","x":"A route is a path with {name} parts, and optional query parameters: \"detail/{id}?tab={tab}\" . Navigate with the values filled in ( \"detail/42?tab=2\" ), and read them from the entry's arguments with getString , getInt , getLong , getFloat and getBool , each an std::optional ."},{"p":"Navigation","h":" Type-safe routes","u":"navigation.html#type-safe-routes","x":"As Compose's @Serializable routes, a struct can be a route. Its required fields are path segments, its std::optional fields query parameters: struct Profile { std::string id; std::optional< int > tab; KOMPOZ_ROUTE ( Profile , id, tab) // the route \"Profile/{id}?tab={tab}\" }; struct Home { KOMPOZ_ROUTE ( Home ) }; void TypedApp () { auto nav = rememberNavController (); NavHost (nav, Home {}, Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( NavGraphBuilder & graph) { graph.composable< Home >([nav]( const NavBackStackEntry &) { Button ([nav] { nav. navigate ( Profile {.id = \"ada\" }); }, [] { Text ( \"Ada's profile\" ); }); }); graph.composable< Profile >([]( const NavBackStackEntry & e) { Profile p = e.toRoute< Profile >(); Text ( \"Profile of \" + p.id + \", tab \" + std:: to_string (p.tab. value_or ( 0 ))); }); }); } Fields can be strings, numbers, bools, enums and std::optional s of them. nav.getBackStackEntry<T>() , nav.popBackStack<T>(inclusive) and entry.hasRoute<T>() work with types too."},{"p":"Navigation","h":" Navigation options","u":"navigation.html#navigation-options","x":"navigate takes NavOptions : The usual bottom-navigation pattern, switching between tabs while keeping each tab's stack and scroll position: nav. navigate (tab, {.popUpTo = \"home\" , .saveState = true , .launchSingleTop = true , .restoreState = true }); After a login flow, clear it from the stack: nav. navigate ( \"home\" , {.popUpTo = \"login\" , .inclusive = true });"},{"p":"Navigation ›  Navigation options","h":"popUpTo","u":"navigation.html#navigation-options","x":"Pops the stack down to this route first…"},{"p":"Navigation ›  Navigation options","h":"inclusive","u":"navigation.html#navigation-options","x":"…and that entry too."},{"p":"Navigation ›  Navigation options","h":"launchSingleTop","u":"navigation.html#navigation-options","x":"No second copy if the destination is already on top."},{"p":"Navigation ›  Navigation options","h":"saveState","u":"navigation.html#navigation-options","x":"Keeps the state of what is popped…"},{"p":"Navigation ›  Navigation options","h":"restoreState","u":"navigation.html#navigation-options","x":"…and brings it back when navigating to it again."},{"p":"Navigation","h":" The current destination","u":"navigation.html#the-current-destination","x":"nav.currentBackStackEntryAsState() returns the top entry and recomposes when it changes: use it to highlight the selected item of a navigation bar. void BottomBar ( NavController nav) { auto entry = nav. currentBackStackEntryAsState (); std::string route = entry ? entry-> route () : \"\" ; NavigationBar ( Modifier (), [&] { NavigationBarItem (route == \"home\" , [nav] { nav. navigate ( \"home\" , {.launchSingleTop = true }); }, Icons :: Filled :: Home , \"Home\" ); NavigationBarItem (route == \"search\" , [nav] { nav. navigate ( \"search\" , {.launchSingleTop = true }); }, Icons :: Filled :: Search , \"Search\" ); }); }"},{"p":"Navigation","h":" State on the back stack","u":"navigation.html#state-on-the-back-stack","x":"Each entry keeps its rememberSaveable state, scroll positions included, while it is on the back stack: going back shows the screen as it was. Each entry is also a ViewModel owner: viewModel<T>() inside a destination gives that entry's, cleared when the entry is popped for good. See Architecture ."},{"p":"Navigation","h":" Nested graphs","u":"navigation.html#nested-graphs","x":"Screens that belong together, such as a sign-in flow, go in a nested graph with a route of its own. Navigating to the graph's route opens its start destination: void Graphs ( NavController nav) { NavHost (nav, \"home\" , Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( NavGraphBuilder & graph) { graph. composable ( \"home\" , [nav]( const NavBackStackEntry &) { Button ([nav] { nav. navigate ( \"login\" ); }, [] { Text ( \"Sign in\" ); }); }); graph. navigation ( \"username\" , \"login\" , [&]( NavGraphBuilder & login) { login. composable ( \"username\" , [nav]( const NavBackStackEntry & e) { auto form = viewModel< LoginViewModel >(*e. parent ()); // shared by the graph's screens Button ([nav] { nav. navigate ( \"password\" ); }, [] { Text ( \"Next\" ); }); }); login. composable ( \"password\" , [nav]( const NavBackStackEntry & e) { auto form = viewModel< LoginViewModel >(*e. parent ()); // the same one Button ([nav] { nav. navigate ( \"home\" , {.popUpTo = \"login\" , .inclusive = true }); }, [] { Text ( \"Done\" ); }); }); }); }); } The graph has an entry of its own on the back stack, below its screens, so its ViewModel s live as long as the flow does."},{"p":"Navigation","h":" Deep links","u":"navigation.html#deep-links","x":"A destination can answer URIs. When the app is opened with a link, the NavHost goes to that destination, with the start destination and the screens of the graphs on the way below it, so that back leads up: NavHost (nav, \"home\" , Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( NavGraphBuilder & graph) { graph. composable ( \"home\" , []( const NavBackStackEntry &) { Text ( \"Home\" ); }); graph. composable ( \"item/{n}\" , { navDeepLink ( \"kompoz://item/{n}\" )}, []( const NavBackStackEntry & e) { Text ( \"Item \" + std:: to_string (e. arguments (). getInt ( \"n\" ). value_or ( 0 ))); }); }); Where links come from: Android : a VIEW intent. Add an intent-filter for the scheme to the manifest, and forward links that arrive while the app is open from MainActivity.onNewIntent to KompozInput.onNewIntent , as the showcase does. Desktop : the command line, ./myapp kompoz://item/42 . Web : the address's # part, app.html#item/42 . The current screen is written there too, so reloading opens the same one. From inside the app, nav.navigate(\"kompoz://item/42\") follows a link onto the top of the stack, and nav.handleDeepLink(link) goes there as if the app had been opened with it. Type-safe routes take navDeepLink<T>(\"kompoz://profile\") ."},{"p":"Navigation","h":" Transitions","u":"navigation.html#transitions","x":"NavHostOptions sets the animation between destinations, as an AnimatedContent transform, one for navigating forward and one for going back: NavHost (nav, \"home\" , Modifier (). fillMaxSize (), [&]( NavGraphBuilder & graph) { graph. composable ( \"home\" , []( const NavBackStackEntry &) { Text ( \"Home\" ); }); }, {.transition = togetherWith ( slideInHorizontally ( 1 ), slideOutHorizontally (- 0.3f )), .popTransition = togetherWith ( slideInHorizontally (- 0.3f ), slideOutHorizontally ( 1 ))}); The default is a cross-fade."},{"p":"Navigation","h":" Back handling","u":"navigation.html#back-handling","x":"BackHandler(enabled, onBack) takes the back button, gesture or Escape while enabled ; the most recently composed enabled one wins. Use it for things that should close before the screen does, such as a selection mode: void Selectable () { auto selecting = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); BackHandler (selecting. get (), [selecting] { selecting. set ( false ); }); Text (selecting. get () ? \"Selecting (back to stop)\" : \"Long press to select\" , Modifier (). combinedClickable ([] {}, [selecting] { selecting. set ( true ); })); } PredictiveBackHandler(enabled, onBack) follows Android's predictive back gesture as it happens: its coroutine gets a flow of the gesture's progress, so the UI can move with the finger, and is cancelled if the gesture is called off. Popups, drawers and sheets handle back themselves."},{"p":"Navigation","h":" See also","u":"navigation.html#see-also","x":"Architecture : ViewModel s per destination. Animation : content transforms and shared elements. State : what rememberSaveable keeps on the back stack."},{"p":"Resources","h":"","u":"resources.html","x":"Files your app ships (fonts, images, vectors), the library's own texts and how they follow the system language, and the platform services an app uses: the clipboard, haptics and opening links."},{"p":"Resources","h":" Assets","u":"resources.html#assets","x":"Kompoz has no Android-style resource compiler. An app's files are assets : files kept next to the program and read by a relative path such as \"images/cover.jpg\" . Where they go depends on the platform: Kompoz's own assets, the bundled fonts and the licenses, come from its assets/ folder; kompoz_copy_assets(myapp) copies them next to your desktop binary on every build (see Setup ). Copy your app's own folder the same way: add_custom_command ( TARGET myapp POST_BUILD COMMAND ${ CMAKE_COMMAND } - E copy_directory ${ CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR }/assets $< TARGET_FILE_DIR :myapp>) On Android, add both folders to the Gradle project's asset directories, as the showcase's app/build.gradle.kts does; for the web, add both to the --preload-file link options, as the showcase's CMakeLists.txt does."},{"p":"Resources ›  Assets","h":"Linux, Windows","u":"resources.html#assets","x":"Next to the executable. Copy them there in your CMake build."},{"p":"Resources ›  Assets","h":"Android","u":"resources.html#assets","x":"In the APK's assets/ , from the folders your Gradle project lists."},{"p":"Resources ›  Assets","h":"Web","u":"resources.html#assets","x":"In the page's .data file, packed with Emscripten's --preload-file ."},{"p":"Resources","h":" Images","u":"resources.html#images","x":"imageResource(\"images/cover.jpg\") loads an image asset once and keeps it; painterResource gives the same as a Painter . JPEG, PNG, WebP and BMP are read (on the web, PNG and BMP; use AsyncImage for the others there). Image ( imageResource ( \"images/cover.jpg\" ), \"Cover\" , Modifier (). size ( 120 ), ContentScale :: Crop ); AsyncImage loads assets and web images alike, at the size they are shown, off the UI thread. See Graphics ."},{"p":"Resources","h":" Vectors","u":"resources.html#vectors","x":"vectorResource(\"vectors/logo.xml\") reads an Android vector drawable asset, so vectors exported for Android work as they are: Icon ( vectorResource ( \"vectors/heart.xml\" ), \"Favorite\" ); // tinted Image ( rememberVectorPainter ( vectorResource ( \"vectors/logo.xml\" )), \"Logo\" , Modifier (). size ( 64 )); // in its own colors For a single path, ImageVector::fromSvgPath(\"M12,2…\") takes SVG path data directly."},{"p":"Resources","h":" Fonts","u":"resources.html#fonts","x":"FontFamily::fromFiles makes a family from font assets; see Text . The bundled Noto Sans and the licenses must ship with every app, which kompoz_copy_assets and the Android and web setups above take care of."},{"p":"Resources","h":" The library's own texts","u":"resources.html#the-librarys-own-texts","x":"Some components show or announce texts of their own: a text field's Cut, Copy and Paste, \"More options\" on an overflow button, the date picker's month names, \"Selected\" read out by screen readers. These are in Strings , which LocalStrings provides, like Compose's string resources. Kompoz has them in English ( Strings::english() , the default) and Turkish ( Strings::turkish() ). The host picks them by the system's preferred language: Turkish on a Turkish system, English otherwise. KOMPOZ_LANGUAGE=tr or en decides instead. An app provides its own, whole or changed, with CompositionLocalProvider : void Localized () { Strings mine = Strings :: english (); mine.copy = \"Duplicate\" ; mine.moreOptions = \"More\" ; CompositionLocalProvider ({ LocalStrings . provides (mine)}, App ); } For another language, fill in a Strings of your own, including its datePicker names, and provide it the same way. Strings::forLanguage(\"tr_TR.UTF-8\") returns the Turkish strings for any Turkish locale name and English for the rest. DatePickerOptions::strings and TimePickerOptions ' labels, when set, win over LocalStrings ."},{"p":"Resources","h":" Your app's own texts","u":"resources.html#your-apps-own-texts","x":"Kompoz doesn't translate your app's texts. A simple approach that fits the same model is a struct of your texts per language, provided through a CompositionLocal of your own and picked by LocalStrings.current().language : struct AppTexts { std::string greeting, signIn; friend bool operator ==( const AppTexts &, const AppTexts &) = default ; }; const AppTexts & textsFor ( const std::string& language) { static const AppTexts en{ \"Welcome\" , \"Sign in\" }, tr{ \"Hoş geldiniz\" , \"Giriş yap\" }; return language == \"tr\" ? tr : en; } inline const CompositionLocal < AppTexts > LocalAppTexts { AppTexts {}}; void Root () { CompositionLocalProvider ({ LocalAppTexts . provides ( textsFor ( LocalStrings . current ().language))}, App ); }"},{"p":"Resources","h":" Platform services","u":"resources.html#platform-services","x":"Services that composables get inside a composition and keep for callbacks, as Compose's LocalUriHandler and friends: void ShareRow ( const std::string& link) { UriHandler links = uriHandler (); ClipboardManager clip = clipboardManager (); HapticFeedback haptics = hapticFeedback (); Row ([&] { TextButton ([links, link] { links. openUri (link); }, [] { Text ( \"Open\" ); }); TextButton ([clip, haptics, link] { clip. setText (link); haptics. performHapticFeedback ( HapticFeedbackType :: Confirm ); }, [] { Text ( \"Copy link\" ); }); }); }"},{"p":"Resources ›  Platform services","h":"uriHandler().openUri(uri)","u":"resources.html#platform-services","x":"Opens a link: one of the app's own deep links in the app, http(s) in the browser, others ( mailto: , tel: ) in the app that takes them."},{"p":"Resources ›  Platform services","h":"clipboardManager().setText(text) , getText()","u":"resources.html#platform-services","x":"The system clipboard's text. clipboard() is Compose 1.8's coroutine version."},{"p":"Resources ›  Platform services","h":"hapticFeedback().performHapticFeedback(type)","u":"resources.html#platform-services","x":"A short vibration, as the system does it: Android's, the browser's on the web, nothing on desktops."},{"p":"Resources ›  Platform services","h":"softwareKeyboardController().hide() , show()","u":"resources.html#platform-services","x":"Hides or shows the focused field's soft keyboard."},{"p":"Resources ›  Platform services","h":"focusManager().clearFocus()","u":"resources.html#platform-services","x":"Takes focus away (see Gestures )."},{"p":"Resources","h":" See also","u":"resources.html#see-also","x":"Graphics : images and vectors in depth. Text : fonts and text styles. Setup : building and packaging with the assets."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"","u":"accessibility.html","x":"Making your app usable with screen readers, keyboards and large text. Kompoz builds a semantics tree, as Compose does, that TalkBack reads on Android and tests query everywhere."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" The semantics tree","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-tree","x":"Alongside the UI tree, Kompoz keeps a semantics tree: what each element means to someone who can't see it. Material components fill it in for you: a Button is a button with its label, a Checkbox says whether it is checked, a Slider its value. TalkBack reads this tree on Android; tests find nodes in it. You need to add to it where meaning isn't on the screen as text: icons, images, custom controls."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" Describing images and icons","u":"accessibility.html#describing-images-and-icons","x":"Give every icon and image that carries meaning a content description, and leave decorative ones empty: IconButton ([] {}, [] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Share , \"Share\" ); }); // announced as \"Share, button\" Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Star , \"\" ); // decorative: not announced Image ( imageResource ( \"images/chart.png\" ), \"Sales grew 20% in March\" , Modifier (). size ( 200 )); An icon inside a button that also has a text label shouldn't repeat it: give the icon an empty description."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" The semantics modifier","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-modifier","x":"Modifier().semantics({…}) sets what a node means: Text ( \"Settings\" , Modifier (). semantics ({.heading = true }), {.font = typography ().headlineSmall}); Modifier().semantics(\"description\") is a shorthand for a content description alone."},{"p":"Accessibility ›  The semantics modifier","h":"contentDescription","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-modifier","x":"What it is, when that isn't its text."},{"p":"Accessibility ›  The semantics modifier","h":"role","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-modifier","x":"SemanticsRole::Button , Checkbox , Switch , RadioButton , Tab , Image , Slider ."},{"p":"Accessibility ›  The semantics modifier","h":"checked","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-modifier","x":"On or off, for toggles and selected tabs."},{"p":"Accessibility ›  The semantics modifier","h":"stateDescription","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-modifier","x":"Its state in words: \"Playing\", \"3 of 5\"."},{"p":"Accessibility ›  The semantics modifier","h":"heading","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-modifier","x":"A heading, so screen reader users can jump between sections."},{"p":"Accessibility ›  The semantics modifier","h":"mergeDescendants","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-modifier","x":"Read with everything inside it as one item."},{"p":"Accessibility ›  The semantics modifier","h":"liveRegion","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-modifier","x":"Changes are announced: LiveRegionMode::Polite or Assertive ."},{"p":"Accessibility ›  The semantics modifier","h":"customActions","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-modifier","x":"Actions offered in TalkBack's actions menu."},{"p":"Accessibility ›  The semantics modifier","h":"progressBarRangeInfo , setProgress","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-modifier","x":"A value in a range that services can read and set."},{"p":"Accessibility ›  The semantics modifier","h":"traversalIndex","u":"accessibility.html#the-semantics-modifier","x":"The reading order among siblings, lower first."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" Merging","u":"accessibility.html#merging","x":"A screen reader moves from item to item. A row of an icon and two texts should be one item, read together, not three. Clickables merge their content already; for other groups, set mergeDescendants : Row ( Modifier (). semantics ({.mergeDescendants = true }). padding ( 16 ), [&] { Icon ( Icons :: Filled :: Person , \"\" ); Column ([&] { Text ( \"Ada Lovelace\" ); Text ( \"Online\" ); }); }); // read as \"Ada Lovelace, Online\" Modifier().clearAndSetSemantics({…}) replaces what is inside with what you set: a rating drawn as five stars becomes \"Rating: 4 of 5\". Modifier().hideFromAccessibility() hides a node and what is inside it. Row ( Modifier (). clearAndSetSemantics ({.contentDescription = \"Rating: 4 of 5\" }), [&] { for ( int i = 0 ; i < 5 ; ++i) Icon (i < 4 ? Icons :: Filled :: Star : Icons :: Filled :: FavoriteBorder , \"\" ); });"},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" Custom controls","u":"accessibility.html#custom-controls","x":"A control you draw yourself must say what it is and how to use it. Prefer the modifiers that do this for you: clickable , toggleable(value, onChange, role) , selectable(selected, onClick, role) and triStateToggleable set the role, the state and the action: void StarToggle () { auto starred = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); Icon (starred. get () ? Icons :: Filled :: Star : Icons :: Filled :: FavoriteBorder , \"Starred\" , Modifier (). toggleable (starred. get (), [starred]( bool v) { starred. set (v); }, SemanticsRole :: Switch ). padding ( 12 )); } For a row that acts as one control, put the modifier on the row and pass an empty callback to the control inside it, so the whole row is one touch target and one item: void WifiRow () { auto on = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( true ); }); Row ( Modifier (). fillMaxWidth (). toggleable (on. get (), [on]( bool v) { on. set (v); }, SemanticsRole :: Switch ). padding ( 16 ), {.verticalAlignment = Alignment :: CenterVertically }, [&] { Text ( \"Wi-Fi\" , Modifier (). weight ( 1 )); Switch (on. get (), {}); }); } A group of radio-button rows goes inside Modifier().selectableGroup() , so services know they belong together."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" Actions that need a gesture","u":"accessibility.html#actions-that-need-a-gesture","x":"Swipe-to-delete, long press menus and drag handles are hard to use with a screen reader. Offer the same actions as custom actions: Box ( Modifier (). semantics ({.customActions = {{ \"Archive\" , [archive] { archive (); return true ; }}, { \"Delete\" , [deleteItem] { deleteItem (); return true ; }}}}));"},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" Values in a range","u":"accessibility.html#values-in-a-range","x":"A custom slider reports its value and lets services set it: Box ( Modifier (). semantics ({.contentDescription = \"Brightness\" , .role = SemanticsRole :: Slider , .progressBarRangeInfo = ProgressBarRangeInfo {level. get (), 0 , 1 }, .setProgress = [level]( float v) { level. set (v); return true ; }}));"},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" Announcing changes","u":"accessibility.html#announcing-changes","x":"A message that appears without moving focus, such as \"Saved\" or an error under a field, should be announced. Mark it as a live region: if (saved) Text ( \"Saved\" , Modifier (). semantics ({.liveRegion = LiveRegionMode :: Polite }));"},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" Touch targets","u":"accessibility.html#touch-targets","x":"Material components keep a touch target of at least 48 dp even when they look smaller, as Compose's do ( LocalMinimumInteractiveComponentSize ). For your own small controls, add Modifier().minimumInteractiveComponentSize() ."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" Text size and contrast","u":"accessibility.html#text-size-and-contrast","x":"Text sizes are in sp and follow the system's font size, also when it changes while the app runs. Let layouts grow with it: prefer a minimum height ( heightIn(56) ) over a fixed one for things with text. Material's color schemes give enough contrast between each on… color and its container; keep to those pairs."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" Keyboard","u":"accessibility.html#keyboard","x":"Every clickable and text field can be reached with Tab and used with Enter or Space, and the arrow keys move between focusable items. Keep custom controls focusable ( clickable and toggleable are) and make sure focus order follows reading order. See Gestures ."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" Platforms","u":"accessibility.html#platforms","x":"On Android, TalkBack reads the semantics tree: labels, roles, states, actions, custom actions and live regions. The desktop platforms' accessibility APIs (AT-SPI on Linux, UI Automation on Windows) are not connected yet, so screen readers there don't see Kompoz apps."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":" See also","u":"accessibility.html#see-also","x":"Testing : finding nodes by their semantics. Material components : what they announce on their own. Gestures : focus and keyboard input."},{"p":"Testing","h":"","u":"testing.html","x":"UI tests that run without a window, as Compose's createComposeRule() : compose a composable, find nodes, act on them, and check the result, with a virtual clock that makes animations and coroutines deterministic."},{"p":"Testing","h":" A first test","u":"testing.html#a-first-test","x":"ComposeTestRule composes, lays out and draws its content headlessly, with a text measurer of fixed sizes. Find nodes by text, content description or test tag, act on them, and assert: #include <kompoz/testing.hpp> using namespace kompoz::testing; void Counter () { auto count = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( 0 ); }); Column ([&] { Text ( \"Count: \" + std:: to_string (count. get ())); Button ([count] { count. set (count. get () + 1 ); }, [] { Text ( \"Add\" ); }); }); } void counterIncrements () { ComposeTestRule rule ([] { Counter (); }); rule. onNodeWithText ( \"Count: 0\" ). assertIsDisplayed (); rule. onNodeWithText ( \"Add\" ). performClick (); rule. onNodeWithText ( \"Count: 1\" ). assertIsDisplayed (); } A failed assertion throws kompoz::testing::AssertionError , saying what it looked for and what it found. Use any test framework, or none: the rule works in a plain function."},{"p":"Testing","h":" Setting up a test target","u":"testing.html#setting-up-a-test-target","x":"Tests need only the core library, no window system: add_executable (myapp_tests tests/main.cpp src/screens.cpp) target_link_libraries (myapp_tests PRIVATE kompoz_core) add_test ( NAME myapp_tests COMMAND myapp_tests) Keep your composables in source files the app and the tests share. Build Kompoz with -DKOMPOZ_BUILD_SDL3=OFF on a build server that has no display or SDL."},{"p":"Testing","h":" Finding nodes","u":"testing.html#finding-nodes","x":"As in Compose, finders use merged semantics: a text inside a clickable answers for that clickable, so onNodeWithText(\"Add\").performClick() clicks the button and assertIsEnabled() checks it. A test tag is the most robust way to find something whose text changes or is translated: void Search () { auto query = rememberTextFieldState (); OutlinedTextField (query, Modifier (). testTag ( \"search\" ), {.label = \"Search\" }); } void typingFillsTheField () { ComposeTestRule rule ([] { Search (); }); rule. onNodeWithTag ( \"search\" ). performTextInput ( \"kompoz\" ); rule. onNodeWithText ( \"kompoz\" ). assertExists (); }"},{"p":"Testing ›  Finding nodes","h":"onNodeWithText(text)","u":"testing.html#finding-nodes","x":"A node whose text is text ; onNodeWithText(text, true) for a substring."},{"p":"Testing ›  Finding nodes","h":"onNodeWithContentDescription(d)","u":"testing.html#finding-nodes","x":"A node with that content description: icons, images."},{"p":"Testing ›  Finding nodes","h":"onNodeWithTag(tag)","u":"testing.html#finding-nodes","x":"A node with Modifier().testTag(tag) ."},{"p":"Testing ›  Finding nodes","h":"onAllNodesWithText(text) , onAllNodesWithTag(tag)","u":"testing.html#finding-nodes","x":"All of them, as a collection: assertCountEquals(n) , [i] , size() ."},{"p":"Testing","h":" Actions","u":"testing.html#actions","x":"Each action waits for the UI to settle afterwards."},{"p":"Testing ›  Actions","h":"performClick()","u":"testing.html#actions","x":"Taps the node's center."},{"p":"Testing ›  Actions","h":"performTextInput(text)","u":"testing.html#actions","x":"Focuses the node and types."},{"p":"Testing ›  Actions","h":"performKey(Key::Enter)","u":"testing.html#actions","x":"Presses a key."},{"p":"Testing ›  Actions","h":"performScrollTo()","u":"testing.html#actions","x":"Scrolls the scrollables around the node to show it."},{"p":"Testing ›  Actions","h":"rule.swipe(from, to, frames)","u":"testing.html#actions","x":"A drag across the window, a frame per step: fast enough to fling."},{"p":"Testing","h":" Assertions","u":"testing.html#assertions","x":"assertExists , assertDoesNotExist , assertIsDisplayed (in the window, not clipped away), assertIsNotDisplayed , assertTextEquals , assertTextContains , assertContentDescriptionEquals , assertHasClickAction , assertIsEnabled , assertIsNotEnabled , assertIsSelected (a selected tab, a ticked checkbox, a switch on), assertIsOn , assertIsOff . They return the node, so they chain: rule. onNodeWithText ( \"Save\" ). assertIsDisplayed (). assertIsEnabled (). performClick (); For anything else, a node gives its bounds() (in dp, in the window) and text() , and exists() . rule.drawnTexts() lists the texts that were actually drawn, for checking what reached the screen."},{"p":"Testing","h":" Time","u":"testing.html#time","x":"The rule's clock is virtual: time moves only when the test says so, so animations, delays and coroutines give the same result on every run. rule.waitForIdle() runs frames until nothing is pending: recomposition, layout, and timers that are due within a couple of seconds. rule.advanceTimeBy(ms) moves the clock forward, running the frames in between. rule.advanceFrame() runs one frame. void Toast ( bool show) { auto visible = remember ([] { return mutableStateOf ( false ); }); LaunchedEffect (show, [show, visible]() -> Task { if (!show) co_return ; visible. set ( true ); co_await delay (std::chrono:: seconds ( 2 )); visible. set ( false ); }); if (visible. get ()) Text ( \"Saved\" ); } void toastGoesAwayAfterTwoSeconds () { ComposeTestRule rule ([] { Toast ( true ); }); rule. onNodeWithText ( \"Saved\" ). assertExists (); rule. advanceTimeBy ( 2100 ); rule. onNodeWithText ( \"Saved\" ). assertDoesNotExist (); } Work given to withBackground runs on real threads; waitForIdle waits for it to finish."},{"p":"Testing","h":" Changing what the test shows","u":"testing.html#changing-what-the-test-shows","x":"The content lambda can read state that the test owns, so the test can change it between steps: void showsTheNewName () { auto name = mutableStateOf (std:: string ( \"Ada\" )); ComposeTestRule rule ([name] { Text ( \"Hello, \" + name. get ()); }); rule. onNodeWithText ( \"Hello, Ada\" ). assertExists (); name. set ( \"Grace\" ); rule. waitForIdle (); rule. onNodeWithText ( \"Hello, Grace\" ). assertExists (); } rule.host() gives the host, to change what the platform would: setSystemDarkTheme(true) , setLayoutDirection(LayoutDirection::Rtl) , setStrings(Strings::turkish()) . The second argument of the rule's constructor is the window's size in dp, {400, 800} by default."},{"p":"Testing","h":" What to test","u":"testing.html#what-to-test","x":"Stateless composables are the easiest: pass data in, check what is shown, check that events call back. Hoisting state (see State ) makes most of your UI testable this way. ViewModels and other logic are plain C++ objects: test them without a rule at all. Screens together with their ViewModel s: the rule composes them as the app would."},{"p":"Testing","h":" Screenshots","u":"testing.html#screenshots","x":"For checking what a screen looks like, run the real app headless with a script and compare the pictures it saves; see Tools ."},{"p":"Testing","h":" See also","u":"testing.html#see-also","x":"Accessibility : the semantics finders use. Architecture : structuring code so it is easy to test. Tools : scripted runs of the real app."},{"p":"Tools","h":"","u":"tools.html","x":"Previews, rebuilding on save, scripted screenshots, profiling and the environment variables that help while you work."},{"p":"Tools","h":" Previews","u":"tools.html#previews","x":"As Compose's @Preview , a preview shows one composable on its own, without the rest of the app, in a frame of the size you choose. Write it next to the composable, in your app's sources: KOMPOZ_PREVIEW ( CounterCardPreview , .width = 400 , .height = 220 ) { MaterialTheme ( lightColorScheme (), [] { CounterCard (); }); } KOMPOZ_PREVIEW ( CounterCardDarkPreview , .width = 400 , .height = 220 , .darkTheme = true ) { MaterialTheme ( darkColorScheme (), [] { CounterCard (); }); } Start the app with KOMPOZ_PREVIEW set to a preview's name and its window shows only that preview, at that size: KOMPOZ_PREVIEW=CounterCardPreview ./build/myapp KOMPOZ_PREVIEW='*' , or a name no preview has, shows all of them one under another, each with its name and size. The options after the name are a PreviewSpec : The text scale is the window's ( KOMPOZ_FONT_SCALE ), not a preview's own. A preview registers itself when the program starts, so it must be in a source file that is linked into the app."},{"p":"Tools ›  Previews","h":".width , .height","u":"tools.html#previews","x":"The frame's size in dp (360 × 640 by default)."},{"p":"Tools ›  Previews","h":".darkTheme","u":"tools.html#previews","x":"What isSystemInDarkTheme() returns inside the preview; unset, the system's."},{"p":"Tools ›  Previews","h":".background","u":"tools.html#previews","x":"A color behind the preview; unset, none."},{"p":"Tools","h":" Rebuild and restart on save","u":"tools.html#rebuild-and-restart-on-save","x":"scripts/dev.sh dev.sh builds the showcase, starts it, and rebuilds and restarts it whenever a source file changes. The app comes back where it was: the screen it was on, scroll positions and rememberSaveable values are written when it closes and read when it starts, through KOMPOZ_STATE_FILE , the way Android brings an app back after ending its process. A build that fails leaves the running app alone. Closing the window or pressing Ctrl+C ends it. Its arguments are the target, the build directory and what the app is started with: scripts/dev.sh showcase build kompoz://components Together with a preview, it gives you a component on its own that comes back a few seconds after each save: KOMPOZ_PREVIEW=CounterCardPreview scripts/dev.sh dev.sh watches Kompoz's own folders ( include , src , platform , examples ). For an app of your own, copy it and change the folders it watches and the target it builds."},{"p":"Tools","h":" Scripted runs and screenshots","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"KOMPOZ_SCRIPT drives the app without a user: clicks, typing, swipes and screenshots, with coordinates in dp. While the script runs, time is virtual, so animations are the same on every run; when it ends, real time takes over and you can go on using the app from where it left off. SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen KOMPOZ_SCRIPT= \"click 160 263; wait 30; shot out.png; quit\" ./build/showcase SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen runs without a window, which is useful on a build server. A preview becomes a picture the same way: SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen KOMPOZ_PREVIEW=CounterCardPreview KOMPOZ_SCRIPT= \"wait 10; shot preview.png; quit\" ./build/myapp The commands, separated by ; : For tests that check the UI without any window at all, see Testing ."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"click x y , rclick x y","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"A tap, or a right click."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"move x y","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"Moves the mouse (hover)."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"down x y , up x y","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"Presses and releases, for holding down."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"drag x1 y1 x2 y2 , mdrag x1 y1 x2 y2","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"A drag with a finger, or with the mouse."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"swipe x1 y1 x2 y2 frames","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"A drag over that many frames, fast enough to fling."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"fdown id x y , fmove id x y , fup id x y","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"Fingers of a multi-touch gesture."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"wheel x y dy , zoom x y factor","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"The mouse wheel, and Ctrl+wheel or a touchpad pinch."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"type text","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"Types text into the focused field."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"key name","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"enter , backspace , escape , tab , shift+tab , space , up , down , left , right ."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"resize w h","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"Resizes the window."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"wait frames","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"Lets frames pass."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"shot file.png","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"Saves a screenshot."},{"p":"Tools ›  Scripted runs and screenshots","h":"quit","u":"tools.html#scripted-runs-and-screenshots","x":"Ends the app."},{"p":"Tools","h":" Seeing recomposition","u":"tools.html#seeing-recomposition","x":"Modifier().recomposeHighlighter() draws a border around a node showing how many times the composable that made it has run, as the Compose snippets' highlighter does: use it to find what recomposes more often than it should. void Row1 ( const std::string& title) { Text (title, Modifier (). recomposeHighlighter ()); } KOMPOZ_TRACE=1 logs, for each recomposition, how many scopes ran and how many were skipped."},{"p":"Tools","h":" Profiling frames","u":"tools.html#profiling-frames","x":"KOMPOZ_PROFILE=1 logs frame times: for each slow frame, the time spent on input, composition and layout, drawing and presenting, with the offscreen layers it drew and the glyphs it rasterized, and a summary every second. Android has no environment variables, so there the profiler looks for a file in the app's storage, and its output goes to adb logcat -s SDL/APP : adb shell run-as dev.kompoz.showcase touch files/kompoz-profile Kompoz records each frame first and draws again only what changed since the frame before; a frame in which nothing on the screen changes is neither drawn nor presented. To see or check this: KOMPOZ_DAMAGE_SHOW=1 outlines what was drawn again in each frame. KOMPOZ_DAMAGE_CHECK=1 also draws each frame from scratch and logs any pixel that differs. KOMPOZ_FULL_REDRAW=1 draws every frame from scratch."},{"p":"Tools","h":" Environment variables","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":""},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_PREVIEW=name","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Shows one preview, or all of them with * ."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_SCRIPT=\"…\"","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Drives the app with the commands above."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_STATE_FILE=file","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Writes the saved state ( rememberSaveable , the navigation back stack) at exit and reads it at start."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_FONT_SCALE=1.3","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Stands in for the system's font scale on the desktop."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_SCALE=2.625","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Stands in for a screen's density, to see a phone's layout on the desktop."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_LANGUAGE=tr","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"The language of the library's own texts, instead of the system's ( en or tr )."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_LAYOUT_DIRECTION=rtl","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Right-to-left or left-to-right layout, instead of the system language's."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_NO_DESKTOP_ACCENT=1","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Ignores the Linux desktop's accent color, so screenshots are the same on every machine."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_TRACE=1","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Logs how many scopes ran and were skipped in each recomposition."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_PROFILE=1","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Logs frame times."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_DAMAGE_SHOW=1 , KOMPOZ_DAMAGE_CHECK=1 , KOMPOZ_FULL_REDRAW=1","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Show, check or turn off redrawing only what changed."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_LAYOUT_CHECK=1","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Checks every incremental layout against a full one (the tests always do)."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_IMAGE_CACHE=dir","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Where AsyncImage keeps the images it downloaded."},{"p":"Tools ›  Environment variables","h":"KOMPOZ_IMAGE_LOG=1","u":"tools.html#environment-variables","x":"Logs where each image came from and how big it was."},{"p":"Tools","h":" Packaging","u":"tools.html#packaging","x":"scripts/package.sh packages the showcase for every platform whose toolchain it finds; see Setup ."},{"p":"Tools","h":" See also","u":"tools.html#see-also","x":"Testing : UI tests that run without a window. Phases : what composition, layout and drawing cost, and how to keep each cheap."},{"p":"Components and priorities","h":"","u":"api.html","x":"Source: the 7bdac078 revision of the android/snippets repository. Across 583 Kotlin files, for every androidx.compose.* symbol we counted how many files import it (the numbers in parentheses). How often an API appears in the official samples is a good proxy for how much real apps need it. Status: ✅ done · 🟡 partly / in another form · ⬜ missing"},{"p":"Components and priorities","h":" Runtime","u":"api.html#runtime","x":""},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"@Composable (276)","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ composable(site, args..., [=]{...}) : restartable and skippable. ComposableScope : inline (like Column)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"remember (135), mutableStateOf (93)","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"rememberSaveable , rememberSaveableStateHolder / SaveableStateProvider","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ inside a SaveableStateHolder the value is kept while its content is not shown (nav entries, saved tabs, items scrolled out of LazyColumn/LazyRow, by their key); rememberScrollState , rememberLazyListState , rememberPagerState , rememberNavController , rememberTextFieldState are saveable. Saveable types ( Saver<T> : numbers, bool, enum, strings, vectors/optionals of these, MutableState , scroll/list/pager states, TextFieldState , NavController , SaveableStateHolder ) are written to bytes by ComposeHost::saveState() : on Android they go into the Activity's Bundle when the app moves to the back"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"getValue / setValue (113/95)","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"🟡 Kotlin by delegation; in C++ .get() / .set()"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"rememberCoroutineScope (33), LaunchedEffect (26), value-returning suspend functions, withContext(Dispatchers.IO/Default)","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ C++20 coroutines: -> Task , co_await delay(...) , cancellation = destroying the frame; TaskOf<T> : a coroutine that returns a value (Kotlin's value-returning suspend fun ) — co_return value; , and from another coroutine T x = co_await f(); (runs as part of it and is cancelled with it; an exception it throws is caught in the awaiting coroutine; can be nested); co_await withBackground([] { return work(); }) (Compose's withContext(Dispatchers.IO/Default) ): the work runs on one of several worker threads, and the coroutine resumes on the UI thread with the returned value (or the thrown exception"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"mutableIntStateOf / FloatStateOf / LongStateOf (20/8/2), mutableDoubleStateOf","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ aliases of mutableStateOf (the value is already stored unboxed)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"CompositionLocalProvider (15), compositionLocalOf (2), staticCompositionLocalOf , providesDefault , CompositionLocalContext","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ A single type, CompositionLocal<T> (identified by its address, never copied; define it globally): CompositionLocal<T>{defaultValue} or compositionLocalOf<T>([] { … }) / staticCompositionLocalOf(…) — the default is computed once, the first time it is read without a provider, and may throw (a local that must be provided: []() -> User { throw … } ); both recompose only the scopes that read it (the static one too). providesDefault(value) : provides the value only if nothing above provides one (a library default the app can override). currentCompositionLocalContext() captures all locals provided "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"ProvideTextStyle","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ ProvideTextStyle({.fontSize = 20, .fontWeight = FontWeight::Bold, .color = …}, content) : the text style of its content is merged with the current one (font, size, weight, style, family into LocalTextStyle ; color into LocalContentColor )"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"LocalLayoutDirection ( LayoutDirection.Rtl ), Modifier.absoluteOffset , absolutePadding","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ CompositionLocalProvider({LocalLayoutDirection.provides(LayoutDirection::Rtl)}, …) : text with no direction of its own (only digits, punctuation) is laid out in the layout direction ( TextDirection::Content , as in Compose; Text , TextField , rememberTextMeasurer ), Row starts from the right, Start / End (alignment, Arrangement , padding, offset ) are mirrored, horizontal scroll/LazyRow/Pager start from the right and follow the finger in the opposite direction (including fling, page snapping, bringing focus into view); the drawer slides in from the right, and SwipeToDismissBox directions are"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"DisposableEffect (10), SideEffect (5), key (1)","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"mutableStateListOf (9)","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ SnapshotStateList<T>"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"mutableStateMapOf ( SnapshotStateMap )","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ mutableStateMapOf<K, V>({{k, v}, …}) : reading subscribes, changing recomposes the readers; entries in key order ( std::map ); get(k) ( std::optional ), getOrDefault , containsKey , put (returns the previous value; putting the same value recomposes no one), putAll , remove (a missing key recomposes no one), clear , update"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"derivedStateOf (8), rememberUpdatedState (5), produceState (2)","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ derivedStateOf readers recompose only when the value changes"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"snapshotFlow (5)","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ co_await snapshotFlow([&] { ... }).collect(...) : notifies when the states it reads change and the result differs (without requesting a frame)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"viewModel() , ViewModel ( onCleared , viewModelScope )","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ the ViewModel of the nearest owner: a navigation entry (cleared once it is fully popped off the stack and gone from the screen; lives on in a tab saved with saveState ) or the app; by type and an optional key. viewModelScope is cancelled on clear. viewModel<T>(*entry.parent()) : the screens of a nested graph share the graph's ViewModel. SavedStateHandle : passed to a ViewModel whose constructor takes one; the saveable values in it ( get / set / remove / keys , getStateFlow observable) are written with the app's saved state (a navigation entry's with the NavController), and after the process "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"Lifecycle , LifecycleOwner , LocalLifecycleOwner , LifecycleEventEffect , LifecycleStartEffect , LifecycleResumeEffect , collectAsStateWithLifecycle","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ states ( Initialized → Created → Started → Resumed , and down to Destroyed ) are passed through step by step, each step an event ( OnCreate … OnDestroy ); addObserver brings a late observer up to the current state with events, and the observer is removed when the returned handle goes away; currentStateAsState() is observable, currentStateFlow() . The app's owner ( ComposeHost::lifecycleOwner() ) follows the window: on Android, going to the background gives Started (state saved at that moment goes into the Bundle) then Created , and Resumed on return; on desktop/web, Started on focus loss, Cr"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"collectAsState (6), StateFlow / MutableStateFlow","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ flow.collectAsState() returns the value and recomposes the caller when it changes; setValue / update (atomic)/ compareAndSet from any thread, collectors on the UI thread; conflated"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"Cold Flow ( flow { emit } , flowOf , emptyFlow ) and its operators","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ the producer is a coroutine; co_await emit(v) resumes once the collector has taken the value (backpressure); map , filter , onEach , transform , take (stops the producer without an exception), distinctUntilChanged , debounce , flatMapLatest (a new value cancels the previous one), combine (two flows or StateFlows), stateIn(scope, initialValue) (Kotlin's SharingStarted.Eagerly ), launchIn , collectAsState(initial) ; on StateFlow, asFlow() and the same operators. When the collecting coroutine is cancelled, the producer's and the operators' own coroutines go too. MutableSharedFlow<T>(replay) ( t"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"Modifier.blur(radius) / blur(radiusX, radiusY, edgeTreatment) , BlurredEdgeTreatment","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ the content is blurred in its own layer and composited ( GraphicsContext::pushBlurLayer ); in SDL the texture is halved (each step a 2×2 average with linear filtering) as many times as the radius's power-of-two exponent, then scaled back up step by step — cheap on the GPU, close to Gaussian but not exact; Rectangle stays within the bounds (beyond the edge counts as the edge pixels), Unbounded spills into the surroundings (three times the radius); 0: no layer. Note: no blur with the software renderer"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"movableContentOf ( MovableContent )","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ remember([] { return movableContentOf([] { … }); }) , then card() : when it is called somewhere else in the same composition (a Column instead of a Row, the other pane), its remembered values, nodes and running effects (LaunchedEffect keeps running, DisposableEffect is not disposed) move there; the new place may be composed before or after the old one. If it is called in two places in the same composition, the second one is a new instance; if it is not called at all, it is forgotten once at the end of composition. Parameterized forms: movableContentOf<P1, P2…>([](const P1&, const P2&…) {...}"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"Channel ( channel.hpp ), BufferOverflow , produce , consumeEach , receiveAsFlow / consumeAsFlow","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ Kotlin's Channel : each element is received once. Capacity Rendezvous (default, the sender waits for a receiver), a number, Buffered (64), Conflated (only the latest), Unlimited ; when full, BufferOverflow::Suspend (waits) / DropOldest / DropLatest . co_await send(v) → true if received or buffered, false if closed (or cancelled while waiting); trySend does not wait and works from any thread. co_await receive() → the next element, or nullopt once closed and drained (Kotlin's receiveCatching ; nothing throws); tryReceive . close() ends sending, remaining elements can still be received; cancel("},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Runtime","h":"withFrameNanos (1)","u":"api.html#runtime","x":"✅ co_await awaitFrame()"},{"p":"Components and priorities","h":" Layout ( foundation.layout )","u":"api.html#layout-foundationlayout","x":""},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Layout ( foundation.layout )","h":"padding (120), fillMaxSize / Width / Height (117/67/6), size (51), width / height (18/39)","u":"api.html#layout-foundationlayout","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Layout ( foundation.layout )","h":"Column (108), Box (92), Row (50), Arrangement (66), Spacer (36)","u":"api.html#layout-foundationlayout","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Layout ( foundation.layout )","h":"PaddingValues (16), offset (16), heightIn / widthIn (4/3), safeDrawingPadding (3)","u":"api.html#layout-foundationlayout","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Layout ( foundation.layout )","h":"aspectRatio (14), wrapContentSize / Width / Height (13), requiredSize / Width / Height (5), requiredSizeIn / WidthIn / HeightIn","u":"api.html#layout-foundationlayout","x":"✅ required*In are ranges that ignore the incoming constraints: an unset bound (max kInfinity ) keeps the incoming one as far as the set bound allows; content that overflows the incoming constraints is centered"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Layout ( foundation.layout )","h":"sizeIn , defaultMinSize , offset { }","u":"api.html#layout-foundationlayout","x":"✅ offset { } with a lambda (read during layout); defaultMinSize applies only when the incoming minimum is 0"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Layout ( foundation.layout )","h":"WindowInsets (7), windowInsetsPadding (4), imePadding (3), systemBars , navigationBarsPadding , consumeWindowInsets","u":"api.html#layout-foundationlayout","x":"✅ types (status/navigation bar, ime, cutout, system gestures, captionBar — a freeform window's title bar, included in systemBars —, mandatorySystemGestures , tappableElement , waterfall , safeGestures = the gesture edges of all of them, safeDrawing/safeContent; captionBarPadding , waterfallPadding , systemGesturesPadding , mandatorySystemGesturesPadding , safeGesturesPadding ; Android 11+), preferredFrameRate(hz) / preferredFrameRate(FrameRateCategory::Normal|High|NoPreference) : while placed, requests a display refresh rate (the fastest request wins; on Android 11+ Surface.setFrameRate on the"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Layout ( foundation.layout )","h":"BoxWithConstraints (6)","u":"api.html#layout-foundationlayout","x":"✅ the layout constraints are written to state, and the content recomposes in the same frame"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Layout ( foundation.layout )","h":"IntrinsicSize (4), FlowRow / FlowColumn (1)","u":"api.html#layout-foundationlayout","x":"✅ width(IntrinsicSize::Min) : texts are as wide as their widest word (breaking at every space, never splitting a word, styled spans at their own sizes; maxLines is not taken into account), a Row is the sum of these, a Column the widest — like Compose's min intrinsic; Max : the single-line width; for height Min = Max (the natural height at the given width)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Layout ( foundation.layout )","h":"AlignmentLine ( FirstBaseline , LastBaseline ), Modifier.alignByBaseline / alignBy , paddingFrom / paddingFromBaseline","u":"api.html#layout-foundationlayout","x":"✅ texts and text fields report their baselines (on a single line, the text's baseline; on multiple lines, those of the first and last visible lines), and parent layouts inherit their children's (the topmost for the first, the bottommost for the last; padding included); inside a Row , alignByBaseline() puts texts of different sizes and button labels on the same line (the row grows if needed); paddingFromBaseline(top, bottom) puts the first baseline that far from the top and the last one that far from the bottom; in your own Layout , placeable[FirstBaseline] / node->alignmentLine(...)"},{"p":"Components and priorities","h":" Foundation","u":"api.html#foundation","x":""},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"background (57), clickable (36), border (14), verticalScroll (15), rememberScrollState (17), BorderStroke , shadow(…, clip, ambientColor, spotColor)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ border(BorderStroke(width, color or brush), shape) : a brush (gradient) border, inside the shape; shadow(elevation, shape, clip, ambientColor, spotColor) : the shadow is a blend of the two colors, faint or strong in proportion to their alphas, and clip clips the content to the shape (as in Compose; unlike Compose, the two-parameter shadow does not clip). ScrollState : value() and maxValue() are observable (as in Compose: a composable that reads them recomposes as it scrolls; with derivedStateOf only what is needed), canScrollForward/Backward() , isScrollInProgress() (observable: while the us"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Overscroll (Android 12+ stretch)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ when verticalScroll / horizontalScroll , lazy lists and pagers are pulled past their end with a finger, the content stretches from the pulled edge (with increasing resistance); when the finger comes back, the stretch is undone first, and on release it springs back; a fling that hits the end does a short stretch. Mouse and wheel do not stretch; a bare scrollable() and the part a parent takes in nested scrolling (e.g. PullToRefresh) do not stretch. LocalOverscrollFactory (Compose 1.8): scroll containers (verticalScroll/horizontalScroll, lazy lists and grids, pagers) created inside CompositionL"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"isSystemInDarkTheme (15)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Image (34) + ContentScale (28), ImageBitmap , Painter , Modifier.paint","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ imageResource : JPEG, PNG, WebP, BMP (synchronously only PNG/BMP on the web), pixels from code. Painter (as in Compose): BitmapPainter , painterResource(\"images/x.png\") , VectorPainter / rememberVectorPainter (black when there is no color filter; in rememberVectorPainter(defaultWidth, defaultHeight, viewportWidth, viewportHeight, [&](ImageVector::Builder& v) {...}) the content is composable: it rebuilds the vector when a state/animation it reads changes, and keeps the same vector if the result is the same), ColorPainter , BrushPainter , Painter::custom([](DrawScope&) {...}, intrinsicSize) ; "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"ColorFilter ( tint , colorMatrix , lighting ), ColorMatrix ( setToSaturation , setToScale , timesAssign ), BlendMode","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ Image(…, colorFilter) , AsyncImageOptions::colorFilter , DrawScope::drawImage(…, colorFilter) : the filtered copy is made once on the CPU per image and filter, and kept as long as the image lives (last 32); tint blend modes are Src, SrcOver, SrcIn (default: the shape in that color), SrcAtop, DstIn, Modulate, Multiply, Screen, Darken, Lighten, Plus; Icon already takes a tint"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"AsyncImage (Coil), rememberAsyncImagePainter","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ AsyncImage(model, contentDescription, modifier, {placeholder, error, contentScale, crossfadeMillis}) : an asset/file path or an http(s) URL, loaded and decoded in the background (on Linux desktop if libjpeg-turbo, libwebp, libcurl are available; on Android BitmapFactory and HttpURLConnection; on the web the browser and fetch ; on Windows WIC and WinINet), one load per model, a 64 MB memory cache (least recently used goes first), crossfade, onState ; the image is decoded at the size layout first gives it (like Coil: a large image in a small box is downscaled by powers of 2, never below the bo"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"LazyColumn (45), items (21), LazyRow (5), rememberLazyListState (7)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ key, contentPadding, spacing, fling; rememberLazyListState(index) opens at an item; reverseLayout (first item at the bottom, like chats); animateScrollToItem (for a distant item it first jumps close, then slides with a spring; corrects the target as items get measured), animateScrollBy , stopScroll ; ScrollState::animateScrollTo/By (stopped by a drag or a new animation); LazyListState::layoutInfo() (observable, after each layout but only when it changes): visibleItemsInfo (index, key hash, offset from the start of the content — after padding —, size, contentType; the pinned header first), vi"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"RoundedCornerShape (32), CircleShape (17), clip (26)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ rounded clip via an offscreen layer; RoundedCornerShapePercent(50) (Compose's RoundedCornerShape(percent) : a percentage of the short side), lerp(shape, shape, t) blends the corners (morphing corners; corner by corner for shapes with per-corner sizes); shadow(elevation, shape) . Shapes with per-corner sizes: RoundedCornerShape(topStart, topEnd, bottomEnd, bottomStart) (dp) or with CornerSize ( {8} dp, CornerPercent(50) fraction) — in RTL, start is on the right; as in Compose, if the two corners of a side don't fit on the short side, both shrink together. Background, border, clipping, shadow,"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Canvas (10), drawBehind (11), drawWithContent (5)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ DrawScope : rect/roundRect/circle/line/arc (pie slice)/text/image/vector, translate (a drawContent() inside it moves too)/inset/clipRect/rotate/scale; PathEffect ( Stroke(…, pathEffect) ): dashPathEffect(intervals, phase) (each contour starts the pattern from the beginning), cornerPathEffect(r) , stampedPathEffect(shape, advance, phase, StampedPathEffectStyle::Translate / Rotate / Morph) (the shape is stamped once every advance dp — translated, turned to the path's direction, or bent along the path; the result is filled, not stroked; phase works as in Skia: p puts the first stamp at advance "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Brush ( solid , linearGradient , horizontalGradient , verticalGradient , radialGradient , sweepGradient ), TileMode","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ color stops or evenly spaced colors; the ends default to the area's edges ( kInfinity ); TileMode::Clamp (end color), Repeated (repeats from the start), Mirror (back and forth), Decal (transparent outside) — in linear and radial gradients; in SDL a tiled gradient is drawn from a 256-texel texture using the mesh's UVs (repeats are sharp), and on clipped paths with vertex colors"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"rememberGraphicsLayer , GraphicsLayer ( record , drawLayer , toImageBitmap )","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ as in Compose 1.7: d.record(layer, [&](DrawScope&) { d.drawContent(); }) records the content (or everything drawn) into the layer instead of drawing it here (from the scope's top-left, at its own size or the scope's); d.drawLayer(layer) draws the recording as many times as needed, at its position in properties() , with alpha, scale, rotation and translation (around the pivot) — a reflection, a shadowed copy; toImageBitmap() turns the recording into pixels at the screen density (the platform draws offscreen: in SDL a target texture + readback; premultiplied colors are corrected); this functio"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"drawWithCache (5), Path (5), arcTo / addArc , DrawScope.drawOval / clipPath / withTransform","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ Path: fill with FreeType (NonZero/EvenOdd) and stroke with FT_Stroker (cap/join), mask cache; arcTo(oval, startAngle°, sweepAngle°, forceMoveTo) (0° on the right, clockwise; cubic segments of at most a quarter turn; joined to the previous point with a line, standalone with forceMoveTo or when it is the first command), addArc (a separate contour; 360° is a full oval), relativeQuadraticTo / relativeCubicTo , addPath(path, offset) , translate . DrawScope : drawOval(color or brush, topLeft, size, style) ; clipPath(path, block, ClipOp::Intersect/Difference) : any path, with a soft edge — the bloc"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"FocusRequester (12), onFocusChanged (3), LocalFocusManager (4) ( clearFocus , moveFocus ), Modifier.focusProperties , Modifier.focusGroup","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ navigation with Tab/Shift+Tab and IME Next/Previous; focusProperties({.canFocus = false}) (does not take focus via Tab, touch or requestFocus ), {.next = r, .down = r2, .right = FocusRequester::cancel()} (movement in that direction goes to that requester's node — or the first focusable inside it —, and stays put with cancel() ; if the target cannot take focus, the usual path applies); as in Compose, these also come from nodes above a focus target (a field's modifier). focusGroup() : arrow keys first look for the nearest candidate in the innermost group that contains focus, then outward group"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"BringIntoViewRequester , Modifier.bringIntoViewRequester","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ co_await requester.bringIntoView() (or a part of the node with bringIntoView(Rect{...}) ): the surrounding scrollables, from inner to outer, reveal it with the least scrolling; animated with the scrollables' spring (medium-low stiffness); outer ones see the target where the inner one will move it (if the inner box suffices, the page does not move)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"BringIntoViewSpec , LocalBringIntoViewSpec","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ how a scroll container brings its content into view (Compose's): calculateScrollDistance(offset, size, containerSize) returns how far to scroll (the focused item, the field above the keyboard, BringIntoViewRequester ); verticalScroll / horizontalScroll , scrollable , lazy lists and grids created inside CompositionLocalProvider({LocalBringIntoViewSpec.provides(...)}, …) pick it up (e.g. keeping the focused card at one third, like a TV row). In RTL the distance is measured from the right. Default: just enough to be visible; if it is larger than the viewport, its start"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Custom indication ( IndicationNodeFactory , IndicationNode )","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ your own indication, as in Compose: the factory creates one node per component; the node receives press (with position), release, cancel, hover and focus interactions through onInteraction , draws around the content with draw(ContentDrawScope&) , and animates inside coroutineScope() (its jobs end when the component goes away). Indication(factory) : clickable(…, {.indication = …}) , LocalIndication (Material buttons too) or Modifier.indication(interactionSource, …) ; on a clickable without a given source, the node listens to its own source. The same factory keeps the same node across recompos"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"TextToolbar , LocalTextToolbar","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ the text selection toolbar is replaced by your own (Compose's): when LocalTextToolbar is provided, Kompoz does not draw its own popup toolbar but calls showMenu(rect, onCopyRequested, onPasteRequested, onCutRequested, onSelectAllRequested) (unavailable actions are empty: no paste/cut in a read-only field, only copy and select all in SelectionContainer ), and calls it again when the position or the actions change; hide() when the selection ends. Show your own toolbar in a popup so that pressing it does not end the selection"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"LocalUriHandler , LocalSoftwareKeyboardController , LocalHapticFeedback , LocalClipboardManager","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ uriHandler().openUri(uri) (the app's own URLs — deep links of a NavHost in the composition, e.g. kompoz://item/7 — open inside the app; others via SDL_OpenURL: the browser, an intent on Android, xdg-open, ShellExecute), softwareKeyboardController().hide()/show() (the field stays focused, and the keyboard comes back on tap; with a hardware keyboard there is nothing to hide), hapticFeedback().performHapticFeedback(HapticFeedbackType::…) (on Android the view's performHapticFeedback , with the closest constant on older versions; navigator.vibrate on the web; none on desktop), clipboardManager() "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"focusable (10) (non-text focus)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ clickables can also be focused with the keyboard; Tab/Shift+Tab, directional focus with the arrow keys, click with Enter/Space, a focus ring, scrolling the focused node into view"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Ripple: ripple() , Indication , LocalIndication , clickable(indication = …)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ a Material ripple on every clickable: it spreads from the pressed point, starting at 0.3 of the larger side and reaching the farthest corner, with its center drifting to the middle, over 225 ms (FastOutSlowIn); it fades in over 75 ms and fades out over 150 ms after release (once it has finished spreading); clipped to the shape; requests no frames while held. ripple(bounded, radius, color) : an unbounded one starts from the center, at the given radius, unclipped (the hover/focus layer and the focus ring are then also a circle of that radius); Indication::Kind::StateLayer is a flat layer, fals"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"MutableInteractionSource , Interaction ( PressInteraction , HoverInteraction , FocusInteraction , DragInteraction ), collectIsPressedAsState / collectIsHoveredAsState / collectIsFocusedAsState / collectIsDraggedAsState , Modifier.hoverable , clickable(interactionSource, indication = null)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ clickable(onClick, {.interactionSource, .indication = false, .onLongClick, .onDoubleClick}) ; the style of the Button / IconButton family and TextFieldOptions take an interactionSource ; focusable(enabled, source) , hoverable(source) , draggable(…, source) , scrollable(…, source) ; ScrollState / LazyListState / PagerState::interactionSource() report the user's drags. Press: Release on release (before the click), Cancel when the finger slides away or scrolling starts; a keyboard click is Press + Release ; hover only with the mouse (touch ends the mouse's hover), on all nested hoverable s; if "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"pointerInput (10), detectTapGestures / detectDragGestures (4/4), combinedClickable (3), PointerEventPass , detectDragGesturesAfterLongPress","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ coroutine-based awaitPointerEvent , consume rules, horizontal/vertical drag. Each event travels in three passes (as in Compose): Initial from outer to inner (the parent sees or consumes it before its children), Main from inner to outer (the default), Final again from outer to inner (after everyone: has someone else consumed it?); awaitPointerEvent(PointerEventPass::Initial) waits for that pass, and the same event can reach a handler several times, pass by pass; a press consumed in Initial is not received by clickables either. Note: the clickables' and scrollables' own handling runs during Ma"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Multi-touch: detectTransformGestures (2), Modifier.transformable , calculateZoom / Rotation / Pan / Centroid","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ an ID per finger, PointerEvent::changes ; one-finger panning can be left to the page with canPan ; if a second finger lands while the page has only just started scrolling with the first (250 ms / 48 dp), the gesture switches to zooming; transformable also zooms with Ctrl+wheel on a mouse and with touchpad pinch (SDL pinch on Wayland/macOS; it arrives as Ctrl+wheel on Windows and the web) ( ComposeHost::zoom ); touchscreens on desktop"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"horizontalScroll (2), LazyVerticalGrid (5) ( GridCells.Fixed/Adaptive , full-row span), HorizontalPager (3) / VerticalPager ( PagerState , page turning by fling, animateScrollToPage ), PageSize , PagerSnapDistance , PagerDefaults.flingBehavior","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ Pager: pageSize ( PageSize::Fill — the whole area without padding, PageSize::Fixed(dp) , or PageSize{[](float availableSpace, float pageSpacing) {…}} ), snapPosition ( SnapPosition::Start / Center / End : where the current page settles; pages do not scroll past their ends — the first page's start rests at the start padding, the last page's end at the end padding; use contentPadding to bring the first and last pages to the center), all visible pages (plus one more and beyondViewportPageCount more) are in the composition; pages take as much space as they want (along the pager up to the page si"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"LazyGridState ( rememberLazyGridState , firstVisibleItemIndex , scrollToItem / animateScrollToItem , layoutInfo )","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ LazyGridOptions::state is now a LazyGridState : the position is tracked by item (the first item of the first visible row), scrolling to an item goes to its row (once the grid is laid out, if it isn't yet), layoutInfo().visibleItemsInfo gives each item's row/column, position and its own size (in a horizontal grid, rows are columns), maxSpan ; it is saved. The underlying row list is lines()"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"LazyStaggeredGridState ( rememberLazyStaggeredGridState , firstVisibleItemIndex , scrollToItem / animateScrollToItem , layoutInfo )","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ LazyStaggeredGridOptions::state is now a LazyStaggeredGridState : the first visible item is the smallest index among the visible ones (observable), scrollToItem puts the item's top at the start of the content and keeps it there until the heights of its neighbors are measured (positions of unmeasured items are estimated from the average; another scroll releases it), layoutInfo().visibleItemsInfo gives the lane ( lane , fullLine if it spans the full line), the scrolled position and the size, plus lanes ; dp scrolling via scrollState()"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"HorizontalMultiBrowseCarousel , HorizontalUncontainedCarousel , rememberCarouselState (M3 Carousel)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ multi-browse: large items that fit the window (close to the preferred width), one medium, one small; as items scroll, they grow and shrink between keylines (always filling the window), the content stays at the large size and is masked from the center ( itemShape , 28 dp corners by default), it snaps to an item on release, and at the end of the list the arrangement flips (so the last item also shows large); uncontained: fixed width, free scrolling; itemSpacing , contentPadding , currentItem , scrollToItem / animateScrollToItem , saveable. HorizontalCenteredHeroCarousel(state, maxItemWidth, …)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"nestedScroll (4) ( NestedScrollConnection , NestedScrollDispatcher , NestedScrollSource )","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ pre/post scroll and fling chain; nested lists, a row inside a pager (the remaining velocity of a row flung past its end turns the page and does not go to the pager's parents; in Compose 1.11's source, the page connection swallows this velocity before the pager), a list in a bottom sheet; a scrollable element acts before the outer drag handlers"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Modifier.anchoredDraggable , AnchoredDraggableState , DraggableAnchors","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ values (any == type, usually an enum) and dp positions: DraggableAnchors<T>{{Start, 0}, {End, 300}} / .at(v, p) , positionOf , closestAnchor(p[, searchUpwards]) , min/maxPosition ; state: currentValue / targetValue / settledValue (observable; the target triggers recomposition only when it changes), offset() (NaN until the anchors arrive; requireOffset() is then 0), progress(from, to) , isAnimationRunning , lastVelocity , updateAnchors(newAnchors[, newTarget]) (from onSizeChanged; at rest it keeps its value and moves to that value's new position, a running animation retargets to the new posit"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"animateItem (5), SwipeToDismissBox (4), draggable (1), draggable2D , scrollable2D","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ Modifier.scrollable2D(rememberScrollable2DState([](Offset d) { ...; return consumed; })) (Compose 1.9): scrolling in any direction after the touch slop; on release, a fling on each axis separately (with the scrollers' deceleration) — an axis stops when it reaches its end —, a press stops the fling, mouse wheel (the horizontal wheel too; at the end it hands off to the surrounding page); isScrollInProgress , dispatchRawDelta , stopScroll ; no overscroll. Modifier.draggable2D(rememberDraggable2DState([](Offset d) {...}), {.interactionSource, .startDragImmediately, .onDragStarted, .onDragStopped"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"PullToRefreshBox ( PullToRefreshState , PullToRefreshDefaults.Indicator )","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ a Box with Modifier.pullToRefresh ; releasing past the threshold calls onRefresh , with a spinning indicator while refreshing. Options: state , threshold , enabled , contentAlignment , indicator . The box no longer clips its content (the indicator clips itself, as in Compose)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Modifier.pullToRefresh , PullToRefreshDefaults.IndicatorBox","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ as in Compose 1.4.0: pullToRefresh(isRefreshing, state, onRefresh, enabled, threshold) on any box; it moves half the finger's distance and slows down more and more past the threshold (at most twice the threshold); pulls are ignored while the state ( distanceFraction : 0 hidden, 1 at the threshold) is animating, and while refreshing, scrolls pass to the list; on the first draw the state is set to match isRefreshing (without animation). PullToRefreshState.snapTo . IndicatorBox(state, isRefreshing, modifier, content, maxDistance, shape, containerColor, elevation) : a circle that emerges from be"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"stickyHeader ( LazyColumn / LazyRow )","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ the section header stays at the top, and the next header pushes it out; a distant header is composed too"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"LazyHorizontalGrid","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ rows split the height, columns are lazy like LazyRow ; FullLine is a whole column"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"LazyVerticalStaggeredGrid , LazyHorizontalStaggeredGrid , StaggeredGridCells","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ items at their own height go into the shortest column (horizontally: at their own width, after the shortest row; horizontalItemSpacing , verticalSpacing ; the state's positions, scroll and layoutInfo are along x); the visible area plus a half-screen margin is composed; FullLine header; state: LazyStaggeredGridState (its scrolling through scrollState() , in dp)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"scrollable (3), rememberScrollableState , scrollableArea","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ drag/fling deltas in the finger's direction; the unused part goes to the outer scrollable; reverseDirection . scrollableArea(state, orientation[, overscrollEffect], enabled, reverseScrolling) (Compose 1.9's): for layouts that place their content from the state themselves: scrollable + clipping along the axis (like the scroll containers, with a 30 dp margin across) + overscroll drawn on top (stretch or the given effect; nullptr : none); with enabled false it scrolls only from code, and the clipping stays"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"KeyboardOptions / KeyboardActions (3/1)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ on Android, the keyboard type (email, number, password...) and the action key; showKeyboardOnFocus = false : when focused from code ( FocusRequester , Tab), typed input goes to the field but the software keyboard does not open; it opens on tap (or long press)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"rememberTextFieldState (5) ( TextFieldState ), multi-line field ( singleLine , minLines , maxLines , TextFieldLineLimits )","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ multi-line by default, as in Compose; wrapping at word boundaries, Enter for a new line, arrows/Home/End across lines, tapping to put the cursor on a line, internal scrolling past maxLines ; a multi-line keyboard on Android. TextFieldState also holds the selection ( selection() , observable; UTF-8 byte offsets), edit([](TextFieldBuffer& b) { b.append(…); b.placeCursorAtEnd(); }) ( replace / insert / remove / selectAll / setSelection ; a replacement shifts the selection along with the text after it), setTextAndSelectAll ; while the field is focused, the keyboard picks up changes made from cod"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"BasicTextField (1)","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ undecorated editable text (placeholder, single/multi-line)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Touch selection handles","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ a long press in a text field selects a word; draggable teardrop handles, a Cut/Copy/Paste/Select all toolbar; it ends on typing, on a focus change or on a tap elsewhere While a handle or the cursor is being dragged with a finger, Android's text magnifier (as in Compose): a 100×48 dp lens with rounded corners, 42 dp above the finger, showing that part of the line magnified 1.25 times (it follows the finger horizontally and stays centered on the line), with a shadow; it disappears on release (in plain-text selection too)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Modifier.magnifier","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ magnifier([] { return sourceCenter; }, {.magnifierCenter, .zoom = 1.25f, .size = {100, 48}, .cornerRadius = 24, .elevation = 4}) (as in Compose): drawn above everything, the area around the source point (relative to the modifier's position) magnified (the scene is redrawn in that layer), placed 42 dp above the source by default; the source is read while drawing, and NaN hides it. Note: popups do not appear in the magnifier"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Cursor handle (on a finger tap), word selection on a mouse double-click","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ dragging the handle moves the cursor, tapping it shows Paste/Select all; the host tells finger and mouse apart (a mouse long press does not select)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Foundation","h":"Selection in plain text ( SelectionContainer , DisableSelection )","u":"api.html#foundation","x":"✅ a single selection spans all the Text s inside (in reading order): a long press with a finger selects a word, the handles drag from one text to the next, Copy/Select all; selection by dragging with the mouse, a word on double-click, Ctrl+C / Ctrl+A / Escape; the contents of DisableSelection are not selected. Colors come from LocalTextSelectionColors ( TextSelectionColors{handleColor, backgroundColor} ; MaterialTheme provides the scheme's primary and 40% of it), in fields too"},{"p":"Components and priorities","h":" Material 3","u":"api.html#material-3","x":""},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Text (160), MaterialTheme (75), Button (59), Surface (18), Card (17), TextButton (12)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"MotionScheme , MaterialExpressiveTheme , MaterialTheme.motionScheme","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ MotionScheme : default/fast/slow springs for things that move (spatial: position, size, shape) and things whose appearance changes (effects: color, opacity); MotionScheme::standard() (spatial damping 0.9 — stiffness 700/1400/300; effects 1.0 — 1600/3800/800) and MotionScheme::expressive() (spatial 0.8/380, 0.6/800, 0.8/200 — bouncy; effects the same); motionScheme() returns the ambient one ( LocalMotionScheme , standard at the top). MaterialTheme(colorScheme, motionScheme, content) ; the two-parameter MaterialTheme keeps the ambient motion; MaterialExpressiveTheme(colorScheme or std::nullopt"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"BasicText , BasicSecureTextField , BasicTextField decorationBox / decorator","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ BasicText(text, modifier, style) : takes nothing from its surroundings (Compose's TextStyle.Default: 14 sp, black; not LocalTextStyle and LocalContentColor ), with a string or an AnnotatedString; BasicSecureTextField(state, modifier, options) : an undecorated password field — just the dots (no container, line or label), cannot be copied; BasicTextField and BasicSecureTextField take a decorator (Compose's decorationBox / decorator ): [&](const Content& innerTextField) {…} draws the surroundings of the text itself and places the text with innerTextField() (which takes the remaining space in a "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"lightColorScheme / darkColorScheme (11/10), HorizontalDivider (8), Checkbox (4), TriStateCheckbox ( ToggleableState On/Off/Indeterminate), Switch (3)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ as in Compose 1.4: a 20 dp box inside a 2 dp border; the check mark is drawn along its path (default spatial spring), in the indeterminate state it bends into a line in the middle, and when unchecked it disappears all at once after 100 ms; colors animate with the effects springs, disabled is onSurface 38% (instant). Sizes as in Compose (see minimumInteractiveComponentSize )"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Switch(checked, onCheckedChange, modifier, SwitchOptions{enabled, interactionSource, thumbContent})","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ Compose 1.4's colors: when on, a primary track and an onPrimary thumb; when off, a surfaceContainerHighest track, an outline thumb and border; when disabled, faded over the surface (onSurface 12% track, surface thumb; 38% when off). The thumb slides with the fast spatial spring (0.9 / 1400) and becomes 28 dp instantly on press. thumbContent : a 16 dp icon in the thumb (onPrimaryContainer when on, surfaceContainerHighest when off), which keeps the thumb at its large size and slides with it (mirrored in RTL); the thumb is 16 dp off, 24 dp on, 28 dp pressed (the transitions are animated)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"TextField (8), OutlinedTextField (6)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ floating label, icons, supporting text, error, password; tap to place the cursor; arrows/selection/clipboard on desktop; textStyle (font, color, alignment); readOnly ; prefix / suffix ( {.label = \"Tutar\", .prefix = \"₺\", .suffix = \",00\"} : once the label moves up — always, if there is no label — they sit on both sides of the text in a muted color; the text makes room for them, and touch and the cursor follow suit); colors ( TextFieldColors , Compose's TextFieldDefaults.colors() ): focused/unfocused/error colors for the text, container, cursor, selection, indicator line, icons, label, placehol"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"SecureTextField , OutlinedSecureTextField , TextObfuscationMode","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ single line, password keyboard (no suggestions), cannot be copied/cut; RevealLastTyped (default: a single typed character — in the middle too — is visible for 1.5 s; deleting, pasting or losing focus hides it immediately), Hidden , Visible (for an eye button); textObfuscationCharacter"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Icon (43) + Material icons ( icons.filled 92)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ SVG path → FreeType rasterization, 35 Filled icons"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"ImageVector.Builder (colored vectors), VectorPath","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ a vector built from paths with their own paints, as in Compose: ImageVector::Builder(defaultWidth, defaultHeight, viewportWidth, viewportHeight).path(\"M…\", fill, stroke, strokeWidth).path(VectorPath{…}).autoMirror().build() ; VectorPath fill/stroke Brush (gradients too), their alphas, stroke cap/join/miter, pathFillType . Image(VectorPainter(v)) draws it with its own paints at its default size; Icon(v) and a color filter paint it in a single tone (strokes too; without strokes, the cached raster fast path is used). Paths can sit in nested groups: .addGroup({.rotate, .pivotX, .pivotY, .scaleX,"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"vectorResource (Android vector drawable XML)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ vectorResource(\"vectors/compass.xml\") reads a <vector> file from the app's assets (on Android, the APK's) once and builds an ImageVector ; ImageVector::fromVectorXml(xml) from a string. width / height (dp), viewportWidth / viewportHeight , autoMirrored , alpha ; nested <group> ( rotation , pivotX/Y , scaleX/Y , translateX/Y , in Android's order); <path> pathData , fillColor / strokeColor ( #RGB , #ARGB , #RRGGBB , #AARRGGBB ), fillAlpha / strokeAlpha , strokeWidth , strokeLineCap / Join , strokeMiterLimit , fillType ; <gradient> inside <aapt:attr name=\"android:fillColor|strokeColor\"> : linea"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"AnimatedImageVector , animatedVectorResource , rememberAnimatedVectorPainter","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ Android's single-file <animated-vector> (as written by Android Studio and Shape Shifter): the drawing inside <aapt:attr name=\"android:drawable\"> , and the animation of each <target android:name> inside <aapt:attr name=\"android:animation\"> : <objectAnimator> ( propertyName or <propertyValuesHolder> s; valueFrom — the element's own value if absent — and valueTo ; valueType float/color/path; duration , startOffset , repeatCount , repeatMode ; Android's interpolators), <set> together or sequentially. Animatable: the rotation, pivot, scale and translation of groups; the pathData of paths (morphin"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Scaffold (34), TopAppBar (16), NavigationBar , BottomAppBar (5)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"FlexibleBottomAppBar (M3 Expressive), BottomAppBarDefaults::exitAlwaysScrollBehavior","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ the flexible bottom bar is 64 dp, with its content spread out 16 dp in from the sides ( horizontalArrangement SpaceBetween, contentPadding , expandedHeight ); BottomAppBarSlots::scrollBehavior / FlexibleBottomAppBarOptions::scrollBehavior : as the content scrolls forward, the bar loses that much height and slides down (the Scaffold's content grows), comes back as it scrolls back, and when left halfway it completes based on its velocity or the nearer end; attach nestedScrollConnection to an ancestor of the scrolled content (the Scaffold)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"ShortNavigationBar , ShortNavigationBarItem (M3 Expressive)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ 64 dp tall (surfaceContainer, with the side and bottom insets of the system bars via windowInsets ): EqualWeight items share the width, with the icon above the label and a 56×32 indicator behind the icon; Centered items are grouped in the middle at equal width (that of the widest), with the label beside the icon and the indicator (a 40 dp pill) wrapping both. The default arrangement depends on the window (equal sharing below 600 dp, centered above), and the item's iconPosition follows the arrangement. The indicator grows in from the center (spring), and the ripple is bounded to it; the selec"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"WideNavigationRail , WideNavigationRailItem , rememberWideNavigationRailState (M3 Expressive)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ 96 dp when collapsed, each icon above its label (64 dp items, 56×32 indicator); with state.expand()/collapse()/toggle() (usually from a menu button in the header) it expands to 220 dp with a spring, and the items turn into 56 dp rows: the label after the icon, the indicator wrapping both (20 dp inset). Items follow the rail's state automatically (overridable with railExpanded ); 40 dp between the header and the items, collapsedWidth / expandedWidth , the start/top/bottom insets of the system bars via windowInsets . ModalWideNavigationRail(modifier, items, {state, header, hideOnCollapse, …}) "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"TopAppBarDefaults scroll behaviors ( pinned / enterAlways / exitUntilCollapsed ), MediumTopAppBar / LargeTopAppBar , TopAppBarState","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ via nestedScroll; a bar left halfway settles at the nearer end"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"AppBarRow , AppBarColumn ( AppBarScope : clickableItem , toggleableItem , customItem ), AppBarMenuState","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ as in Compose 1.5: actions that fit are icon buttons in the bar, the rest go into the ⋮ menu (visible only when something is left out; space is reserved for the button, and the last action can use that space); maxItemCount caps the number of slots (the menu button being the last); overflowIndicator is your own open button; AppBarColumn is the same, vertically (a rail, a vertical toolbar; menu button at the bottom)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Top bar colors, heights and insets (Compose's TopAppBarColors , expandedHeight , collapsedHeight , windowInsets )","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ in TopAppBarSlots : containerColor (surface), scrolledContainerColor (surfaceContainer), navigationIconContentColor and titleContentColor (onSurface), actionIconContentColor and subtitleContentColor (onSurfaceVariant; actions are now onSurfaceVariant, as in Compose). expandedHeight (single-line bars 64 dp; medium/large 112/152, 24 more with a subtitle) and collapsedHeight (medium/large 64; if the expanded height is smaller than that, it is raised to match), with the constants TopAppBarDefaults::TopAppBarExpandedHeight , Medium/LargeAppBarCollapsed/ExpandedHeight . windowInsets (default TopAp"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"IconButton (16), FloatingActionButton (10), ExtendedFloatingActionButton (4)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"MediumFloatingActionButton , SmallExtendedFloatingActionButton / MediumExtendedFloatingActionButton / LargeExtendedFloatingActionButton (M3 Expressive)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ the medium FAB is 80 dp (icon 28, corner 20); the extended FABs are 56 / 80 / 96 dp tall (icons 24 / 28 / 36, labels titleMedium / titleLarge / headlineSmall, side padding 16 / 26 / 28, icon-to-label gap 8 / 12 / 16, corners 16 / 20 / 28); when expanded turns off, the label fades and the button shrinks to a square FAB around its icon (with a spring), and accessibility still reads its label"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"ToggleFloatingActionButton , FloatingActionButtonMenu , FloatingActionButtonMenuItem (M3 Expressive)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ a FAB that stays checked: primaryContainer → primary, from a rounded square (16 dp) to a circle, optionally from one size to another ( containerSize → checkedContainerSize , shrinking toward contentAlignment ); the content reads checkedProgress() (e.g. to rotate a plus sign by 45° into a cross), and the content color transitions too. Menu: the actions sit in a column above the button (4 dp apart, 8 dp from the button), pill-shaped (56 dp, primaryContainer, icon + label); they appear bottom to top when opening and disappear top to bottom when closing, 50 ms apart (expanding and fading); horiz"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"HorizontalFloatingToolbar , VerticalFloatingToolbar , FloatingToolbarDefaults (M3 Expressive)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ a pill-shaped toolbar floating over the content (64 dp, 8 dp inner padding, shadow): the middle actions are always visible, while leadingContent / trailingContent open and close only with expanded (expanding horizontally or vertically). Colors: standardFloatingToolbarColors() (surfaceContainer; its FAB secondaryContainer) or vibrantFloatingToolbarColors() (primaryContainer; its FAB tertiaryContainer). The FAB variant: the toolbar plus a FAB 8 dp after it (below it when vertical); when collapsed, only the FAB remains ( StandardFloatingActionButton / VibrantFloatingActionButton ). exitAlwaysSc"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"ToggleButton , ElevatedToggleButton , TonalToggleButton , OutlinedToggleButton , ButtonShapes (M3 Expressive)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ ToggleButton(checked, onCheckedChange, modifier, {shapes, colors, enabled, interactionSource}, content) : when checked, the corners morph from round to squarer (12 dp), and squarer still while pressed (8 dp) (Compose's fast spatial spring: damping 0.9, stiffness 1400); unchecked/checked colors depend on the type (surfaceContainer → primary; Elevated surfaceContainerLow + shadow; Tonal secondaryContainer → secondary; Outlined border → inverseSurface), with a checkbox role and state for accessibility. Plain buttons also get squarer while pressed with ButtonStyle::shapes = ButtonShapes{}"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"ButtonGroup , ButtonGroupScope::animateWidth (M3 Expressive)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ ButtonGroup([&](const ButtonGroupScope& group) { Button(onClick, group.animateWidth(interactionSource), {.interactionSource = interactionSource}, …); … }) : buttons in a row with gaps ( spacing , 12 dp); the one held down widens by expandedRatio (15%), its neighbors give up that share half each (at an end, the single neighbor gives all of it), and the row's width stays the same (fast spatial spring); a squeezed button keeps its height. The horizontal padding of buttons (and ToggleButton s) now flexes: when the full width given is too narrow, the edges shrink before the text does. Connected g"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"SplitButtonLayout , SplitButtonDefaults::LeadingButton / TrailingButton / TrailingButtonIcon (M3 Expressive)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ the primary action and the menu button 2 dp apart; outer corners half, the inner corners where they meet 4 dp (8 dp while pressed); the menu button (48 dp) is checked while its menu is open: it turns fully round and its arrow ( TrailingButtonIcon ) flips; colors via SplitButtonStyle::kind (Filled/Tonal/Elevated/Outlined, the same when checked); checked state for accessibility"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"FilledIconButton / FilledTonalIconButton / OutlinedIconButton , IconToggleButton / FilledIconToggleButton / FilledTonalIconToggleButton / OutlinedIconToggleButton","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ IconToggleButton(checked, onCheckedChange, modifier, {.checkedContainerColor, .checkedContentColor, .enabled, .interactionSource}, content) ; colors are the Material 3 defaults (filled: gray container → primary; tonal: → secondary container; outlined: when on, the border goes away and a dark inverse surface appears); a checked/unchecked checkbox for accessibility; the ripple stays inside the circle, in the content color"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"SnackbarHost / SnackbarHostState (7/8), Circular / LinearProgressIndicator (5), ListItem (13)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ Progress indicators as in Compose 1.4: round caps, a 4 dp gap (plus the caps) between the indicator and the track (secondaryContainer), a stop dot at the end of the determinate linear one; the indeterminate linear one has two lines (heads and tails on their own timings within 1.75 s, emphasized accelerate), the indeterminate circular one has no track and rotates 1080° in a 6 s cycle, plus another 90° every 1.5 s over 300 ms, with its arc growing from 10% to 87% and shrinking back. co_await state.showSnackbarAsync(message, actionLabel, duration, withDismissAction) → SnackbarResult::ActionPerf"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Interactive ListItem(onClick / selected / checked, ...) , SegmentedListItem , ListItemShapes , ListItemDefaults::segmentedShapes","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ as in M3 Expressive: the whole row takes the touch (click, select or check; long press), its shape morphs with the state (4 dp at rest, 12 on hover, 16 when pressed, focused or selected), the selected/checked one sits on secondaryContainer; disabled is onSurface 38%; 16/10 dp padding, 12 dp gap, at least 56 dp. SegmentedListItem + segmentedShapes(i, n) : rows that look like a single card (the top corners of the first and the bottom corners of the last are 16 dp), SegmentedGap (2 dp) apart"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"LinearWavyProgressIndicator , CircularWavyProgressIndicator (M3 Expressive), WavyProgressIndicatorDefaults","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ determinate ( progress ) and indeterminate versions: the indicator advances as a wave, while the track ( trackColor , secondaryContainer by default) stays flat and is separated from the indicator by gapSize ; a stop dot ( stopSize ) at the end of the linear one's track. The wave height comes from amplitude(progress) (default: flat below 10% and above 95%, with the change animated over 500 ms), wavelength (linear 40 / indeterminate 20 / circular 15 dp), waveSpeed (dp/s, by default one wavelength per second; when flat, the wave stops and requests no frames); the linear one's wave fills its hei"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"LoadingIndicator , ContainedLoadingIndicator (M3 Expressive)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ a 38 dp shape inside 48 dp keeps rotating and morphs into the next one every 650 ms (soft burst, 9-sided cookie, pentagon, pill, sunny, 4-sided cookie, oval; each morph also turns a quarter turn with a slight overshoot, on top of one full turn every 4.7 s); in the determinate version, the shape grows from a circle to a soft burst as progress goes from 0 to 1, and turns half a turn. The contained one is onPrimaryContainer in a primaryContainer circle; the other one is primary. Note: instead of Material's shapes (androidx.graphics.shapes polygons and Morph), the shapes are approximated with di"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Slider thumb and track ( thumb / track ), SliderState ( rememberSliderState ), SliderColors , SliderDefaults::Thumb/Track","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ SliderOptions::thumb / track are built from the slider's state (value, range, steps, pressed/dragging, colors ); the default is the classic circle thumb and a thin track. Presets: CircleThumb (classic, with a halo while pressed), Thumb (Material 3's 4×44 dp bar, half as thick while pressed; lying flat on a vertical slider), ShapeThumb(shape) (any shape: square, MaterialShapes diamond/cookie/sunny…; grows a little while pressed), IconThumb(icon) , ValueThumb (a pill showing the value); ClassicTrack (4 dp, step dots), Track (Material 3: 16 dp, a 6 dp gap from the thumb, 2 dp inner corners, a s"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Label (M3), Modifier.minimumInteractiveComponentSize , LocalMinimumInteractiveComponentSize","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ Label(label, modifier, interactionSource, isPersistent, content) : while the interaction source reports pressed/dragged/hovered (or always), a label above the content, centered, 4 dp away (below it if there is no room); it takes no input. minimumInteractiveComponentSize : anything smaller grows to 48 dp (a local value; 0: off), with the content centered; touches (and mouse clicks) landing in that area go to the clickable inside. Users, as in Compose: Slider , RangeSlider , all IconButton / IconToggleButton types (a 40 dp circle in a 48 dp area), Checkbox / TriStateCheckbox / RadioButton when"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"FilterChip (3), AssistChip / SuggestionChip / InputChip (and the shadowed, borderless ElevatedAssistChip / ElevatedSuggestionChip / ElevatedFilterChip ), RadioButton , Slider (2), SegmentedButton (2) ( SingleChoiceSegmentedButtonRow : radio buttons, selectableGroup ; MultiChoiceSegmentedButtonRow + SegmentedButton(checked, onCheckedChange, …) : checkboxes; {.enabled, .icon, .interactionSource} ), PrimaryTabRow / Tab (2) (and LeadingIconTab , with its icon beside the text, 48 dp), Badge / BadgedBox , ElevatedButton / FilledTonalButton , ElevatedCard / OutlinedCard , VerticalDivider , ButtonDefaults","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ Buttons as in Compose 1.4: 40 dp height, at least 58 dp width, 8 dp vertical padding; ButtonStyle::contentPadding ( ButtonDefaults::ButtonWithIconContentPadding etc.: 16 dp at the start for a button with an icon); the outlined button uses outlineVariant; filled/tonal buttons get 1 dp on hover, the elevated button 1 dp (3 on hover), and they drop back while pressed"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"RangeSlider","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ the thumb nearest the press point responds, and the thumbs never cross; when they overlap, the drag direction picks one; steps"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"VerticalSlider (M3 Expressive)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ an upright slider: as tall as possible, 44 dp wide; the value increases upward (downward with reverseDirection ), taps and vertical drags adjust it (it does not take horizontal drags), all of SliderOptions (steps, interactionSource , onValueChangeFinished , enabled ); it shares its implementation with the horizontal Slider"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"SecondaryTabRow , PrimaryScrollableTabRow / SecondaryScrollableTabRow ( ScrollableTabRow )","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ secondary: a thin indicator as wide as the tab; scrollable: tabs at their own width (at least 90 dp), edge padding, and the selected tab scrolls to the center"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"dynamicLight / DarkColorScheme (10/10)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ Android 12+ wallpaper palette (JNI, Compose's tone mapping); on Linux, from the desktop's accent color (XDG portal, TonalPalette::fromSeed ); the default scheme on other desktops"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Dialog / AlertDialog (5/2), ModalBottomSheet (3), DropdownMenu / DropdownMenuItem , Popup , BackHandler","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ overlay layer: popups are composed in place, drawn on top, and receive input; back/Escape closes them. ModalBottomSheet(onDismissRequest, rememberModalBottomSheetState(skipPartiallyExpanded), …) : a sheet taller than half the screen opens halfway ( PartiallyExpanded ), expands fully when dragged up, and hides when dragged down (built on anchoredDraggable , with the fling velocity); currentValue / targetValue / isVisible are observable; show() , hide() (from code, onDismissRequest is not called — co_await state.hide(); showBottomSheet.set(false); ), expand() , partialExpand() ; the scrim, bac"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"PredictiveBackHandler(enabled) { progress -> ... } , BackEventCompat","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ Android 14+ back gesture: while enabled, when a gesture starts, the onBack coroutine starts with the flow of steps ( Flow<BackEventCompat> : progress 0..1, touchX / touchY in dp, swipeEdge ); the flow completes when the gesture ends and the coroutine continues (close, navigate back); if the gesture is cancelled, the coroutine is cancelled (an RAII guard inside it undoes the effect); the back key/Escape starts it with an empty flow. If there is a popup or focus, they are closed/cleared when the gesture ends. On Android 13+, the app registers its OnBackInvokedCallback only while something will"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Modifier.systemGestureExclusion() , systemGestureExclusion(rect(coordinates))","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ drags that start at the edge go to the app instead of the system's back/home gesture (Compose's; with gesture navigation on Android 10+, where Android allows at most 200 dp per edge): the host collects the visible rectangles after layout ( ComposeHost::systemGestureExclusion() ), and on Android calls View.setSystemGestureExclusionRects when they change; the second form narrows it to a rectangle local to the node"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Autofill ( ContentType , autofillManager() )","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ Compose 1.8's autofill: semantics({.contentType = ContentType::Username + ContentType::EmailAddress}) (Username, Password, NewPassword, EmailAddress, PhoneNumber, CreditCardNumber, PostalCode, SmsOtpCode and Compose's other types; combined with + ) tells what a text field accepts. On Android 8+, text fields are exposed to autofill as virtual views of the surface (hints; password fields via setDataIsSensitive ): the password manager is notified when focus enters/leaves a field, when the text changes, and when fields appear and disappear; the manager fills the fields ( SemanticsAction::SetText"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Modifier.sensitiveContent(isContentSensitive = true)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ while the node stays in the layout, it declares that it shows sensitive content (a password, a card number) (Compose's): the host tracks its presence after layout ( ComposeHost::sensitiveContent() ), and on Android 15+ the window is hidden in screen sharing and recordings ( View.setContentSensitivity(SENSITIVE) ); false withdraws the hint. No effect on desktop and the web"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Modifier.clipScrollableContainer(orientation)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ clips only along that axis, leaving a 30 dp margin on both sides of the cross axis (Compose's; the way scroll containers clip, so that card shadows stay visible)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Modifier.keepScreenOn()","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ as long as the node stays in the layout, the screen does not dim/sleep (Compose's; video, recipes); the host tracks its presence after layout ( ComposeHost::keepScreenOn() ), and the platform disables SDL's screensaver ( FLAG_KEEP_SCREEN_ON on Android). Kompoz apps now let the screen dim by default (SDL's default kept the screen on at all times)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"BottomSheetScaffold , rememberBottomSheetScaffoldState , SheetState ( expand / partialExpand / hide )","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ an always-present bottom sheet: sheetPeekHeight of it is visible (above the navigation bar), and it opens by dragging/flinging as far as its content (at most the screen); a list inside scrolls once the sheet is fully open, and when pulled down the list first scrolls back to the top, then the sheet goes down; sheetDragHandle , sheetSwipeEnabled , topBar , snackbarHost ; currentValue / targetValue are observable and saved ( rememberSaveable ); it does not re-add system bar insets that the surrounding layout has already consumed"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"ModalNavigationDrawer (4) ( DrawerState , ModalDrawerSheet , NavigationDrawerItem ), NavigationRail (3)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ opening/closing by dragging, back key; bar ↔ rail via LocalWindowSize . Back gesture (Android 14+, as in M3): ModalDrawerSheet / DismissibleDrawerSheet widens by at most 12 dp for a gesture from the drawer's own edge, narrows by 24 dp for one from the other edge, and gets 48 dp shorter (from the middle of its outer edge; the content keeps its shape); on completion the drawer closes (a widened one narrows back while closing), and on cancel it returns to its previous state. As in M3, there are also sheet variants that take a DrawerState"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"SearchBar (3), SearchBarDefaults.InputField","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ a pill in the layout that expands into a full-screen search view (back closes it, the keyboard's Search key triggers onSearch )"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"BasicAlertDialog , AlertDialogDefaults , AlertDialog(onDismiss, slots, modifier, properties) , DialogProperties::usePlatformDefaultWidth","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ as in Compose 1.4.0. BasicAlertDialog(onDismiss, modifier, content, properties) : your own content, 280–560 dp wide; the minimum width is passed on to the content too (Compose's propagateMinConstraints ). AlertDialog : shape (28 dp corners), containerColor (surfaceContainerHigh), iconContentColor (secondary), titleContentColor (onSurface), textContentColor (onSurfaceVariant); in a short window the text leaves room for the buttons ( weight(1, false) ); the buttons sit at the end, 8 dp apart, and when they don't fit, they stack upward in rows (the confirm button on top, rows 12 dp apart). useP"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"DatePicker , DatePickerDialog , rememberDatePickerState","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ month pages are swiped or changed with the arrows, and tapping the month name shows a year grid; day, month and year can be picked in any order (when the month changes, the selected date moves to the same day); std::chrono dates, Turkish/English strings ( DatePickerStrings , by default LocalStrings 's); input mode ( DisplayMode::Input , via the pencil next to the headline or rememberDatePickerState(..., DisplayMode::Input) ): the date is typed into an outlined field using digits only, the separators appear automatically (the GG.AA.YYYY pattern comes from strings.inputPattern ), and once comp"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"TimePicker , TimeInput , TimePickerDialog , rememberTimePickerState","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ dial (tap/drag; once the hour is picked it moves on to the minutes; an inner ring in 24-hour mode), ÖÖ/ÖS (AM/PM) in 12-hour mode, side by side in a landscape window; TimeInput is two number fields"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"DateRangePicker , rememberDateRangePickerState","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ Başlangıç/Bitiş (Start/End) tabs in the header, and the selected one is edited with DatePicker 's calendar (instead of Compose's vertical list); the range is shown as a band, and the end cannot be before the start; input mode (the pencil or DisplayMode::Input ): start and end are typed into two side-by-side fields (like DatePicker's); a start after the end or an end before the start shows \"Geçersiz tarih aralığı girişi\" (invalid date range input) and is not selected; a momentarily mistyped start does not wipe out an end typed earlier"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"DockedSearchBar","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ the field stays in place, and the suggestions open below it at the same width, over the page (240 dp – 2/3 of the window); tapping outside / back closes it"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"SearchBarState , rememberSearchBarState , ExpandedFullScreenSearchBar , ExpandedDockedSearchBar (new search API)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ the expanded/collapsed state lives in one object ( currentValue / targetValue / progress are observable; animateToExpanded() / animateToCollapsed() can be co_await ed, snapTo ), and the query in a TextFieldState : SearchBarDefaults::InputField(state, textFieldState, {.onSearch, .placeholder, icons}) , the collapsed bar is SearchBar(state, inputField) , and the expanded form is separate: full screen (as in M3, it expands by growing out of the collapsed bar and shrinks back into it: the surface goes from the bar's bounds to the window's, the field at the bar's height moves to 8 dp below the st"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"TooltipBox , PlainTooltip , RichTooltip , rememberTooltipState","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ on a long press (the click underneath does not fire) or when the mouse hovers; above the anchor, or below it if there is no room; a transient one lasts 1.5 s, a persistent one closes on a tap outside; with caretSize ( TooltipDefaults::caretSize ), an arrow pointing at the anchor's center (shadowed on a rich tooltip). M3 1.4's positioning: TooltipBox(TooltipDefaults::rememberTooltipPositionProvider(TooltipAnchorPosition::Above/Below/Left/Right/Start/End, spacingBetweenTooltipAndAnchor = 4), …) — on that side, centered on the anchor along the cross axis, or on the opposite side if there is no "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"MenuBar , MenuBarMenu , MenuBarItem , KeyShortcut (Compose Multiplatform desktop window menus)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ drawn as a strip at the top of the window (not a native menu): clicking a title opens its menu below it, and while one is open, moving the mouse onto another title opens that one; items show their shortcuts on the right ( Ctrl+Shift+S ), checked items ( checked ), submenus (opening beside the item on hover; on the left if there is no room on the right), separators, disabled items; clicking outside / Escape closes it, and the cards are fitted to the window. Shortcuts also work while the menu is closed: if the focused node and the handlers around it (like a text field's Ctrl+A) don't take them"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"Modifier.onPointerEvent ( PointerEventType.Enter/Exit/Move/Press/Release/Scroll ), Modifier.onClick ( PointerMatcher , keyboardModifiers ), TooltipArea ( TooltipPlacement.CursorPoint/ComponentRect ) (Compose Multiplatform desktop)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ onPointerEvent : when the mouse enters/leaves the node (including leaving the window), while it moves over it, when a button is pressed and released ( event.button : Primary/Secondary/Tertiary/Back/Forward; SDL's buttons, rclick / mclick in scripts), and when the wheel turns ( change.scrollDelta , positive downward; consuming it stops the scrolling); keyboardModifiers are the held Shift/Ctrl/Alt (the platform reports them on every mouse and key event: ComposeHost::setKeyboardModifiers ); no event passes ( PointerEventPass ), innermost first. onClick : with the primary button and finger taps "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"ContextMenuArea , ContextMenuItem , text field right-click menu (Compose Multiplatform desktop)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ a right click ( ComposeHost::secondaryClick ; SDL's right button, rclick x y in scripts) opens the innermost menu under it at the clicked point, flipping left/up if it doesn't fit in the window; rows are as wide as the widest item (112–280 dp); selecting an item runs it and closes the menu, clicking outside / Escape closes it, and an enabled = false item is faded and not clickable. In a text field, the click focuses the field and moves the cursor there if it is outside the selection; Cut/Copy are disabled without a selection or in a password field, Cut/Paste are disabled when read-only; copy"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"VerticalScrollbar , HorizontalScrollbar , rememberScrollbarAdapter , ScrollbarStyle (Compose Multiplatform desktop)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ the thumb is as long as the visible fraction (at least 16 dp) and sits where the content is; it is read while drawing (scrolling composes nothing); dragging scrolls the content by the thumb's travel, clicking the track scrolls one page (the visible size) in that direction; the color darkens while hovered and dragged ( hoverDurationMillis ); no thumb when there is nothing to scroll. rememberScrollbarAdapter(ScrollState) ( ScrollState::viewportSize exists too) and (LazyListState) (the content size estimated from the average of the visible items, as in Compose); your own ScrollbarAdapter works "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"ShapeOutline , OutlineShape , MaterialShapes , RoundedPolygon , Morph","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ shapes other than rounded rectangles: OutlineShape(outline) (points in the unit square, clockwise; star-shaped around the center) works for background, border (inside), shadow, clip , graphicsLayer clipping and ripple, and is mirrored in RTL. Material's 35 shapes such as MaterialShapes::Cookie9Sided() ( all() with their names; close to Material's: rounded polygons, stars, petal and pixel shapes), toShape(outline) ; for your own shapes, roundedPolygon({{x, y, rounding}, …}) and roundedStar(points, innerRatio, rounding/innerRounding) . Morph(start, end) : angle-by-angle blending between two sh"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"DropdownMenuPopup , DropdownMenuGroup , MenuDefaults , checked/selected DropdownMenuItem (M3 Expressive menus)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ the popup has no card of its own; each group is a card (surfaceContainerLow, shadowed), with groups MenuDefaults::GroupSpacing (2 dp) apart; groupShape(index, count) is 16 dp at the menu's ends and 4 dp at the joins, itemShape 12 dp at the group's ends and 4 dp in between. DropdownMenuItem(checked, onCheckedChange, …) (checkbox role) and DropdownMenuItem(selected, onClick, …) (radio role): when checked/selected, on tertiaryContainer, 12 dp rounded, with a ✓ before the text ( checkedLeadingIcon ); items in a group are 4 dp inset. MenuItemOptions::supportingText on every item (a small descript"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"ExposedDropdownMenuBox , ExposedDropdownMenu , ExposedDropdownMenuDefaults.TrailingIcon","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ tapping the field opens/closes it; the menu sits below the field at the same width (above it if there is no room) and scrolls for long lists; with a read-only field or a field that filters as you type"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"PermanentNavigationDrawer / PermanentDrawerSheet , DismissibleNavigationDrawer / DismissibleDrawerSheet","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ permanent: the content sits beside it; dismissible: no scrim, it pushes the content, dragging and the back key"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Material 3","h":"currentWindowAdaptiveInfo() , WindowSizeClass , NavigationSuiteScaffold (material3-adaptive)","u":"api.html#material-3","x":"✅ window size classes (width Compact < 600 ≤ Medium < 840 ≤ Expanded dp, height 480 / 900; isWidthAtLeastBreakpoint ); NavigationSuiteScaffold shows the items in a bottom bar, a rail or (optionally) a permanent drawer depending on the window ( NavigationSuiteScaffoldDefaults::calculateFromAdaptiveInfo : as in Compose, a bar in a short window, a rail at medium/expanded width); the content stays in place when the window crosses a breakpoint (its state is kept) and does not re-add the system insets that the bar/rail already avoids; Scaffold also no longer counts insets consumed around it. ListDet"},{"p":"Components and priorities","h":" Animation","u":"api.html#animation","x":""},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Animation","h":"animateFloatAsState (7), animateColorAsState (3), FastOutSlowInEasing (4)","u":"api.html#animation","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Animation","h":"tween (18), spring (7), infiniteRepeatable (5), repeatable , snap , easings","u":"api.html#animation","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Animation","h":"Animatable (13), animateDecay , exponentialDecay / rememberSplineBasedDecay , VelocityTracker","u":"api.html#animation","x":"✅ co_await anim.animateTo(...) ; for floats: animateTo(targetValue, spring, initialVelocity) (spring physics carries the velocity: a flung value overshoots, a released one returns with the drag velocity), animateDecay(initialVelocity, DecayAnimationSpec) (the velocity falls off as exp(−friction·t) and stops below the threshold), updateBounds(lowerBound, upperBound) (snapTo and animations stop at the bounds), velocity() , targetValue() (where the decay will stop). exponentialDecay(frictionMultiplier, absVelocityThreshold) (Compose's: 4.2/s), splineBasedDecay() (the scrollables' fling), calculat"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Animation","h":"AnimatedVisibility (11) + fadeIn / fadeOut (11), AnimatedContent (10), Crossfade (2)","u":"api.html#animation","x":"✅ fade/expand/shrink/slide/scale; exiting content receives no touch or focus; AnimatedContent(n, [](const int&) {...}) also works without a type argument; as the transitionSpec , [](const T& initial, const T& target) { return togetherWith(…); } (the direction depends on the states: from below when a counter increases, from above when it decreases); SizeTransform (default: the container grows with a spring from the old content's size to the new one's, clipping with clip ; togetherWith(…).withSizeTransform(SizeTransform{.clip = false}) , or std::nullopt — as large as what is shown, like Crossfad"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Animation","h":"rememberInfiniteTransition (4), animateContentSize (2), updateTransition (6), MutableTransitionState , rememberTransition , AnimatedVisibility(visibleState)","u":"api.html#animation","x":"✅ MutableTransitionState<S>(initialState) : setTargetState(…) starts the transition — a target different from the initial state animates on first appearance too (an AnimatedVisibility arriving as the screen opens, a row added to a list); currentState() catches up once the values arrive, and isIdle() tells that it has come to rest there (not if it turned back on the way; that is when to remove a row that is fully gone from the list); all of them are observable. AnimatedVisibility(visibleState, modifier, enter, exit, content) : shows according to the target, starting from the current state; it u"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Animation","h":"Modifier.animateBounds , LookaheadScope (Compose 1.8)","u":"api.html#animation","x":"✅ when layout moves the node or changes its size, it slides there from where it was drawn with a spring (or the given AnimationSpec ), with its content laid out at the in-between size; its position is measured relative to the nearest LookaheadScope (a Box), and scrolling that does not animate. There is no lookahead pass: if under an aligned parent its position changes while its size grows, the path reaches the target within the duration but is not perfectly linear; touch goes to where it is placed; without movableContentOf , content moved to another parent is recreated (without animation). Mod"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Animation","h":"Modifier.approachLayout , DeferredTargetAnimation","u":"api.html#animation","x":"✅ the content is first measured as it will end up (lookahead: ApproachMeasureScope::lookaheadSize ); while isMeasurementApproachInProgress(lookaheadSize) is true, approachMeasure(scope, measure, constraints, placeAt) lays it out at the in-between size, and afterwards like the lookahead; state read there triggers a new layout. DeferredTargetAnimation<T>(spec) : the first target is taken as is, and it animates toward a new one via updateTarget(target, scope) ; isIdle , pendingTarget . Note: the lookahead is local (Compose's covers the whole LookaheadScope), and there is no placement approach ( i"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Animation","h":"Navigation: NavHost , NavController , rememberNavController , composable(route) , navigate , popBackStack , currentBackStackEntryAsState","u":"api.html#animation","x":"✅ routes with {name} path segments and optional ?name={name} query parameters ( arguments().getInt/getString/… ); NavOptions{popUpTo, inclusive, saveState, launchSingleTop, restoreState} (bottom navigation tabs keep their own stacks); the back key pops the stack; the back gesture (Android 14+) reveals the screen below with the pop transition, as far as the gesture's progress (seeking along the transition's timeline, as in Compose), pops on completion with the transition continuing from where it was, and on cancel the top screen becomes fully visible again; forward and back transitions ( NavHos"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  Animation","h":"SharedTransitionLayout (8), sharedElement , sharedBounds , rememberSharedContentState","u":"api.html#animation","x":"✅ the content of AnimatedContent / AnimatedVisibility receives an AnimatedVisibilityScope ; the entering element moves from its partner's place (or, in an interrupted transition, from where it was being drawn at that moment) to its own with a spring animation, drawn above the content (even if its parent is fading), while its partner is hidden; sharedElement is re-measured at the in-between size, sharedBounds crossfades the two and scales to the width as in Compose (text does not reflow); exiting content waits until the transition ends. sharedBounds(…, boundsTransform, SharedResizeMode::scaleTo"},{"p":"Components and priorities","h":" UI, drawing and text","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":""},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Modifier (207), Alignment (98), Color (86), dp (147)","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"graphicsLayer (14), alpha , shadow (2)","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ scale/rotation/translation/alpha/clip; the lambda version is read at draw time"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Modifier.dropShadow , Modifier.innerShadow (Compose 1.9; Shadow(color, offset, blurRadius, spread) )","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ a shadow beneath the shape (spread, offset, blurred) or inside it (as if cast from its edge), drawn before what follows; in SDL the blurred mask is built once on the CPU per size, corner and blur with three box blurs (Skia's sigma), and released after a second without use; very large ones (over 4096 px) and the other drawing surfaces fall back to fading rings (no inner shadow there). Rounded-rectangle shapes (circle, pill) and outline shapes (heart, cookie, GenericShape ; in SDL the path is rasterized with FreeType and goes through the same blur, on other surfaces its bounds are used). No br"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"scale , rotate shorthand modifiers","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ graphicsLayer shorthands"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Modifier.zIndex","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ drawing and touch order among siblings (equal ones keep composition order); in a custom Layout , Placeable::place(x, y, zIndex) , and the two add up"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Modifier.dragAndDropSource , Modifier.dragAndDropTarget , DragAndDropTransferData , DragAndDropEvent","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ in-app drag and drop: long press and drag with a finger (direct drag with the mouse), a faded copy of the source follows the pointer, and targets that opt in via shouldStartDragAndDrop receive onStarted / onEntered / onMoved / onExited / onDrop / onEnded ; text + MIME types + localState (any value); Escape cancels; haptic feedback at the start. Drags coming from other apps go to the same targets (desktop, SDL's drop events): files from a file manager ( text/uri-list , with the paths in files ) or text from a browser ( text/plain ); since SDL reports what is being carried only on drop, target"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Brush (13) gradients","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ linear/horizontal/vertical/radial/sweep, color stops; background(Brush) , drawPath(path, brush)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"onSizeChanged (5), Layout (5) ( Measurable / Placeable , layoutId ), the layout modifier (5)","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"rememberSnapFlingBehavior , SnapPosition , LazyListOptions::flingBehavior","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ LazyRow(…, {.flingBehavior = rememberSnapFlingBehavior(state, SnapPosition::Center)}) : when a drag or fling (or the wheel) ends, the item nearest to where the fling would land (spline decay) stops aligned to the start/center/end; it reaches distant items too. In grids as well: LazyVerticalGrid(…, {.flingBehavior = rememberSnapFlingBehavior(gridState, SnapPosition::Start)}) stops with a row aligned (a column in a horizontal grid). Note: not in the pager (the pager already snaps to pages)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"CenterAlignedTopAppBar , Card(onClick) / ElevatedCard(onClick) / OutlinedCard(onClick) , SmallFloatingActionButton / LargeFloatingActionButton , FabPosition","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ the centered title sits at the center of the whole bar, as wide as fits between the icons on both sides (reserving the wider side's width on both sides); cards as in Compose 1.4 have 12 dp corners and clip their content to those corners (filled: surfaceContainerHighest, elevated: surfaceContainerLow at 1 dp, outlined: surface + a 1 dp outlineVariant border); the ripple of clickable cards follows their corners; 40 / 96 dp FABs; ScaffoldSlots::floatingActionButtonPosition : End (default), Start , Center (above the bottom bar), EndOverlay (overlapping the bottom bar)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"TopAppBarSlots::subtitle (M3 Expressive subtitle)","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ below the title, small (labelMedium) and onSurfaceVariant; in TopAppBar it is centered together with the title in the 64 dp, in CenterAlignedTopAppBar both are centered; MediumTopAppBar / LargeTopAppBar grow by 24 dp for it (136 / 176 dp), with the subtitle below the large title (labelLarge / titleMedium), and it appears in the top row together with the small title as the bar collapses"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"MediumFlexibleTopAppBar , LargeFlexibleTopAppBar , TopAppBarSlots::titleHorizontalAlignment , TwoRowsTopAppBar","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ M3 Expressive's flexible bars: a larger title (headlineMedium / displaySmall) and subtitle (labelLarge / titleMedium), 112 / 120 dp (136 / 152 with a subtitle), 64 dp when collapsed; titleHorizontalAlignment puts the large title (and subtitle) of a two-row bar at the start, center or end, and when centered the collapsed title is centered too (with end: the collapsed one at the start). TwoRowsTopAppBar(title(bool expanded), slots, modifier, subtitle(bool)) : the title and subtitle are called twice — true for the large row, false for the collapsed top row — and receive the ambient text style r"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Modifier.pointerHoverIcon (desktop), PointerIcon","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ pointerHoverIcon(PointerIcon::Hand / Text / Crosshair / Default or Cursor::Move, ResizeHorizontal, ResizeVertical, NotAllowed, Wait, overrideDescendants) : the innermost one under the pointer wins (an icon, a clickable's hand, a text field's I-beam); overrideDescendants puts the outer one above its descendants'; SDL's system cursors (desktop, web). Checkbox/TriStateCheckbox/Switch/RadioButton take an interactionSource , and RadioButton also enabled . Notes: no custom image cursors"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Modifier.selectable , toggleable , triStateToggleable , selectableGroup , matchParentSize , fillParentMaxWidth/Height/Size","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ selectable(selected, onClick, SemanticsRole::RadioButton) / toggleable(value, onValueChange, role) / triStateToggleable(state, onClick, role) : a clickable row with its semantic information (selected/checked, indeterminate); give no callback to the RadioButton / Checkbox / TriStateCheckbox / Switch inside it ( {} : Compose's onClick = null ) — then they are not clickable, and the row takes the touch. selectableGroup() marks a radio group. In a Box, matchParentSize() : as large as the size set by the other children, without growing the box (backgrounds, overlays). In a lazy list item, fillPar"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Modifier.onGloballyPositioned , Modifier.onPlaced , LayoutCoordinates","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ reported after layout whenever its position or size changes (scrolling included): size() , positionInRoot/InWindow() , positionInParent() (relative to the parent's content; unaffected by scrolling), boundsInRoot/InWindow() (cut by the surrounding scrollers and clips — clipToBounds , clip(shape) —; empty if not visible), localToRoot , rootToLocal , localToWindow , windowToLocal ; the size at its place in the chain ( padding(10).onGloballyPositioned : the inside). onPlaced fires only when its size or its position within its parent changes. The contents of popups are also relative to the window"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Modifier.onVisibilityChanged , onFirstVisible , LayoutBoundsHolder / Modifier.layoutBounds","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ as in Compose 1.9: true once the whole node (or more than minFractionVisible of it) has been visible in the window — or, with {.viewport = holder} , within a list's/box's area — for minDurationMs ; false immediately when it becomes invisible, leaves the layout or is removed; onFirstVisible only the first time (impression counting). Modifier.onLayoutRectChanged(callback, throttleMillis = 0, debounceMillis = 64) (Compose 1.8): RelativeLayoutBounds ( boundsInRoot , boundsInWindow , positionInWindow() , width() / height() , fractionVisibleInRect(r) , fractionVisibleInWindow() ) when its position"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"SubcomposeLayout ( subcompose(slotId) { … } )","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ slots are requested during measurement: a slot requested for the first time (or whose dependency changed) is composed in the same frame and layout runs again (until then it has nothing to measure); a slot can depend on another slot's size or be chosen according to the constraints; a slot that is no longer requested goes away (with its state and effects); pre-measurements (intrinsics) do not change the slots. Notes: the content runs later, during composition (capture by value); it recomposes when dependency changes, not when the slot content's lambda changes"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"TextStyle (24), FontWeight (26), TextAlign (16), TextOverflow (9), TextDecoration , FontStyle (4), softWrap , TextDirection , autoSize ( TextAutoSize.StepBased ), letterSpacing , lineHeight , TextAlign.Justify , TextIndent , brush , shadow ( Shadow ), drawStyle , textGeometricTransform","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ textGeometricTransform ( TextGeometricTransform(scaleX, skewX) ; TextStyle and SpanStyle ): stretches glyphs horizontally (their advances too, so measurement, cursor and selection follow) and skews them (a point y above the baseline moves y·skewX to the right; in SDL via a FreeType transform; color emoji stay as they are); drawStyle ( TextStyle and SpanStyle ; passed down via ProvideTextStyle ): Stroke(width, cap, join) strokes the glyph outlines instead of filling them (width in dp, half on each side of the outline; in SDL with FreeType's stroker, the join taken from join ; color emoji stay"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Modifier.basicMarquee ( MarqueeAnimationMode , MarqueeSpacing , MarqueeDefaults )","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ content that doesn't fit (measured without a width limit) scrolls in place, with a copy following after spacing ; iterations ( MarqueeDefaults::Forever ), repeatDelayMillis , initialDelayMillis , velocity (negative: the other direction), starting on the right in RTL; WhileFocused only while focused (place it before focusable() ). Only the drawing scrolls (layout and touch stay in place, as in Compose), and layout is not redone while it scrolls"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"rememberTextMeasurer , TextMeasurer.measure , DrawScope.drawText(TextLayoutResult) , Text(onTextLayout = …) , TextLayoutResult","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ rememberTextMeasurer() takes the composition's text style and content color as defaults; measure(text or AnnotatedString, TextStyle, maxWidth, maxHeight) (line count, overflow, wrapping, alignment and direction come from the style; except autoSize). Result: size() (wrapped text is as wide as the given width, otherwise the widest line), lineCount , hasVisualOverflow / didOverflowWidth / didOverflowHeight (maxLines or height), getLineStart/End/Top/Bottom/Baseline/Left/Right , isLineEllipsized , first/lastBaseline , getLineForOffset , getLineForVerticalPosition , getOffsetForPosition , getBound"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"AnnotatedString / SpanStyle (6/3), LinkAnnotation ( Url , Clickable ), TextLinkStyles , BaselineShift , minLines , TextOverflow.StartEllipsis/MiddleEllipsis , InlineTextContent , appendInlineContent , AnnotatedString.fromHtml","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ buildAnnotatedString , nested withStyle ; ParagraphStyle ( withStyle(ParagraphStyle{…}, …) , addStyle , pushStyle ; textAlign , textDirection , lineHeight , lineHeightStyle , textIndent , lineBreak ): its range becomes a paragraph of its own (with line breaks before and after it), its settings replace the text's, in nested ones the inner one wins, and line height trimming applies to each block's own first and last line; Compose's pushStyle / pop() / pop(index) (styles left open close at the end of the text) and string annotations ( addStringAnnotation , pushStringAnnotation , withAnnotation "},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"FontFamily (17), FontVariation","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ Default/SansSerif/Serif/Monospace (system fonts) + FontFamily::fromFiles ; missing weights/italics are synthesized. Variable fonts: a FontFile 's variationSettings ( FontVariation::weight(650) , width , slant , italic , grade , opticalSizing , a font's own axis with {\"XXXX\", value} ; FontVariation::settings(weight, style, ...) derives wght and ital from the weight and style) selects that instance: the same file can appear in a family as several weights (in SDL each instance is a separate FreeType face, via FT_Set_Var_Design_Coordinates ; HarfBuzz shapes with it too)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Fallback fonts (symbols, emoji, CJK)","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ missing glyphs come from the system fallback chain; color emoji: CBDT (FreeType), COLRv0 (FreeType), Android COLRv1 (the Android text stack)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Emoji sequences (ZWJ, flags, skin tones, keycaps, tag sequences)","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ a single glyph (the font's own ligature; on Android, color emoji fonts go through the platform's text stack); the cursor, deletion and line breaking treat the whole grapheme cluster as one"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"Text shaping and right-to-left text","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ all text goes through HarfBuzz (desktop and Android): kerning, ligatures, Arabic letter joining, Indic scripts (Devanagari…); simplified Unicode bidi (UAX #9; isolates and bracket pairs are supported, embeddings/overrides are not): text inside a bracket pair ( () , [] , {} , 〈〉 , 「」 … Unicode's paired brackets; with a 63-entry stack as in BD16, and a closing bracket also closes those still open inside it) takes the embedding level's direction if it contains that direction, and the other direction only if it contains only the other direction and the text before the pair is also in that direct"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"sp (41), LocalDensity (16)","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ Android system font size (also when it changes at runtime)"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"onKeyEvent / onPreviewKeyEvent (3/6)","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ KeyEvent (KeyDown/KeyUp, Ctrl/Shift/Alt); order: preview from outer to inner → text field → onKeyEvent from inner to outer"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"semantics (9) and accessibility","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ Modifier.semantics({contentDescription, role, checked, stateDescription, heading}) , clickable nodes merge their content; {.mergeDescendants = true} (a row's icon and texts are read as one unit, while buttons inside stay separate), clearAndSetSemantics({...}) (nothing inside is read — buttons included —, and the node says only what it is given: five stars → \"Puan: 4 / 5\" (Rating: 4 / 5)), hideFromAccessibility() (both the node and its contents leave the tree); multiple semantics on one node are merged (a toggleable's role/state + a description given later). On Android, AccessibilityNodeProvi"},{"p":"Components and priorities ›  UI, drawing and text","h":"ui.test (41)","u":"api.html#ui-drawing-and-text","x":"✅ ComposeTestRule : onNodeWithText/Tag/ContentDescription , onAllNodesWith… + assertCountEquals ; assertExists / DoesNotExist / IsDisplayed / TextEquals / TextContains / HasClickAction / IsEnabled / IsSelected / IsOn …; performClick / TextInput / Key / ScrollTo ; AssertionError on failure"},{"p":"Components and priorities","h":" Build log","u":"api.html#build-log","x":"Smart recomposition + recomposeHighlighter ✅ LazyColumn / LazyRow + items ✅ Icon + IconButton and 35 Material icons ✅ Coroutines, Animatable , tween / spring , progress indicators ✅ Scaffold + TopAppBar + NavigationBar + FloatingActionButton + Snackbar ✅ Image + ContentScale , Shape + clip , alpha ✅ AnimatedVisibility / AnimatedContent / Crossfade ✅ derivedStateOf , mutableStateListOf , rememberUpdatedState , produceState ✅ Canvas / drawBehind / drawWithContent , aspectRatio , wrapContentSize , requiredSize , graphicsLayer + scaleIn / scaleOut , pointerInput + detectTapGestures / detectDragGestures , combinedClickable ✅ Brush gradients, Path (fill/stroke), drawWithCache ✅ Material components: ListItem , chips, RadioButton , Slider , SegmentedButton , tabs, Badge , button/card variants, BottomAppBar ✅ Popup , Dialog / AlertDialog , DropdownMenu , ModalBottomSheet , BackHandler (including the Android back button) ✅ LazyVerticalGrid , HorizontalPager , BoxWithConstraints , onSizeChanged , animateContentSize ✅ Focus and text input: FocusRequester , FocusManager , onFocusChanged , KeyboardOptions / KeyboardActions , filled/outlined TextField ✅ ModalNavigationDrawer , NavigationRail , LocalWindowSize ✅ Layout , Modifier.layout , layoutId , IntrinsicSize , FlowRow / FlowColumn , updateTransition ✅ AnnotatedString / SpanStyle /links, italics, TextOverflow / softWrap , LocalDensity + system font scale ✅ FontFamily + glyph fallback (symbols, color emoji, CJK) ✅ animateItem , SwipeToDismissBox , draggable ✅ Accessibility: semantics tree + Android AccessibilityNodeProvider ✅ nestedScroll , VerticalPager , TopAppBar scroll behaviors, MediumTopAppBar / LargeTopAppBar ✅ Dynamic color (Material You): dynamicLightColorScheme / dynamicDarkColorScheme ; Switch / Checkbox enabled ✅ WindowInsets types, windowInsetsPadding / imePadding / consumeWindowInsets , SystemBarsAppearance ✅ Keyboard and focus: focusable , onKeyEvent / onPreviewKeyEvent , directional focus, bring-into-view (also w"},{"p":"Components and priorities","h":" Next","u":"api.html#next","x":"iOS and macOS (requires macOS and Xcode; the code is portable but hasn't been compiled yet). Desktop accessibility APIs (AT-SPI, UI Automation, NSAccessibility); TalkBack works on Android. Item reuse in lazy lists via contentType (Compose's SubcomposeLayout pool): measured, not needed for now (0.06–0.13 ms per frame in a 10,000-row list). No equivalent of Kotlin's by delegation: state is read and written with .get() / .set() ."}];
